// In-depth material (Health and the Body, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
nutri:{s:[
["Carbohydrates","Carbohydrates are the body’s main source of energy. Starches are found in rice, bread, corn, root crops and noodles; sugars in fruit, milk and sweets. Whole grains, such as brown rice, release energy more slowly and contain more fiber than refined grains. Too much added sugar can harm health."],
["Proteins","Proteins build and repair muscles, organs, skin and hair and make enzymes and antibodies. They are made of building blocks called amino acids. Good sources include fish, chicken, eggs, meat, milk, beans, mongo, tofu and nuts. Growing children and teenagers need plenty of protein."],
["Fats","Fats provide energy, help the body absorb vitamins A, D, E and K and protect organs. Unsaturated fats in fish, nuts and vegetable oils are healthier than saturated fats in fatty meat and butter. Trans fats, found in some processed and fried foods, raise the risk of heart disease and are being banned in many countries."],
["Iodine, iron and vitamin A","Around the world, nutrition programs often target three micronutrients. Iodized salt prevents goiter and protects children’s brain development. Iron prevents anemia, which causes tiredness. Vitamin A, from yellow and green vegetables and given in capsules to young children, prevents night blindness and strengthens immunity."],
["Food labels","Packaged foods have nutrition labels showing serving size, energy in calories, and amounts of fat, sugar, salt and other nutrients. Reading labels helps people compare foods and choose healthier options. Ingredients are listed from largest to smallest amount."],
["Healthy habits","Healthy eating includes plenty of vegetables and fruit, whole grains, lean proteins and water, and limiting sugary drinks, salty snacks and fried foods. Eating breakfast, eating meals with family and not skipping meals help. The World Health Organization and national health agencies publish guidelines."]],
sp:["Moringa, the miracle tree","Moringa is a fast-growing tree native to northern India and now grown across the tropics, from Africa to Southeast Asia and the Caribbean. Its small leaves are rich in vitamins A and C, calcium, iron and protein and are cooked in soups, stews and curries or dried into powder. Because it grows easily, survives drought and is cheap, nutrition programs promote moringa to fight malnutrition, and it is sometimes called a miracle tree."],
g:[["Nutrient","A substance the body needs from food"],["Calorie","A unit of food energy"],["Protein","A nutrient that builds and repairs the body"],["Micronutrient","A vitamin or mineral needed in small amounts"]],
f:["Rice, wheat and maize provide about half of all the food energy people eat.","Vitamin C is found in calamansi, guava and green mangoes.","Stunting, being too short for one’s age, affects about one in five young children worldwide."]},
eye:{s:[
["A camera-like organ","The eye works somewhat like a camera. Light enters through the clear cornea, passes through the pupil, the black opening, and is focused by the lens onto the retina at the back. The colored iris widens or narrows the pupil to control how much light gets in, like a camera’s aperture."],
["Rods and cones","The retina contains about 120 million rod cells, which work in dim light and see in black and white, and about 6 million cone cells, which see color and fine detail in bright light. There are three kinds of cones, sensitive to red, green and blue light. The brain combines their signals to see millions of colors."],
["Seeing in 3D","Because our two eyes are a few centimeters apart, each sees a slightly different view. The brain combines them to judge distance and depth, called stereoscopic vision. Predators such as cats and owls also have forward-facing eyes, while many prey animals have eyes on the sides of their heads to see more around them."],
["Glasses and contact lenses","In nearsightedness, or myopia, distant objects look blurry; in farsightedness, near objects do. Glasses, invented in Italy around 1290, and contact lenses correct these problems. Myopia is increasing worldwide, especially in East Asia, partly because children spend less time outdoors and more time looking at screens and books up close."],
["Cataracts and glaucoma","A cataract is a clouding of the lens that makes vision blurry. It is the leading cause of blindness worldwide but can be fixed by surgery replacing the lens with an artificial one. Glaucoma damages the optic nerve, often because of high pressure in the eye, and can cause blindness without early treatment."],
["Tears and blinking","People blink about 15 to 20 times a minute. Each blink spreads tears across the eye, keeping it moist and clean. Tears contain salt, oils and antibacterial substances. They drain through small ducts into the nose, which is why your nose runs when you cry."]],
sp:["The 20-20-20 rule","Looking at screens for long periods can cause digital eye strain, with dry, tired eyes, headaches and blurred vision, partly because people blink less while concentrating. Eye doctors recommend the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet, or 6 meters, away for 20 seconds. Spending time outdoors each day may also help protect children’s eyes from becoming nearsighted."],
g:[["Retina","The light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye"],["Pupil","The opening that lets light into the eye"],["Lens","The part that focuses light"],["Myopia","Nearsightedness"]],
f:["The eye can distinguish about 10 million colors.","Babies are born seeing mostly blurry shapes and limited color.","Your eye muscles are among the most active muscles in the body."]},
blood:{s:[
["About five liters","An adult has about 5 liters of blood, around 7 to 8% of body weight. It travels through blood vessels that, if laid end to end, would stretch about 100,000 km, more than twice around Earth. Most of these vessels are tiny capillaries."],
["White blood cells","White blood cells defend the body against infection. Some, called neutrophils, engulf and destroy bacteria. Lymphocytes make antibodies and remember germs. There are far fewer white cells than red cells, but their numbers rise when the body is fighting an infection."],
["Platelets","Platelets are tiny cell fragments that help blood clot. When a vessel is cut, platelets stick to the wound and to each other, forming a plug. Proteins in plasma then form a mesh of fibers called fibrin that traps blood cells into a clot. A scab forms on the surface as it dries."],
["Bone marrow","Blood cells are made in the bone marrow, found especially in the hip bones, breastbone, ribs and spine. Red blood cells live about 120 days, then are broken down in the spleen and liver. The body makes about 2 million new red cells every second."],
["Blood diseases","Anemia, a lack of red blood cells or hemoglobin, causes tiredness and is often due to lack of iron. Sickle cell disease and thalassemia are inherited conditions affecting hemoglobin; sickle cell disease is common in Africa and thalassemia around the Mediterranean and in South and Southeast Asia. Leukemia is a cancer of the blood-forming cells. Hemophilia prevents blood from clotting properly."],
["Karl Landsteiner","Early blood transfusions often killed patients. In 1901 Austrian doctor Karl Landsteiner discovered the A, B and O blood groups, showing that mixing incompatible blood makes it clump. This made safe transfusions possible. He also helped discover the Rh factor in 1940. He won the Nobel Prize in 1930, and World Blood Donor Day, 14 June, is held on his birthday."]],
sp:["Donating blood","One donation of about 450 milliliters of blood can be separated into red cells, plasma and platelets, helping up to three patients, such as accident victims, mothers in childbirth and people with cancer. In most countries, healthy people aged about 17 or 18 to 65 who weigh at least 50 kg may donate, sometimes from 16 with a parent’s consent. Whole blood can usually be donated every three or four months. The World Health Organization aims for all blood to come from unpaid volunteers."],
g:[["Plasma","The liquid part of blood"],["Hemoglobin","The protein that carries oxygen in red cells"],["Platelet","A cell fragment that helps clotting"],["Transfusion","Giving blood to a patient"]],
f:["Type O negative blood can be given in emergencies to people of any type.","The most common blood type in the world is type O positive.","Blood makes up about 7 to 8% of body weight."]},
skin:{s:[
["The largest organ","The skin is the body’s largest organ, covering about 2 square meters in an adult and weighing several kilograms. It is thinnest on the eyelids and thickest on the soles of the feet. It protects the body from germs, injury and water loss and helps control temperature."],
["Epidermis","The outer layer, the epidermis, is constantly renewed. New cells form at its base and move up, filling with tough keratin and dying as they reach the surface, where they flake off. You shed tens of thousands of skin cells every minute. The whole surface replaces itself about every month."],
["Dermis","Below the epidermis, the dermis contains blood vessels, nerve endings, hair follicles, sweat glands and oil glands, supported by fibers of collagen and elastin that give skin strength and stretch. Beneath it lies a layer of fat that stores energy and keeps the body warm."],
["Touch","Skin contains millions of nerve endings that sense touch, pressure, vibration, heat, cold and pain. Fingertips, lips and the tongue are especially sensitive. Fingerprints, unique patterns of ridges on the fingertips, help grip and improve touch. Even identical twins have different fingerprints."],
["Acne","Acne is common during the teenage years, when hormones make oil glands more active. Pores become blocked with oil and dead skin, and bacteria can cause pimples. Washing the face gently, not squeezing pimples and seeing a doctor for severe acne help. Diet’s role is still being studied."],
["Skin conditions","Common skin conditions include eczema, which causes itchy, dry patches; fungal infections such as ringworm and an-an, known medically as tinea versicolor; and scabies, caused by tiny mites. Leprosy, now curable, once caused great fear. Skin cancer is linked to too much ultraviolet light from the Sun."]],
sp:["Melanin and sunscreen","Melanin is the pigment that gives skin, hair and eyes their color and helps protect skin from the Sun’s ultraviolet rays. People with more melanin have darker skin, but everyone can be harmed by too much sun. Sunburn, skin aging and skin cancer are linked to UV exposure; Australia and New Zealand have the world’s highest rates of skin cancer. Dermatologists recommend shade, protective clothing and broad-spectrum sunscreen with SPF 30 or more, reapplied every two hours outdoors."],
g:[["Epidermis","The outer layer of skin"],["Dermis","The inner layer of skin"],["Melanin","The pigment that colors skin"],["Keratin","A tough protein in skin, hair and nails"]],
f:["Much household dust contains dead skin cells.","Goosebumps are caused by tiny muscles raising hairs.","Fingernails grow about 3 to 4 millimeters a month."]},
vacc:{s:[
["Smallpox","Smallpox was a terrible disease that killed about 30% of those infected and scarred many survivors. It killed an estimated 300 million people in the 20th century alone. In Asia and Africa, people practiced variolation, deliberately infecting people with material from mild cases to protect them, centuries before vaccines."],
["Antibodies and memory","A vaccine contains a harmless form or piece of a germ, or instructions to make one. The immune system responds by making antibodies and memory cells. If the real germ later invades, the immune system recognizes it quickly and fights it off before it can cause serious illness."],
["The Balmis Expedition","In 1803 Spain sent the Balmis Expedition around the world to spread the smallpox vaccine to its empire. With no refrigeration, the vaccine was carried alive in orphan boys, vaccinated one after another during the voyage. The expedition vaccinated thousands of people in the Caribbean, Mexico, the Philippines and China, one of the first international health campaigns."],
["Eradicating smallpox","In 1967 the World Health Organization launched an intensified campaign to wipe out smallpox, finding cases and vaccinating everyone around them. The last natural case was in Somalia in 1977. In 1980 the WHO declared smallpox eradicated, the only human disease ever wiped out completely."],
["The Expanded Program on Immunization","In 1974 the World Health Organization launched the Expanded Programme on Immunization to bring basic vaccines to children everywhere. Babies now receive vaccines against tuberculosis, hepatitis B, polio, diphtheria, tetanus, whooping cough, measles and more. Since 2000, Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance, has helped poorer countries vaccinate more than a billion children."],
["Vaccine hesitancy","Some people delay or refuse vaccines because of fears, misinformation or distrust. A fraudulent 1998 study falsely linking the measles vaccine to autism, later retracted, led to falling vaccination and measles outbreaks in Britain, the United States and elsewhere. Controversies over other vaccines have had similar effects. Health workers work to rebuild trust with accurate information."]],
sp:["mRNA vaccines","During the COVID-19 pandemic, scientists created vaccines in record time. Some used a new technology, messenger RNA, mRNA, which gives cells instructions to make a harmless piece of the virus’s spike protein, training the immune system. Hungarian-born scientist Katalin Karikó and American Drew Weissman had spent years working out how to make mRNA safe to use. Their work enabled vaccines that protected billions of people, and they won the 2023 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],
g:[["Vaccine","A substance that trains the immune system"],["Immunity","Protection against a disease"],["Eradication","Wiping out a disease completely"],["Herd immunity","Protection of a community when most are immune"]],
f:["Vaccines prevent an estimated 3.5 to 5 million deaths a year.","Polio cases worldwide have fallen by over 99% since 1988.","Rinderpest, a cattle disease, is the only other disease eradicated, in 2011."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hlth3.js */
// In-depth material (Health and the Body, part 3): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
exercise:{s:[
["Aerobic and anaerobic","Aerobic exercise, such as brisk walking, swimming, cycling and dancing, uses oxygen to produce energy over longer periods and strengthens the heart and lungs. Anaerobic exercise, such as sprinting or lifting heavy weights, involves short, intense bursts that build power and muscle, using energy without enough oxygen."],
["Strength, flexibility and balance","A complete exercise routine builds four things: endurance, strength, flexibility and balance. Push-ups, squats and climbing build strength. Stretching and yoga improve flexibility. Balance exercises, such as standing on one leg, help prevent falls, especially in older people."],
["Exercise and the brain","Exercise increases blood flow to the brain and helps it grow new connections. Regular physical activity improves memory, attention and learning, and reduces stress, anxiety and symptoms of depression. Even a short walk can improve mood and focus."],
["Preventing disease","Regular exercise lowers the risk of heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, some cancers and osteoporosis. It helps control weight and improves sleep. The World Health Organization estimates that physical inactivity contributes to millions of deaths a year worldwide."],
["Traditional games and dances","Games played all over the world are great exercise, such as tag, hopscotch, skipping rope and tug-of-war. Many countries have their own, such as kabaddi in India, a team game of tagging and holding breath, and tinikling in the Philippines, in which dancers hop between clapping bamboo poles. Brazilian capoeira blends martial arts, dance and music."],
["Staying safe","Exercising safely means drinking water, wearing proper shoes, warming up, using protective gear such as helmets and building up slowly. In hot climates, it is best to exercise in the cooler early morning or late afternoon to avoid heat exhaustion and heatstroke."]],
sp:["The 10,000 steps idea","The popular goal of walking 10,000 steps a day began in Japan in the 1960s, when a company sold a pedometer called manpo-kei, meaning 10,000-step meter. The number was chosen partly because it sounded good. Recent studies suggest health benefits begin at lower numbers, with large gains from around 7,000 to 8,000 steps a day, and more steps generally help further. What matters most is moving more and sitting less."],
g:[["Aerobic","Using oxygen for sustained energy"],["Endurance","The ability to keep going for a long time"],["Flexibility","The ability to move joints through their full range"],["Heatstroke","Dangerous overheating of the body"]],
f:["Children and teenagers should get at least 60 minutes of activity a day.","Muscles grow stronger during rest after exercise.","Regular physical activity is linked to a longer, healthier life."]},
ear:{s:[
["Three parts","The ear has three parts. The outer ear, the visible pinna and the ear canal, collects sound. The middle ear contains the eardrum and three tiny bones. The inner ear contains the snail-shaped cochlea for hearing and the vestibular system for balance."],
["The tiniest bones","The three bones of the middle ear, the hammer, anvil and stirrup, also called the malleus, incus and stapes, pass vibrations from the eardrum to the inner ear, amplifying them. The stapes, about 3 millimeters long, is the smallest bone in the human body."],
["Hair cells","Inside the cochlea, fluid moves in waves when sound vibrations arrive. About 15,000 or more tiny hair cells sense these movements and send signals through the auditory nerve to the brain. Different parts of the cochlea respond to different pitches. Damaged hair cells do not grow back in humans."],
["Range of hearing","Young people can hear sounds from about 20 to 20,000 vibrations per second, or hertz. As people age, they gradually lose the ability to hear high sounds. Dogs can hear up to about 45,000 hertz, and bats and dolphins much higher. Elephants can hear very low sounds."],
["Balance and dizziness","The vestibular system has three fluid-filled semicircular canals that sense head movement and two organs that sense gravity and acceleration. If you spin and stop, the fluid keeps moving, making you dizzy. Motion sickness happens when your eyes and inner ears send the brain conflicting signals."],
["Earwax and ear care","Earwax protects the ear canal by trapping dust and germs and is usually moved out naturally. Pushing cotton buds into the ear can push wax deeper and damage the eardrum. Ear infections, common in young children, can cause pain and fever. A doctor should check ear pain or discharge."]],
sp:["Cochlear implants","A cochlear implant is an electronic device that can give a sense of sound to some people with severe hearing loss. A microphone and processor behind the ear pick up sounds and send signals to electrodes placed inside the cochlea, which stimulate the auditory nerve directly. The first multichannel implants were developed in the 1970s, including by Australian surgeon Graeme Clark. Over a million people worldwide now use them, and newborn hearing screening, now routine in many countries, helps detect hearing loss early."],
g:[["Cochlea","The spiral organ of hearing"],["Eardrum","A thin membrane that vibrates with sound"],["Decibel","A unit measuring loudness"],["Vestibular system","The inner ear’s balance system"]],
f:["Sounds above about 85 decibels can damage hearing over time.","Your ears keep hearing while you sleep, but the brain ignores most sounds.","Ears and noses keep growing slowly throughout life."]},
teeth:{s:[
["Two sets of teeth","Humans have two sets of teeth. Children have 20 baby, or milk, teeth, which begin to appear at about six months. From about age six, they fall out and are replaced by up to 32 permanent teeth, including four wisdom teeth, which often appear between ages 17 and 25 or never appear at all."],
["Enamel, dentin and pulp","The visible crown of a tooth is covered with enamel, the hardest substance in the human body. Beneath it is dentin, a bony layer. In the center is the pulp, containing nerves and blood vessels. The root, anchored in the jawbone, is covered with cementum."],
["How cavities form","Bacteria in the mouth form a sticky film called plaque. When we eat sugary or starchy foods, the bacteria produce acids that dissolve tooth enamel, creating holes called cavities. Eating sweets often, especially sticky ones and sugary drinks, increases the risk."],
["Fluoride","Fluoride, a mineral added to most toothpastes, strengthens enamel and helps repair early damage. Dentists recommend brushing twice a day for two minutes with fluoride toothpaste, cleaning between teeth with floss and visiting a dentist regularly. Fluoride varnish can protect children’s teeth."],
["Braces","Braces straighten crooked teeth and correct bite problems by applying gentle, steady pressure that slowly moves teeth through the jawbone. They may be metal, ceramic or clear removable aligners. Treatment usually lasts one to three years, followed by a retainer to keep teeth in place."],
["Dental health worldwide","Oral diseases affect about 3.5 billion people, and untreated tooth decay in adult teeth is the most common health condition in the world. Sugary foods and drinks are the main cause. Fluoride in toothpaste and water, school toothbrushing programs and taxes on sugary drinks in countries such as Mexico and the United Kingdom help protect teeth."]],
sp:["Ancient dentistry","People have tried to fix teeth for thousands of years. A tooth found in Pakistan, about 9,000 years old, shows holes drilled with a bow drill. Ancient Egyptians are thought to have bound loose teeth with gold wire, and the Maya inlaid teeth with jade and other gemstones. In ancient Southeast Asia, people decorated teeth with gold pegs or filed them into patterns, showing that beautiful teeth were valued long before modern dentistry."],
g:[["Enamel","The hard outer coating of teeth"],["Plaque","A sticky film of bacteria on teeth"],["Cavity","A hole in a tooth caused by decay"],["Orthodontist","A dentist who straightens teeth"]],
f:["Each person’s teeth are unique, which helps identify people.","Sharks can replace teeth throughout their lives, but humans cannot.","Toothbrushes with bristles were used in China by the 1400s."]},
water:{s:[
["Mostly water","Water makes up about 50 to 65% of an adult’s body weight and even more in babies. The brain and heart are about three-quarters water, and the lungs even more. Even bones are about a third water. Every cell needs water to work."],
["Inside and outside cells","About two-thirds of the body’s water is inside cells. The rest is outside cells, in blood plasma, lymph and fluid between cells. Salts called electrolytes, such as sodium and potassium, help control how water moves between these spaces and keep nerves and muscles working."],
["Thirst","When the body needs water, the brain detects that the blood has become slightly more concentrated. It triggers thirst and releases a hormone that tells the kidneys to save water. Older people may feel less thirsty and can become dehydrated without noticing."],
["Sweat","Sweating cools the body as sweat evaporates from the skin. In hot weather or during exercise, people can lose a liter or more of sweat an hour. Sweat carries salt as well as water. In humid climates, sweat evaporates more slowly, so the body cools less efficiently."],
["Too much water","Drinking huge amounts of water very quickly can be dangerous, though it is rare. It can dilute the sodium in the blood, a condition called hyponatremia, which can cause confusion and in severe cases death. It has occurred in some long-distance runners and in drinking contests."],
["Water and food","About a fifth of the water people take in comes from food. Fruits and vegetables such as watermelon, cucumber and tomatoes, and soups and stews, contain much water. Coconut water is a popular natural drink across the tropics."]],
sp:["Oral rehydration solution","Diarrhea causes the body to lose water and salts quickly and is dangerous for young children. In the 1960s and 1970s, researchers in Bangladesh and India showed that a simple drink of clean water mixed with the right amounts of salt and sugar could be absorbed by the gut even during diarrhea. This oral rehydration solution, ORS, has saved tens of millions of lives. It is cheap and available in pharmacies and health centers worldwide. The medical journal The Lancet called it potentially the most important medical advance of the 20th century."],
g:[["Dehydration","Lack of enough water in the body"],["Electrolyte","A salt that helps control body fluids"],["Hyponatremia","Too little sodium in the blood"],["ORS","Oral rehydration solution"]],
f:["You lose water when you breathe out; you can see it on a cold mirror.","Urine color is a simple sign of hydration: pale yellow is good.","A person can survive only a few days without water."]},
flu:{s:[
["Rhinoviruses","The common cold can be caused by more than 200 viruses, most often rhinoviruses, from the Greek for nose. Colds are mild, with a runny nose, sneezing, sore throat and cough, and usually last about a week. Because there are so many viruses, people can catch many colds in their lives."],
["Influenza","Influenza, or flu, is caused by influenza viruses, mainly types A and B. It usually comes on suddenly, with high fever, body aches, tiredness, headache and cough. Most people recover within one to two weeks, but flu can cause pneumonia and be deadly, especially for babies, older people and those with health problems."],
["Antibiotics do not help","Colds and flu are caused by viruses, so antibiotics, which kill bacteria, do not cure them. Rest, fluids and medicines to relieve fever and pain help. Antiviral drugs can shorten flu if taken early. A doctor should be seen if breathing becomes difficult or fever is very high or lasting."],
["The flu vaccine","Because flu viruses change constantly, scientists create a new flu vaccine each year based on the strains expected to spread. The World Health Organization chooses the strains twice a year, for the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. In the tropics, flu can circulate all year."],
["Bird and swine flu","Some flu viruses infect animals such as birds and pigs. Occasionally they jump to humans. Avian influenza, or bird flu, such as H5N1, has killed many poultry and some people. The 2009 H1N1 pandemic, called swine flu, came from a virus with pig, bird and human genes."],
["COVID-19","COVID-19, caused by a coronavirus called SARS-CoV-2, emerged in late 2019 and spread worldwide, causing a pandemic. It caused millions of deaths. Many countries closed schools and borders, ordered lockdowns and ran mass vaccination drives. The WHO declared the global health emergency over in May 2023, though the virus continues to circulate."]],
sp:["The 1918 flu around the world","The 1918 influenza pandemic, wrongly called the Spanish flu, spread worldwide in three waves and killed an estimated 50 million people, more than the First World War. Ships and troop movements carried it to almost every corner of the globe. India lost perhaps 12 million people or more, and Western Samoa about a fifth of its population. Schools closed and public gatherings were limited, measures that echoed a century later during COVID-19."],
g:[["Virus","A tiny germ that reproduces inside cells"],["Pandemic","A disease spreading across the world"],["Antiviral","A medicine that fights viruses"],["Strain","A variety of a virus"]],
f:["Washing hands and covering coughs reduce the spread of colds and flu.","Children catch more colds than adults, perhaps six to eight a year.","Chicken soup and warm drinks can soothe cold symptoms."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hlth4.js */
// In-depth material (Health and the Body, part 4): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
kidney:{s:[
["Bean-shaped filters","The kidneys are two bean-shaped organs, each about the size of a fist, lying against the back muscles just below the ribs. Together they filter about 180 liters of blood plasma a day, returning almost all of it to the blood and producing about 1 to 2 liters of urine."],
["Nephrons","Each kidney contains about a million tiny filtering units called nephrons. In each, a ball of capillaries, the glomerulus, filters the blood. The filtered fluid then passes through a long tubule, where water, sugar, salts and other useful substances are taken back into the blood, leaving waste behind as urine."],
["The urinary system","Urine flows from each kidney down a tube called a ureter into the bladder, a stretchy muscular bag that can hold about 400 to 600 milliliters. When it fills, nerves signal the brain that it is time to urinate. Urine leaves the body through the urethra."],
["Hormones from the kidneys","The kidneys also make hormones. One, erythropoietin, tells the bone marrow to make red blood cells. Another helps control blood pressure. The kidneys also activate vitamin D, helping the body absorb calcium for strong bones. Kidney disease can therefore cause anemia and weak bones."],
["Diabetes and high blood pressure","The two leading causes of kidney failure are diabetes and high blood pressure, which damage the tiny blood vessels in the nephrons. Kidney disease often has no symptoms until it is advanced. Controlling blood sugar and blood pressure, drinking enough water and avoiding overuse of painkillers protect the kidneys."],
["Dialysis and transplants","When kidneys fail, patients need dialysis or a kidney transplant. In hemodialysis, a machine filters the blood, usually three times a week for several hours. Peritoneal dialysis uses the lining of the abdomen as a filter. A transplant from a living or deceased donor can free patients from dialysis. People can live normally with just one healthy kidney."]],
sp:["Living with kidney failure","When the kidneys fail, patients need dialysis or a transplant to survive. Hemodialysis usually takes about four hours, three times a week, while peritoneal dialysis can be done at home. A transplanted kidney from a living or deceased donor gives most people a better life, but there are far more patients waiting than organs available. Doctors urge people to reduce salt, control diabetes and blood pressure and drink enough water."],
g:[["Nephron","A tiny filtering unit in the kidney"],["Dialysis","Artificial filtering of the blood"],["Bladder","A bag that stores urine"],["Ureter","A tube from kidney to bladder"]],
f:["Kidney stones can be as small as sand or as large as a golf ball.","All the blood in the body passes through the kidneys many times a day.","The first successful kidney transplant was performed in 1954, between identical twins."]},
hormone:{s:[
["Chemical messengers","Hormones are chemical messengers made by glands and carried in the blood to target organs. They control growth, energy use, mood, sleep, reproduction and the balance of water and salts. Even tiny amounts can have big effects."],
["The pituitary gland","The pituitary gland, about the size of a pea, sits at the base of the brain. It is sometimes called the master gland because it controls other glands, such as the thyroid and adrenal glands. It makes growth hormone and is itself controlled by the hypothalamus, part of the brain."],
["The thyroid","The thyroid, a butterfly-shaped gland in the neck, makes hormones that control how fast the body uses energy, its metabolism. It needs iodine to work. Too little iodine can cause the thyroid to swell into a goiter; adding iodine to salt has greatly reduced this problem around the world."],
["Adrenaline","The adrenal glands, on top of the kidneys, release adrenaline when you are frightened or excited. It makes the heart beat faster, breathing quicken and muscles receive more blood, preparing the body for fight or flight. Cortisol, another adrenal hormone, helps the body cope with longer-term stress."],
["Puberty","During puberty, usually between about ages 8 and 14, the pituitary signals the ovaries or testes to make sex hormones, estrogen and testosterone. These cause growth spurts, body changes and the development of adult features. Hormonal changes can also affect mood and skin. Puberty timing varies a lot between people."],
["Insulin and blood sugar","The pancreas makes insulin, which helps cells take in sugar from the blood for energy. In type 1 diabetes, the body cannot make insulin. In type 2, more common, the body does not respond to it well. In 1921 Frederick Banting and Charles Best in Canada isolated insulin, turning type 1 diabetes from a death sentence into a manageable disease."]],
sp:["Diabetes, a global challenge","Diabetes affects more than 500 million adults worldwide, many of whom do not know they have it, and the number is rising fastest in low- and middle-income countries. Risk factors include being overweight, physical inactivity, a diet high in sugar and refined carbohydrates, and family history. Untreated diabetes can damage the eyes, kidneys, nerves and heart. Regular checkups, healthy food, fewer sugary drinks and exercise help prevent and control type 2 diabetes. World Diabetes Day is 14 November, Banting’s birthday."],
g:[["Gland","An organ that makes hormones"],["Endocrine system","The body’s system of hormone glands"],["Metabolism","Chemical processes that use energy"],["Insulin","A hormone that controls blood sugar"]],
f:["Melatonin, the sleep hormone, is made by the pineal gland.","Oxytocin is sometimes called the cuddle hormone.","Growth hormone is released mostly during deep sleep."]},
hygiene:{s:[
["Germs on hands","Hands touch many surfaces and then the face, so they spread germs easily. People touch their faces many times an hour without noticing. Germs on hands can cause diarrhea, colds, flu, skin infections and eye infections. Handwashing is one of the cheapest and most effective ways to prevent illness."],
["Why soap works","Soap molecules have one end that is attracted to water and another that is attracted to oils and fats. They break apart the oily layer that holds dirt and many germs on skin, and even break apart the fatty outer layer of some viruses. Rinsing washes it all away. Scrubbing for at least 20 seconds is important."],
["When to wash hands","Health experts advise washing hands before eating and preparing food, after using the toilet, after changing diapers, after blowing your nose or coughing, after touching animals and when hands look dirty. Alcohol-based hand sanitizer, with at least 60% alcohol, works when soap and water are not available."],
["Clean water and toilets","Safe drinking water and toilets prevent diseases such as cholera, typhoid and hepatitis A. Billions of people still lack safely managed sanitation. India’s Clean India campaign, launched in 2014, built more than 100 million household toilets. Boiling water or using filters makes water safe to drink."],
["Personal hygiene","Personal hygiene includes bathing regularly, brushing teeth, trimming nails, wearing clean clothes and washing hair. Customs vary: in hot, humid countries many people bathe twice a day, and in Japan the evening bath is a cherished daily ritual. Good hygiene helps prevent skin infections and body odor and boosts confidence."],
["Global Handwashing Day","Global Handwashing Day is held every year on 15 October to promote washing hands with soap. It began in 2008. Schools around the world hold handwashing activities. During the COVID-19 pandemic, handwashing stations appeared at entrances of shops, offices and places of worship."]],
sp:["Florence Nightingale’s hospitals","When Florence Nightingale arrived at the British army hospital at Scutari, near Istanbul, during the Crimean War in 1854, she found filthy wards, blocked drains and rats. Many soldiers died from infections. She organized cleaning, laundry, fresh air, better food and clean water, and the death rate eventually fell sharply. Her work helped make nursing a respected profession, and International Nurses Day is celebrated on her birthday, 12 May."],
g:[["Hygiene","Practices that keep people clean and healthy"],["Sanitation","Safe disposal of human waste"],["Sanitizer","A liquid that kills germs on hands"],["Contamination","Making something unclean with germs"]],
f:["Singing Happy Birthday twice takes about 20 seconds.","Damp towels can grow bacteria, so they should be dried and washed regularly.","Cholera is spread through water contaminated with feces."]},
malaria:{s:[
["Plasmodium","Malaria is caused by tiny single-celled parasites of the genus Plasmodium. Five species infect humans; Plasmodium falciparum is the most deadly, and Plasmodium vivax the most widespread. A monkey malaria, Plasmodium knowlesi, also infects people in parts of Southeast Asia."],
["Anopheles mosquitoes","Malaria is spread by female Anopheles mosquitoes, which bite mainly between dusk and dawn. When an infected mosquito bites, parasites enter the blood, travel to the liver, multiply and then invade red blood cells, destroying them. Another mosquito biting the person can pick up the parasites and spread them."],
["Fever and chills","Symptoms usually appear 10 to 15 days after the bite, with fever, chills, sweating, headache, vomiting and muscle pain. The fever may come in cycles. Without quick treatment, falciparum malaria can cause severe anemia, breathing problems and brain damage. Young children and pregnant women are most at risk."],
["Quinine and artemisinin","For centuries, malaria was treated with quinine, from the bark of the South American cinchona tree. Today, the most effective treatments are artemisinin-based combination therapies. Artemisinin was discovered in the 1970s by Chinese scientist Tu Youyou, from sweet wormwood, a plant in traditional Chinese medicine. She won the Nobel Prize in 2015."],
["Nets and spraying","Insecticide-treated bed nets protect sleepers from mosquitoes and have saved millions of lives. Spraying insecticide on house walls, clearing standing water and quick testing and treatment also reduce malaria. Today about 95 percent of malaria deaths occur in Africa."],
["Vaccines","In 2021 the WHO recommended RTS,S, the first malaria vaccine, for children in areas with high malaria. A second vaccine, R21, was approved in 2023. Millions of children in Africa now receive them. Scientists are also developing new drugs and mosquitoes that cannot spread the parasite."]],
sp:["Ronald Ross","For centuries, people thought malaria was caused by bad air; its name comes from the Italian mala aria. In 1880 French army doctor Alphonse Laveran saw the parasites in a patient’s blood. In 1897, in India, British doctor Ronald Ross dissected mosquitoes and found malaria parasites in the stomach of an Anopheles mosquito that had fed on an infected person, proving that mosquitoes spread the disease. He won the Nobel Prize in 1902. 20 August, the day of his discovery, is World Mosquito Day."],
g:[["Parasite","An organism that lives on or in another"],["Plasmodium","The parasite that causes malaria"],["Anopheles","The mosquito that spreads malaria"],["Bed net","A net protecting sleepers from mosquitoes"]],
f:["World Malaria Day is 25 April.","Most malaria deaths occur among young children in Africa.","More than 40 countries have been certified malaria-free by the WHO."]},
allergy:{s:[
["An overreaction","An allergy is an overreaction of the immune system to a substance that is normally harmless, such as pollen, dust or a food. These substances are called allergens. The immune system mistakes them for dangerous invaders and produces antibodies called immunoglobulin E, IgE."],
["Histamine","When a person is exposed again to an allergen, IgE antibodies on immune cells called mast cells trigger the release of histamine and other chemicals. Histamine causes itching, swelling, sneezing, runny nose, watery eyes and rashes. Antihistamine medicines block its effects."],
["Food allergies","Common food allergens include peanuts, tree nuts, milk, eggs, wheat, soy, fish and shellfish. Which are most common varies by region: peanut allergy is common in Western countries, while shellfish allergy is a frequent cause in much of Asia. Some children outgrow milk and egg allergies, but peanut and shellfish allergies often last for life."],
["Hay fever and asthma","Allergic rhinitis, or hay fever, causes sneezing, a runny or blocked nose and itchy eyes. In temperate countries pollen is a major trigger in spring and summer, while in the tropics dust mites, cockroaches and mold are common causes all year. Asthma narrows the airways, causing wheezing and breathlessness; allergies are a common trigger. Inhalers can open the airways."],
["Insect stings and medicines","Some people are allergic to bee, wasp or ant stings, or to medicines such as penicillin. Anyone who has had a serious reaction should tell doctors, wear a medical alert bracelet and avoid the trigger. Doctors may prescribe an emergency medicine to carry."],
["The hygiene hypothesis","Allergies have become more common in recent decades, especially in cities. One idea, the hygiene hypothesis, suggests that children who grow up with less exposure to germs, farm animals and nature may have immune systems more likely to overreact. Scientists now think diverse microbes, rather than dirt itself, may be important."]],
sp:["Anaphylaxis","Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening allergic reaction that can develop within minutes. Symptoms may include swelling of the throat and tongue, difficulty breathing, wheezing, a drop in blood pressure, dizziness and collapse. It is a medical emergency. People at risk often carry an epinephrine auto-injector, which they inject into the thigh, and then must get to a hospital immediately. Quick action saves lives."],
g:[["Allergen","A substance that triggers an allergy"],["Histamine","A chemical causing allergy symptoms"],["Antihistamine","A medicine that blocks histamine"],["Anaphylaxis","A severe allergic reaction"]],
f:["Dust mites are too small to see and live in bedding and pillows.","Some people are allergic to the latex in rubber gloves.","Introducing peanut foods early in infancy can reduce peanut allergy risk, under medical guidance."]},
spine:{s:[
["33 vertebrae","The spine, or backbone, is a column of 33 bones called vertebrae in babies. Some fuse together, so adults have 24 separate vertebrae plus the sacrum and tailbone. The spine supports the head and body, lets us bend and twist, and protects the spinal cord."],
["Five regions","The spine has five regions: 7 cervical vertebrae in the neck, 12 thoracic vertebrae in the upper back, attached to the ribs, 5 lumbar vertebrae in the lower back, which carry the most weight, the sacrum, made of 5 fused vertebrae, and the coccyx, or tailbone. Nearly all mammals, even giraffes, have 7 neck vertebrae."],
["Shock absorbers","Between the vertebrae are intervertebral discs, pads with a tough outer ring and a soft, jelly-like center. They act as shock absorbers when we walk, run and jump. Discs lose water during the day and regain it at night, so people are slightly taller in the morning."],
["A cable of nerves","The spinal cord is a bundle of nerves about 45 centimeters long running through a canal in the vertebrae from the brain to the lower back. It carries messages between the brain and body and controls reflexes, such as pulling your hand from something hot. Damage to it can cause paralysis."],
["Back pain","Back pain is one of the most common health problems and a leading cause of missed work. It is often caused by strained muscles, poor posture, lifting heavy objects the wrong way or lack of exercise. Most back pain improves within weeks with gentle activity. Strong core muscles help support the spine."],
["Scoliosis","Scoliosis is a sideways curve of the spine, often appearing during the growth spurt of early adolescence. Many cases are mild, but some need a brace or surgery. School screenings and doctors check for it by looking at the back while a child bends forward."]],
sp:["Heavy school bags","Many students carry heavy school bags, which can strain their backs and shoulders. Health experts suggest that a bag should weigh no more than about 10 to 15% of a child’s body weight. Using both straps, packing the heaviest items closest to the back and carrying only what is needed help. Some countries have set limits on school bag weight, and many schools provide lockers or digital textbooks."],
g:[["Vertebra","One of the bones of the spine"],["Spinal cord","The bundle of nerves inside the spine"],["Disc","A cushion between vertebrae"],["Posture","The way the body is held"]],
f:["Astronauts can grow a few centimeters taller in space.","The spine has natural S-shaped curves for balance and shock absorption.","Sitting slumped puts more pressure on lower back discs than standing."]}
});

;
/* data/animals-load.js */
// Loader for the Animal Kingdom articles in data/animals1.js to animals5.js.
// Each article object: id, t (title), i (icon), w (Wikipedia page for the photo), r (related ids),
// p (three short opening sections), l (lead), s (four sections), k (key facts), d (key dates),
// s2 (six in-depth sections), sp (Spotlight), g (key words), f (Did you know facts), fun (fact of the day),
// tl (Filipino summary) and cons (IUCN status, optional).
window.ADDART=a=>{const M=window.MORE=window.MORE||[],D=window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{},D2=window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};
window.TL=window.TL||{};window.WIKI=window.WIKI||{};window.FUN=window.FUN||{};window.CONS_NEW=window.CONS_NEW||{};
M.push({id:a.id,t:a.t,c:a.c||"Animals and Nature",i:a.i,p:a.p,r:a.r||[]});
D[a.id]={l:a.l,s:a.s,k:a.k,d:a.d};D2[a.id]={s:a.s2,sp:a.sp,g:a.g,f:a.f};
if(a.tl)TL[a.id]=a.tl;if(a.w)WIKI[a.id]=a.w;if(a.fun)FUN[a.id]=a.fun;if(a.cons)CONS_NEW[a.id]=a.cons};

;
/* data/animals1.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 1: lions, gorillas, bears and giraffes.
ADDART({id:"lion",t:"Lions",i:"🦁",w:"Lion",r:["tiger","eco","sahara"],cons:["VU","Lion"],
p:[["The social cat","Lions are the only big cats that live in groups. A pride usually has a few related females, their cubs and one or more adult males."],["Hunters of the grasslands","Lionesses do most of the hunting, often working as a team to catch zebras, wildebeest and buffalo."],["A shrinking range","Lions once lived across Africa, southern Europe and western Asia. Today most live in Africa south of the Sahara, with a small population in India."]],
l:"The lion is a powerful big cat of the African savanna and one of the most famous animals on Earth. Lions are the only cats that live in family groups called prides, and their deep roars, golden coats and the males’ shaggy manes have made them symbols of strength for thousands of years.",
s:[["Prides","A pride may have from three to about thirty lions. Its core is a group of related females who stay together for life, while young males leave when they are two or three years old. Males that take over a pride defend its territory with roars, scent marks and fights."],["The mane","Only male lions grow a mane. A dark, full mane shows that a male is healthy and strong, which helps him attract females and warn rivals. Manes also protect the neck during fights. Some lions in hot places, such as Tsavo in Kenya, have very small manes or none."],["Hunting together","Lions hunt mostly at dusk, at night or at dawn. A group of lionesses spreads out and creeps toward a herd, then some chase the prey toward others waiting in ambush. Only about one hunt in four succeeds. After a large kill, a lion may not eat again for several days."],["Asiatic lions","A small number of lions survive in Asia, in and around the Gir Forest of Gujarat, India. They were close to extinction in the early 1900s, with perhaps only a few dozen left. Strict protection helped them recover, and a 2020 count found 674 Asiatic lions."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Panthera leo"],["Weight","Males about 150–250 kg"],["Length","Up to about 3 m including tail"],["Lifespan","About 10–14 years in the wild"],["Roar","Can be heard about 8 km away"]],
d:[["c. 30,000 BCE","Cave lions painted on the walls of Chauvet Cave, France"],["1965","Gir Forest in India made a protected sanctuary for Asiatic lions"],["1996","Lions first listed as Vulnerable on the IUCN Red List"],["2013","World Lion Day first observed on 10 August"],["2020","Count finds 674 Asiatic lions in Gujarat"]],
s2:[["Cubs","A lioness gives birth to two to four cubs after a pregnancy of about 110 days. She hides them in thick bushes for the first weeks, then brings them to the pride. Females often give birth around the same time and nurse each other’s cubs. Many cubs do not survive their first two years."],["Rest and sleep","Lions spend up to 20 hours a day resting or sleeping, usually in the shade. Hunting takes a great deal of energy, and resting during the heat of the day saves it. When lions are active, they patrol their territory, mark it with scent and roar to keep rivals away."],["The roar","A lion’s roar can be heard about 8 kilometers away. Lions roar most often at night and at dawn, telling other prides where they are. Members of a pride sometimes roar together in a chorus. The special shape of the voice box lets lions, tigers, leopards and jaguars roar."],["Lions and other animals","Lions compete with spotted hyenas for food. Hyenas sometimes steal lion kills, and lions sometimes steal from hyenas. Lions also kill cheetah and leopard cubs. By keeping grazing animals moving, lions help stop the grass in one area from being eaten down completely."],["Threats","Lion numbers in Africa have fallen by about half since the early 1990s. Farms and towns take over their land, and lions that kill cattle may be killed by herders in return. Lions are also hunted for sport and for their bones. Fewer than 25,000 are thought to remain in the wild."],["Symbols of power","Lions appear on coats of arms, flags and statues around the world, from ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt to medieval Europe. The Sphinx has a lion’s body. Singapore’s name means lion city in Sanskrit, and lion dances are performed at Chinese New Year wherever Chinese communities live."]],
sp:["The lion dance","At Chinese New Year, in China and in Chinatowns around the world, performers dance inside colorful lion costumes to drums, gongs and cymbals. The lion is believed to scare away bad luck and bring good fortune. Two dancers work together, one as the head and one as the body and tail."],
g:[["Pride","A family group of lions"],["Mane","The long hair around a male lion’s head and neck"],["Ambush","A surprise attack from a hiding place"],["Territory","An area an animal defends against others"]],
f:["Lions can sleep up to 20 hours a day.","A lion’s roar can be heard about 8 kilometers away.","Lionesses do most of a pride’s hunting."],
fun:"Lions are the only big cats that live in family groups.",
tl:"Ang leon ay malaking pusa na nakatira sa mga grupong tinatawag na pride sa mga damuhan ng Aprika. Ang mga babaeng leon ang kadalasang nangangaso, at may makapal na buhok o mane sa leeg ang mga lalaki."});

ADDART({id:"gorilla",t:"Gorillas and Great Apes",i:"🦍",w:"Gorilla",r:["evo","dna","elephant"],cons:["CR","Western gorilla"],
p:[["Largest primates","Gorillas are the biggest of all primates. Adult males, called silverbacks, can weigh more than 160 kg."],["Gentle giants","Despite their size, gorillas are shy plant eaters that live quietly in family groups in the forests of central Africa."],["Our close cousins","Gorillas, chimpanzees, bonobos and orangutans are the great apes, the closest living relatives of humans."]],
l:"Gorillas are the largest living primates, powerful forest apes from the heart of Africa. They live in close family groups led by a silverback male and eat mostly leaves, stems and fruit. With chimpanzees, bonobos and orangutans, they form the great apes, our nearest relatives in the animal world.",
s:[["Two species","There are two species of gorilla. The western gorilla lives in the lowland forests of countries such as Gabon, Cameroon and the Republic of the Congo. The eastern gorilla lives farther east, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Rwanda and Uganda, and includes the famous mountain gorillas."],["The silverback","A group, or troop, is usually led by one adult male called a silverback because of the silver hair on his back. He protects the group, settles quarrels and decides where it travels and rests. A troop may have several females and their young."],["Daily life","Gorillas spend most of the day eating and resting. They walk on all fours, leaning on the knuckles of their hands. Each evening, every gorilla except young babies builds a fresh nest of bent branches and leaves on the ground or in a tree to sleep in."],["Mountain gorillas","Mountain gorillas live in the cool, misty forests of the Virunga volcanoes and Bwindi Impenetrable Forest. Their numbers fell to a few hundred in the 1980s. Careful protection and tourism that pays for rangers helped them recover to more than 1,000 by 2018."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Gorilla gorilla and Gorilla beringei"],["Weight","Males up to about 200 kg"],["Height","Males about 1.7 m when standing"],["Lifespan","About 35–40 years in the wild"],["Diet","Mostly leaves, stems and fruit"]],
d:[["1847","Western gorilla first described by scientists"],["1967","Dian Fossey founds the Karisoke Research Center in Rwanda"],["1985","Dian Fossey is murdered at her camp"],["2018","Mountain gorilla moved from Critically Endangered to Endangered"],["2018","Survey counts more than 1,000 mountain gorillas"]],
s2:[["Great apes","The great apes are gorillas, chimpanzees, bonobos and orangutans, together with humans. They have no tails, large brains and long arms. Humans share about 98 percent of their DNA with gorillas and about 99 percent with chimpanzees. Orangutans live in Asia, on Borneo and Sumatra; the others live in Africa."],["Chimpanzees and bonobos","Chimpanzees live in noisy communities and make tools, such as sticks for fishing termites out of their mounds and stones for cracking nuts. Bonobos look similar but are more peaceful and are led by females. Jane Goodall’s studies of chimpanzees in Tanzania, begun in 1960, changed how scientists see animals."],["Orangutans","Orangutans are the red apes of the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra and spend most of their lives in the trees. Their name comes from Malay words meaning person of the forest. Mothers care for their young for up to eight years, longer than any animal except humans."],["Clever communicators","Gorillas use many sounds, from grunts and barks to the loud hoots and chest beats of a silverback. In captivity, some apes have learned hundreds of signs. A gorilla named Koko learned more than 1,000 signs based on American Sign Language, though scientists debate how much she understood."],["Threats","All gorillas are endangered. Forests are cleared for farms, logging and mining, and gorillas are hunted for bushmeat. Gorillas can also catch human diseases, and the Ebola virus has killed thousands. Western gorillas and eastern gorillas are both listed as Critically Endangered."],["Saving gorillas","National parks, rangers and local communities protect gorillas from poachers. In Rwanda and Uganda, visitors pay for permits to watch mountain gorillas for one hour, and the money supports the parks and nearby villages. Each year Rwanda holds a naming ceremony, Kwita Izina, for newborn gorillas."]],
sp:["Dian Fossey","The American scientist Dian Fossey spent 18 years living among mountain gorillas in Rwanda. She learned to recognize each animal by its nose print and won their trust by copying their sounds and behavior. Her book Gorillas in the Mist made the world aware of the threats they faced. She was killed at her camp in 1985."],
g:[["Primate","A mammal group that includes monkeys, apes and humans"],["Silverback","An adult male gorilla with silver hair on his back"],["Troop","A family group of gorillas"],["Bushmeat","Meat from wild animals hunted for food"]],
f:["Gorillas build a new sleeping nest every night.","Each gorilla has a unique nose print.","Humans share about 98 percent of their DNA with gorillas."],
fun:"Every gorilla has a unique nose print, like a human fingerprint.",
tl:"Ang gorilya ang pinakamalaking unggoy o primate sa mundo at nakatira sa mga gubat ng gitnang Aprika. Kumakain ito ng dahon at prutas, at malapit na kamag-anak ng tao kasama ang chimpanzee at orangutan."});

ADDART({id:"bear",t:"Bears",i:"🐻",w:"Brown bear",r:["arctic","wolf","eco"],cons:["VU","Polar bear"],
p:[["Eight species","There are eight kinds of bear: brown, American black, Asiatic black, polar, sun, sloth and spectacled bears, and the giant panda."],["Big and strong","Bears have heavy bodies, short tails, strong claws and an excellent sense of smell."],["Most eat plants too","Except for the polar bear, most bears eat a mix of plants, insects, fish and meat."]],
l:"Bears are large, heavy mammals with thick fur, strong claws and a keen sense of smell. The eight species live in North and South America, Europe and Asia, from the Arctic ice to tropical rainforests. Some sleep through the winter, and most eat almost anything, from berries to salmon.",
s:[["Where bears live","Brown bears roam forests and mountains across North America, Europe and northern Asia. Polar bears live on the Arctic sea ice. Sun bears and sloth bears live in South and Southeast Asia, the spectacled bear lives in the Andes of South America, and the giant panda lives in the bamboo forests of China."],["The biggest and smallest","The largest bears are polar bears and Kodiak brown bears, whose males can weigh more than 600 kg. The smallest is the sun bear of Southeast Asia, which weighs only about 25 to 65 kg and has a golden patch on its chest shaped like a rising sun."],["Winter sleep","In cold places, many bears spend the winter in a den, living on stored body fat. Their heartbeat and breathing slow down, and they may go months without eating or drinking. Mothers give birth to tiny cubs during this winter rest and feed them milk until spring."],["A super sense of smell","A bear’s nose is one of the best in the animal world. Polar bears can smell a seal through ice and snow, and brown bears can smell food from kilometers away. This helps bears find food, but it also draws them to campsites and garbage."]],
k:[["Species","8"],["Largest","Polar bear and Kodiak bear, males over 600 kg"],["Smallest","Sun bear, about 25–65 kg"],["Lifespan","About 20–30 years"],["Top speed","Brown bear about 50 km/h"]],
d:[["1902","The teddy bear is named after US President Theodore Roosevelt"],["1961","The giant panda becomes the symbol of the World Wildlife Fund"],["1973","Five Arctic countries sign an agreement to protect polar bears"],["2008","Polar bear listed as threatened under US law"],["2016","Giant panda moved from Endangered to Vulnerable"]],
s2:[["Polar bears","Polar bears are the largest land carnivores. They hunt seals from the sea ice and can swim for days. Under their white-looking fur, their skin is black, which helps soak up heat. As the Arctic warms and sea ice melts earlier each year, polar bears have less time to hunt."],["Brown bears and grizzlies","Brown bears include the grizzlies of North America and the huge Kodiak bears of Alaska. In summer, brown bears gather at rivers to catch salmon swimming upstream, sometimes snatching fish from the air. They also dig for roots and ground squirrels with their long front claws."],["The giant panda","The giant panda eats almost nothing but bamboo, munching for up to 14 hours a day. A special wrist bone works like a thumb to grip the stalks. Pandas live wild only in the mountains of central China. Their numbers have grown thanks to reserves and bamboo corridors."],["Bears of Asia","Asiatic black bears have a white V on their chests. Sloth bears use their long lips and missing front teeth to suck up termites. The sun bear of Southeast Asia, the smallest bear, has the longest tongue of any bear, used to lick honey and insects."],["Bears and people","Bears appear in folk tales, constellations such as the Great Bear and stories like Winnie-the-Pooh. They can also be dangerous, so people in bear country store food carefully. Some bears are still killed for their body parts or kept captive, and many lose their forests to farms."],["Cubs","Bear cubs are tiny at birth. A newborn panda weighs only about 100 grams, about 1/900 of its mother’s weight. Cubs stay with their mothers for one to three years, learning where to find food and how to stay safe. Mother bears are fiercely protective."]],
sp:["How the teddy bear got its name","In 1902 US President Theodore Roosevelt, on a hunting trip in Mississippi, refused to shoot a bear that had been tied to a tree. A newspaper cartoon showed the scene, and a toy shop owner in New York made a stuffed bear and called it Teddy’s bear. The toy became popular around the world."],
g:[["Carnivore","An animal that eats mainly meat"],["Den","A sheltered home where an animal rests or gives birth"],["Hibernation","A long, deep winter rest"],["Omnivore","An animal that eats both plants and animals"]],
f:["A polar bear’s skin is black under its fur.","A newborn giant panda weighs only about 100 grams.","The sun bear has the longest tongue of any bear."],
fun:"Under its thick white-looking fur, a polar bear has black skin.",
tl:"Ang oso ay malaking mamalya na may makapal na balahibo, matitibay na kuko at matalas na pang-amoy. May walong uri nito, kabilang ang polar bear at ang giant panda, ngunit walang katutubong oso sa Pilipinas."});

ADDART({id:"giraffe",t:"Giraffes",i:"🦒",w:"Giraffe",r:["elephant","lion","rift"],cons:["VU","Giraffe"],
p:[["The tallest animal","Giraffes are the tallest animals on Earth. A large male can stand about 5.5 meters, taller than a two-story house."],["Long necks","A giraffe’s neck can be nearly 2 meters long, yet it has only seven bones, the same number as a human neck."],["Leaf eaters","Giraffes use their height and long tongues to reach leaves at the tops of acacia trees on the African savanna."]],
l:"The giraffe is the tallest animal in the world, a long-legged, long-necked browser of the African savanna. Its patterned coat, gentle eyes and great height make it one of the most recognizable animals on Earth. Giraffes are quieter and more threatened than many people realize.",
s:[["Built for height","A giraffe’s legs alone can be taller than an adult person. Its long neck lets it feed on leaves that other grazers cannot reach. To pump blood all the way up to its brain, a giraffe has a heart that weighs about 11 kg and very high blood pressure."],["Tongue and diet","A giraffe’s dark tongue is about 45 to 50 centimeters long. It wraps around thorny acacia branches and strips off the leaves. Thick, sticky saliva protects its mouth from the thorns. A giraffe can eat about 30 kg of leaves a day and gets much of its water from them."],["Patterns","Every giraffe has its own pattern of patches, like a fingerprint. Under each patch is a network of blood vessels that may help release body heat. Giraffes in different parts of Africa have different patterns, from the jagged patches of the Masai giraffe to the neat net of the reticulated giraffe."],["One species or four?","For a long time, all giraffes were treated as one species with several subspecies. In 2016 a genetic study suggested there are actually four separate species. Scientists still debate the question, and the IUCN Red List continues to treat giraffes as one species."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Giraffa camelopardalis"],["Height","Males up to about 5.5 m"],["Weight","Up to about 1,900 kg"],["Neck bones","7, like a human"],["Lifespan","About 20–25 years in the wild"]],
d:[["1414","A giraffe from East Africa is presented to the Ming emperor of China"],["1827","Zarafa the giraffe arrives in Paris and becomes a celebrity"],["2014","World Giraffe Day first held on 21 June"],["2016","Giraffe listed as Vulnerable on the IUCN Red List"],["2016","Genetic study proposes four giraffe species"]],
s2:[["Babies that fall","A giraffe mother gives birth standing up, so the calf drops about 2 meters to the ground. The fall breaks the birth sac and starts the baby breathing. Within an hour, the calf can stand and walk. Newborn calves are already about 1.8 meters tall."],["Sleeping and drinking","Giraffes sleep very little, often less than an hour a day in short naps, and usually standing up. Drinking is awkward and risky: a giraffe must spread its front legs wide or bend its knees to lower its head to the water, which leaves it open to attack by lions."],["Defense","An adult giraffe is too tall for most predators, but lions sometimes hunt them. A giraffe defends itself with powerful kicks that can kill a lion. Its height also gives it a good view of approaching danger, and other animals such as zebras often stay near giraffes."],["Necking","Male giraffes fight by swinging their long necks and hitting each other with their heads, a contest called necking. The short, horn-like knobs on their heads, called ossicones, are covered with skin and hair. The winner usually earns the chance to mate."],["The okapi","The giraffe’s only living close relative is the okapi of the rainforests of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. It has a shorter neck, a dark brown body and zebra-like stripes on its legs. Western scientists did not describe the okapi until 1901."],["Silent decline","Giraffe numbers fell by about 40 percent between the 1980s and 2015. Their habitat is turned into farms, and some are hunted for meat. About 117,000 remain in the wild. Conservation groups now move giraffes to safe areas where they once lived, a process called translocation."]],
sp:["The giraffe that came to China","In 1414 a giraffe from Malindi, in present-day Kenya, arrived at the court of the Yongle Emperor in China on one of Admiral Zheng He’s voyages. Officials said it was a qilin, a magical creature that brings good luck, and court artists painted it. The voyage shows how far trade routes stretched across the Indian Ocean."],
g:[["Browser","An animal that eats leaves, shoots and twigs"],["Ossicones","The skin-covered horns on a giraffe’s head"],["Necking","Fighting by swinging the neck"],["Subspecies","A group within a species that looks a little different"]],
f:["A giraffe’s neck has only seven bones, like ours.","A newborn giraffe falls about 2 meters at birth.","Giraffes often sleep less than an hour a day."],
fun:"A giraffe’s neck has the same number of bones as yours: seven.",
tl:"Ang dyirap ang pinakamatangkad na hayop sa mundo, na umaabot sa mga 5.5 metro. Gamit ang mahabang leeg at dila, kumakain ito ng mga dahon sa tuktok ng mga puno sa Aprika."});

;
