// Animal Kingdom articles, part 2: kangaroos, the Philippine tarsier, birds and eagles.
ADDART({id:"kangaroo",t:"Kangaroos and Marsupials",i:"🦘",w:"Red kangaroo",r:["evo","eco","whale"],cons:["LC","Red kangaroo"],
p:[["Pouched mammals","Kangaroos are marsupials, mammals whose babies finish growing in a pouch on the mother’s belly."],["Champion hoppers","Kangaroos travel by hopping on their big back legs, using their long tails for balance."],["Australia’s animals","Most of the world’s marsupials, including kangaroos, koalas and wombats, live in Australia and New Guinea."]],
l:"Kangaroos are hopping marsupials found in Australia and New Guinea. Like all marsupials, they give birth to tiny, undeveloped young that grow inside the mother’s pouch. The red kangaroo is the largest marsupial alive, and kangaroos have become a national symbol of Australia.",
s:[["What is a marsupial?","Marsupials are mammals that give birth to very small young after a short pregnancy. The newborn crawls into its mother’s pouch, attaches to a teat and keeps growing there for months. There are more than 300 kinds of marsupial, including kangaroos, koalas, wombats, possums, Tasmanian devils and the opossums of the Americas."],["Hopping","A kangaroo’s back legs work like springs. Stretchy tendons store energy each time it lands and release it on the next jump, so fast hopping uses surprisingly little energy. Red kangaroos can reach about 55 km/h for short distances and cover around 8 meters in a single bound."],["The joey","A newborn kangaroo, called a joey, is only about 2 centimeters long, the size of a jelly bean. It is born blind and hairless after about a month of pregnancy and climbs through its mother’s fur to the pouch. A red kangaroo joey stays inside for about eight months."],["Mobs","Kangaroos live in groups called mobs. They graze on grass mostly in the early morning, evening and at night, and rest in the shade during the heat of the day. Large males, called boomers, sometimes fight by grabbing each other, kicking with their back legs and leaning on their tails."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Osphranter rufus (red kangaroo)"],["Height","Males up to about 1.8 m standing"],["Weight","Males up to about 90 kg"],["Top speed","About 55 km/h"],["Pregnancy","About 33 days"]],
d:[["c. 45,000 years ago","Giant kangaroos and other huge marsupials die out in Australia"],["1770","Cook’s expedition records the word kangaroo in Queensland"],["1908","Kangaroo and emu appear on Australia’s coat of arms"],["1936","The last known thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, dies in a zoo"],["1984","Kangaroo appears on the Australian one-dollar coin"]],
s2:[["Where the name came from","In 1770 the crew of James Cook’s ship Endeavour saw kangaroos while repairing the ship in northern Queensland. They learned the word gangurru from the local Guugu Yimithirr people. The word was written down as kangaroo and spread into English and many other languages."],["Kinds of kangaroo","The red kangaroo of the dry interior is the largest. Eastern and western grey kangaroos live in forests and grasslands. Smaller relatives are called wallabies and wallaroos. Tree kangaroos of New Guinea and northern Queensland have strong front legs and climb in the rainforest canopy."],["Koalas and wombats","Koalas live in eucalyptus trees and sleep up to 20 hours a day because their leafy diet gives them little energy. Wombats are stocky burrowers with backward-facing pouches, so no soil gets in while they dig. Wombats are famous for their cube-shaped droppings."],["Marsupials of the Americas","The Virginia opossum of North America is the only marsupial found north of Mexico. When threatened, it may play dead, lying still and giving off a bad smell. South America has many small opossums. Long ago, marsupials also lived in Antarctica when it was warmer and joined to Australia and South America."],["Lost giants","Until about 45,000 years ago, Australia was home to giant marsupials, including a short-faced kangaroo taller than a person and the hippo-sized Diprotodon. The thylacine, a striped meat eater also called the Tasmanian tiger, was hunted to extinction; the last known one died in a zoo in 1936."],["Kangaroos today","Unlike many animals, large kangaroos are common, numbering in the tens of millions. Farm dams and pastures have given them more water and grass. Kangaroos can damage crops, so their numbers are managed. They are also a danger on country roads at dusk, when they often hop in front of cars."]],
sp:["The Wallace Line","Marsupials live mainly in Australia, New Guinea and the Americas. The westernmost wild marsupials in Asia are cuscuses, slow, woolly tree climbers found on Sulawesi and other islands of eastern Indonesia. An invisible boundary called the Wallace Line, running between Bali and Lombok and between Borneo and Sulawesi, roughly separates the animals of Asia from those of Australia. It is named after the naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace, who noticed it in the 1850s."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal whose young grow in a pouch"],["Joey","A baby kangaroo"],["Mob","A group of kangaroos"],["Tendon","A strong cord joining muscle to bone"]],
f:["A newborn kangaroo is about the size of a jelly bean.","Kangaroos cannot easily walk backward.","Wombats produce cube-shaped droppings."],
fun:"A newborn kangaroo is only about 2 centimeters long.",
tl:"Ang kanggaru ay marsupial, isang mamalya na nagpapalaki ng anak sa isang supot sa tiyan. Tumatalon ito gamit ang malalakas na hulihang paa at nakatira sa Australia at New Guinea."});

ADDART({id:"tarsier",t:"The Philippine Tarsier",i:"🐒",w:"Philippine tarsier",r:["bohol","phbio","mindanao"],cons:["NT","Philippine tarsier"],
p:[["A tiny primate","The Philippine tarsier is one of the smallest primates in the world, small enough to sit in the palm of a hand."],["Enormous eyes","Each of its eyes is about as big as its whole brain, helping it see in the dark."],["A Philippine treasure","It lives only in the southeastern Philippines, including Bohol, Samar, Leyte and Mindanao."]],
l:"The Philippine tarsier is a tiny, wide-eyed primate found only in the southeastern Philippines. Active at night, it leaps between branches to catch insects and has become one of the country’s best-loved animals and a symbol of Bohol. It is shy, sensitive and protected by law.",
s:[["Size","An adult Philippine tarsier has a body only about 9 to 16 centimeters long and weighs about 80 to 160 grams, less than a small mango. Its thin tail is about twice as long as its body. Long fingers with round pads help it grip branches."],["Eyes and head","A tarsier cannot move its huge eyes in their sockets. Instead, it can turn its head about 180 degrees to look behind itself, like an owl. Its large, thin ears can move separately to pick up the sounds of insects in the dark."],["A night hunter","Tarsiers sleep during the day, clinging to tree trunks in thick vegetation. At night they hunt crickets, grasshoppers, beetles and other small animals, catching them with their hands after a sudden leap. They can jump many times their own body length from tree to tree."],["Where they live","Philippine tarsiers live in forests and thickets on Bohol, Samar, Leyte, Mindanao and nearby islands. They need dense cover and quiet. The species is listed as Near Threatened because its forests are cleared and some animals are caught for the pet trade, where they rarely survive."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Carlito syrichta"],["Body length","About 9–16 cm"],["Weight","About 80–160 g"],["Head turn","About 180 degrees"],["Found in","Bohol, Samar, Leyte and Mindanao"]],
d:[["1758","Linnaeus gives the tarsier its first scientific name"],["1996","Philippine Tarsier Foundation set up in Bohol"],["1997","Proclamation No. 1030 makes the tarsier a specially protected species"],["2010","Scientists place the Philippine tarsier in its own genus, Carlito"],["2012","Study finds tarsiers communicate with ultrasound"]],
s2:[["Silent screams","In 2012 scientists discovered that Philippine tarsiers make calls at about 70 kilohertz, far too high for humans to hear. Many seemed to be screaming silently. These ultrasonic calls may let tarsiers warn each other without being heard by predators, and their ears are tuned to them."],["A very old family","Tarsiers belong to an ancient group of primates. Fossils show that tarsier relatives lived in Asia more than 45 million years ago. They are neither monkeys nor lemurs. Some scientists think they are more closely related to monkeys, apes and humans than lemurs are."],["Sensitive to stress","Tarsiers are easily stressed by noise, bright light, touch and crowds. Stressed animals may stop eating or injure themselves. For this reason, visitors at sanctuaries are asked to stay quiet, keep their distance and never use camera flashes. Tarsiers kept as pets rarely live long."],["Family life","A female gives birth to a single baby after a pregnancy of about six months. The baby is born with fur and open eyes and can climb within a day. The mother sometimes carries it in her mouth. Young tarsiers become independent after a few months."],["Local names","In Boholano the tarsier is called mamag or maumag, and in other places it has names such as magau and malmag. Some people once believed tarsiers brought bad luck. Today it appears on the 200-peso banknote, on souvenirs and on many tourism posters."],["Protection","The tarsier is protected by Proclamation No. 1030 of 1997 and by the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act of 2001. It is illegal to catch, sell or keep a tarsier. The Philippine Tarsier Sanctuary in Corella, Bohol, protects forest where wild tarsiers can live naturally."]],
sp:["Visiting the Tarsier Sanctuary","At the Philippine Tarsier Sanctuary in Corella, Bohol, guides lead visitors along a forest path to spot tarsiers resting in the trees. Talking loudly, touching and flash photography are not allowed. The sanctuary protects forest habitat and supports research and education about these tiny primates."],
g:[["Primate","A mammal group that includes tarsiers, monkeys and apes"],["Nocturnal","Active at night"],["Ultrasound","Sound too high for humans to hear"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"]],
f:["Each tarsier eye is about as big as its brain.","A tarsier can turn its head about 180 degrees.","Philippine tarsiers make calls too high for humans to hear."],
fun:"Each of a tarsier’s eyes is about as large as its brain.",
tl:"Ang mamag o tarsier ay isa sa pinakamaliit na primate sa mundo at matatagpuan lamang sa timog-silangang Pilipinas, gaya ng Bohol. Malalaki ang mata nito at nangangaso ng insekto sa gabi; protektado ito ng batas."});

ADDART({id:"birds",t:"Birds",i:"🐦",w:"Bird",r:["dino","evo","penguin"],
p:[["Feathered animals","Birds are the only animals with feathers. They lay hard-shelled eggs and have beaks instead of teeth."],["Living dinosaurs","Scientists agree that birds evolved from small, feathered meat-eating dinosaurs."],["Everywhere on Earth","About 11,000 species of birds live on every continent, from Antarctic penguins to tropical hummingbirds."]],
l:"Birds are warm-blooded animals with feathers, beaks and wings, and most of them can fly. They live on every continent and in almost every habitat. Birds pollinate flowers, spread seeds and control insects, and their songs and colors have delighted people for thousands of years. They are also the living descendants of dinosaurs.",
s:[["Built to fly","A flying bird has hollow, light bones, a strong breastbone for its flight muscles and feathers shaped to lift it into the air. Air sacs connected to its lungs give it a steady supply of oxygen. Some birds, such as ostriches, penguins and kiwis, have lost the ability to fly."],["Feathers","Feathers keep birds warm and dry, help them fly and show off bright colors. A bird replaces its old feathers in a process called molting. Down feathers trap warm air, while stiff flight feathers on the wings and tail steer and lift. No other living animal has feathers."],["Eggs and nests","All birds lay eggs, usually in a nest. Nests may be cups of grass, holes in trees, mud pots or simple scrapes on the ground. Parents keep the eggs warm until they hatch. Many chicks are born blind and helpless, while ducklings and chickens can walk soon after hatching."],["Birds of the world","About 11,000 bird species live on every continent. South America has the most, earning it the name the bird continent, and Colombia, Peru and Brazil each have more than 1,800 species. Islands such as New Zealand, Madagascar, Hawaii and the Philippines have many birds found nowhere else, and island birds are especially at risk of extinction."]],
k:[["Species","About 11,000"],["Largest","Ostrich, up to about 2.7 m tall"],["Smallest","Bee hummingbird, about 6 cm long"],["Fastest","Peregrine falcon, over 300 km/h in a dive"],["Longest migration","Arctic tern, about 70,000 km a year"]],
d:[["c. 1681","The dodo becomes extinct on Mauritius"],["1861","Archaeopteryx, a feathered dinosaur-bird fossil, is described"],["1914","Martha, the last passenger pigeon, dies"],["1922","The organization now called BirdLife International is founded"],["1996","Sinosauropteryx shows that some dinosaurs had feathers"]],
s2:[["From dinosaurs to birds","In 1861, two years after Darwin’s Origin of Species, a fossil called Archaeopteryx was described in Germany. It had feathers and wings but also teeth and a bony tail. Since the 1990s, many feathered dinosaurs have been found in China, showing that birds are living theropod dinosaurs, relatives of Velociraptor."],["Migration","Many birds fly long distances each year between their breeding grounds and places with more food. The Arctic tern flies from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back, about 70,000 kilometers a year. Birds find their way using the Sun, the stars, landmarks and Earth’s magnetic field."],["Songs and calls","Birds use calls to warn of danger and keep in touch, and songs to attract mates and defend territories. Young songbirds learn their songs by listening to adults. Some, like mynas and lyrebirds, copy other sounds, even car alarms. Many birds sing a dawn chorus at sunrise."],["Beaks for every job","A bird’s beak shows what it eats. Eagles have hooked beaks for tearing meat, hummingbirds have long thin beaks for sipping nectar, and parrots have strong curved beaks for cracking seeds. Darwin noticed that finches on the Galápagos Islands had different beaks suited to different foods."],["Extinction","Since 1500, more than 150 bird species have died out, including the dodo of Mauritius and the passenger pigeon of North America, once the most numerous bird on Earth. Today about one in eight bird species is threatened, mainly by habitat loss, hunting, pollution and introduced predators such as rats and cats."],["Watching birds","Birdwatching, or birding, is one of the world’s most popular hobbies. People record what they see, helping scientists track bird numbers. Events such as the Christmas Bird Count, begun in North America in 1900, and the Global Big Day count birds across the world, and wetlands along migration routes attract huge flocks."]],
sp:["The dodo","The dodo was a large, flightless pigeon that lived only on Mauritius in the Indian Ocean. It had no fear of people, and after sailors arrived in the late 1500s it was hunted, and introduced rats, pigs and monkeys ate its eggs. The last confirmed sighting was in 1662. The phrase dead as a dodo still means gone forever."],
g:[["Molting","Shedding old feathers and growing new ones"],["Migration","A regular journey between two places"],["Theropod","A group of two-legged, mostly meat-eating dinosaurs"],["Down","Soft, fluffy feathers that keep a bird warm"]],
f:["Birds are the only living animals with feathers.","The Arctic tern flies about 70,000 kilometers a year.","A peregrine falcon can dive at more than 300 km/h."],
fun:"The Arctic tern sees more daylight than any other animal, flying between the Arctic and Antarctic summers.",
tl:"Ang ibon ay hayop na may balahibo, tuka at pakpak, at karamihan ay nakalilipad. May mga 11,000 uri nito sa mundo, at higit sa 600 sa Pilipinas, kung saan halos ikatlo ay dito lamang matatagpuan."});

ADDART({id:"eagle",t:"Eagles and the Philippine Eagle",i:"🦅",w:"Philippine eagle",r:["phbio","mindanao","birds"],cons:["CR","Philippine eagle"],
p:[["Birds of prey","Eagles are large birds of prey with hooked beaks, sharp talons and superb eyesight."],["Our national bird","The Philippine eagle, found only in the Philippines, is one of the largest and rarest eagles in the world."],["Symbols of power","Eagles appear on flags, seals and coats of arms in many countries."]],
l:"Eagles are powerful birds of prey that hunt with sharp talons and keen eyes. There are around 60 species around the world. The Philippines is home to one of the largest and most magnificent, the Philippine eagle, the country’s national bird and one of the most endangered birds of prey on Earth.",
s:[["What makes an eagle","Eagles are large members of the hawk family. They have broad wings for soaring, strong feet with curved talons for gripping prey and hooked beaks for tearing meat. Their eyes are many times sharper than ours, letting them spot prey from far away."],["The Philippine eagle","The Philippine eagle stands about 1 meter tall and has a wingspan of about 2 meters. It has a shaggy crest of brown and cream feathers, a deep blue-gray beak and pale blue eyes. It lives in the rainforests of Mindanao, Luzon, Samar and Leyte."],["Hunting","Philippine eagles hunt in the forest, often in pairs. They catch flying lemurs, called colugos, as well as monkeys, civets, snakes, hornbills and other birds. Because they were once thought to eat mainly monkeys, they were long called monkey-eating eagles."],["Slow to breed","A Philippine eagle pair stays together for life and raises just one chick every two years. Both parents care for it for about 20 months. With so few young, the population recovers very slowly. Scientists estimate there are about 400 breeding pairs left in the wild."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Pithecophaga jefferyi"],["Wingspan","About 2 m"],["Length","Up to about 1 m"],["Breeding pairs","About 400 in the wild"],["National bird","Since 1995"]],
d:[["1896","John Whitehead collects the first Philippine eagle known to science, on Samar"],["1978","The monkey-eating eagle is renamed the Philippine eagle"],["1987","Philippine Eagle Foundation established in Davao City"],["1992","Pag-asa, the first Philippine eagle bred in captivity, hatches"],["1995","Philippine eagle declared the national bird"]],
s2:[["Eagles of the world","The heaviest eagles include Steller’s sea eagle of Russia and the harpy eagle of South American rainforests, which can weigh around 9 kg. The golden eagle hunts across the Northern Hemisphere, and the bald eagle, which eats mostly fish, is the national bird of the United States."],["Eyes like binoculars","An eagle’s eyes are large for its head and packed with light-sensitive cells. It can see prey such as a rabbit from more than a kilometer away. Eagles also see more colors than people, including ultraviolet light. A clear inner eyelid protects the eye while flying."],["Nests","Eagles build huge nests of sticks, called eyries, high in trees or on cliffs. Many pairs use the same nest for years, adding more sticks each season. Some bald eagle nests have grown wider than 2.5 meters. Philippine eagles nest in very tall forest trees."],["Threats","The Philippine eagle is critically endangered. Its forests are cut for logging, farms and mining, and some eagles are shot or trapped. Each pair needs a large territory of forest, so the loss of trees leaves little room. Killing a Philippine eagle is punishable by law with prison and a heavy fine."],["Pag-asa","In 1992 the Philippine Eagle Foundation in Davao City hatched the first Philippine eagle bred in captivity. Named Pag-asa, which means hope, it lived for 27 years. The foundation breeds eagles, returns some to the wild and works with Indigenous communities who guard eagle nests in their forests."],["Eagles as symbols","Eagles have stood for strength and freedom since ancient times. Roman legions carried eagle standards, and eagles appear on the flags of Mexico, Egypt and Albania. In 1782 the bald eagle was placed on the Great Seal of the United States. The Philippine eagle appears on the 1,000-peso polymer banknote first issued in 2022 and on many stamps."]],
sp:["Our national bird","In 1995 President Fidel V. Ramos signed Proclamation No. 615, making the Philippine eagle the national bird in place of the maya. Its name was changed from monkey-eating eagle in 1978 to show that it belongs to the nation. Every June the country celebrates Philippine Eagle Week to raise awareness of the bird and its forests."],
g:[["Bird of prey","A bird that hunts other animals with its feet and beak"],["Talon","A sharp, curved claw"],["Eyrie","An eagle’s nest"],["Colugo","A gliding mammal, also called a flying lemur"]],
f:["A Philippine eagle pair raises just one chick every two years.","Eagles can see ultraviolet light.","Pag-asa means hope; it was the first Philippine eagle bred in captivity."],
fun:"The Philippine eagle’s wingspan is about 2 meters.",
tl:"Ang agila ay malaking ibong mandaragit na may matatalas na kuko at mata. Ang Philippine eagle o haribon ang pambansang ibon ng Pilipinas, ngunit mga 400 pares na lamang ang natitira sa gubat."});

;
/* data/animals3.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 3: owls, parrots, the whale shark and jellyfish.
ADDART({id:"owl",t:"Owls",i:"🦉",w:"Barn owl",r:["birds","eagle","bat"],
p:[["Hunters of the night","Most owls hunt at night, using sharp hearing and large eyes to find prey in the dark."],["Silent wings","Soft, fringed feathers let owls fly almost without a sound."],["Found worldwide","About 250 species of owl live on every continent except Antarctica."]],
l:"Owls are birds of prey built for hunting in the dark. With forward-facing eyes, super-sensitive hearing and wings that make almost no noise, they can catch mice, insects and other small animals at night. Their round faces and big eyes have made them symbols of wisdom in many cultures.",
s:[["Eyes","An owl’s eyes are so large that they fill much of its skull. They are shaped like tubes rather than balls, so they cannot move in their sockets. To look around, an owl turns its head, up to about 270 degrees, thanks to a flexible neck with 14 bones, twice as many as ours."],["Ears","Owls hear extremely well. In many species, one ear opening is higher than the other, so a sound reaches each ear at a slightly different moment. This lets an owl pinpoint its prey exactly. A barn owl can catch a mouse in total darkness by sound alone."],["Silent flight","The front edges of an owl’s flight feathers have soft, comb-like fringes, and the feather surfaces are velvety. These break up the rush of air that makes noise. Prey cannot hear the owl coming, and the owl can hear the prey as it swoops."],["Pellets","Owls usually swallow small prey whole. Later they cough up the bones, fur and feathers they cannot digest as a neat bundle called a pellet. Scientists and students pull pellets apart to discover exactly what an owl has eaten."]],
k:[["Species","About 250"],["Largest","Eurasian eagle-owl and Blakiston’s fish owl, wingspan up to about 1.9 m"],["Smallest","Elf owl, about 40 g"],["Neck turn","Up to about 270 degrees"],["Neck bones","14"]],
d:[["c. 60 million years ago","Earliest known owl fossils"],["c. 500 BCE","Coins of Athens show an owl, symbol of the goddess Athena"],["1758","Linnaeus names the snowy owl"],["1971","Roger Payne shows that barn owls can find prey by sound alone"],["1997","The Harry Potter books make the snowy owl famous"]],
s2:[["Faces like dishes","Many owls have a round facial disc of stiff feathers around their eyes. It works like a satellite dish, gathering sounds and directing them to the ears. The barn owl’s heart-shaped face is one of the best sound collectors of any bird."],["Some remarkable owls","The Blakiston’s fish owl of eastern Russia and Japan and the Eurasian eagle-owl are among the largest owls, with wingspans of up to about 1.9 meters. The tiny elf owl of the American deserts nests in cactus holes. The snowy owl hunts across the Arctic tundra, and the burrowing owl of the Americas lives underground in old animal burrows."],["What owls eat","Small owls eat insects, spiders and worms. Medium owls catch mice, rats, frogs and small birds. Large eagle-owls can take rabbits and even young deer, and fish owls snatch fish from rivers. By eating rats and mice, owls help farmers protect their crops."],["Day and night","Not all owls hunt only at night. Snowy owls of the Arctic hunt in the 24-hour daylight of summer, and burrowing owls of the Americas are active by day and live in holes in the ground. Most owls rest by day, hidden by camouflage feathers in trees."],["Owls in stories","In ancient Greece the owl was linked with Athena, goddess of wisdom. In other cultures owls have been seen as bad omens, and in many places their night calls were once believed to warn of death or illness. Today owls appear in many children’s books."],["Threats","Owls are harmed by the loss of old trees and forests, by pesticides and rat poisons that poison the owls that eat rats, and by collisions with cars. Some people put up nest boxes for barn owls on farms, providing natural pest control without chemicals."]],
sp:["Feathers that teach engineers","Engineers study owl feathers to make machines quieter. Fringed edges inspired by owl wings have been tested on wind turbine blades, computer fans and aircraft parts to reduce noise. Copying designs from nature in this way is called biomimicry."],
g:[["Facial disc","A round ring of feathers that gathers sound"],["Pellet","A bundle of bones and fur an owl coughs up"],["Camouflage","Colors that help an animal blend in"],["Biomimicry","Copying designs from nature"]],
f:["An owl can turn its head up to about 270 degrees.","Owls cannot move their eyes in their sockets.","Barn owls can catch mice in total darkness."],
fun:"An owl’s neck has 14 bones, twice as many as a human neck.",
tl:"Ang kuwago ay ibong mandaragit na nangangaso sa gabi gamit ang malalaking mata at matalas na pandinig. Halos walang tunog ang paglipad nito, at kaya nitong ipihit ang ulo nang hanggang 270 digri."});

ADDART({id:"parrot",t:"Parrots",i:"🦜",w:"Parrot",r:["birds","palawan","phbio"],cons:["CR","Philippine cockatoo"],
p:[["Clever and colorful","Parrots are bright, noisy and among the most intelligent of all birds."],["Strong beaks, clever feet","They have curved beaks for cracking nuts and feet with two toes pointing forward and two back."],["Talking birds","Many parrots can copy human words and other sounds."]],
l:"Parrots are colorful, intelligent birds found mostly in warm tropical regions. There are about 400 species, including macaws, cockatoos, lovebirds and lorikeets. Their curved beaks, grasping feet and remarkable ability to copy sounds make them popular, but many are now threatened by habitat loss and the pet trade.",
s:[["Beaks and feet","A parrot’s curved beak is strong enough to crack hard nuts and seeds. Its feet have two toes facing forward and two facing back, so it can grip branches and hold food like a hand. Parrots often use their beaks as a third foot when climbing."],["Intelligence","Parrots have large brains for their size. Some can solve puzzles, use tools and understand simple ideas such as color, shape and number. In the wild they live in noisy flocks, learning calls from one another and keeping in touch as they fly between feeding places."],["Talking","Parrots copy sounds using a voice organ called the syrinx and a thick, flexible tongue. African grey parrots are especially good mimics. In the wild, parrots copy the calls of their flock, and pet parrots often copy the words, laughter and phone ringtones they hear at home."],["Long lives","Many large parrots live for a long time. Macaws and cockatoos can live 50 years or more, and some have lived beyond 80 in captivity. Because they live so long and need company and attention, keeping a parrot as a pet is a big responsibility."]],
k:[["Species","About 400"],["Largest","Hyacinth macaw, about 1 m long"],["Heaviest","Kākāpō, up to about 4 kg"],["Smallest","Buff-faced pygmy parrot, about 8 cm"],["Lifespan","Large parrots 50 years or more"]],
d:[["1977","Irene Pepperberg begins her studies with Alex the African grey parrot"],["1995","Only 51 kākāpō are left in New Zealand"],["2000","Spix’s macaw becomes extinct in the wild"],["2007","Alex the parrot dies, knowing more than 100 words"],["2024","Kākāpō numbers pass 240 thanks to careful protection"]],
s2:[["Kinds of parrot","Parrots include the big, long-tailed macaws of South America, crested cockatoos of Australia and Southeast Asia, small lovebirds of Africa, nectar-eating lorikeets and budgerigars, the small green-and-yellow parakeets of Australia that are popular pets around the world."],["Alex the African grey","Scientist Irene Pepperberg worked with an African grey parrot named Alex for 30 years. Alex learned more than 100 words and could name colors, shapes and materials and count small numbers of objects. His last words to her each evening were said to be You be good. I love you."],["The kākāpō","The kākāpō of New Zealand is a flightless, night-active parrot that is the heaviest in the world. It smells sweet and musty, and males boom loudly to attract mates. By 1995 only 51 were known. A careful recovery program, with every bird named and tracked, has more than quadrupled their number."],["Parrots of many lands","Parrots live mostly in warm regions of Central and South America, Africa, South and Southeast Asia, Australia and New Zealand. Australia has the most cockatoos, the Amazon basin has many macaws, and Africa is home to the grey parrot. Some, such as the hanging parrots of Asia, sleep upside down like bats."],["The katala","Many parrots are endangered because they are trapped for the pet trade and lose their forests. The Philippine cockatoo fell to fewer than 1,000 birds, and Spix’s macaw of Brazil became extinct in the wild in 2000. Protection programs, sometimes employing former poachers as nest guards, are helping several species slowly recover."],["The pet trade","Millions of wild parrots have been captured to sell as pets, and many die on the way. Today about one in four parrot species is threatened. International law controls the trade of most parrots, and buying captive-bred birds from responsible breeders helps protect wild ones."]],
sp:["Spix’s macaw","Spix’s macaw, a blue parrot from northeastern Brazil, became extinct in the wild in 2000 because of trapping and habitat loss. Birds kept in captivity were bred in programs in Germany, Belgium and Brazil, and in 2022 the first group was released back into its native dry forest. Its story shows both how quickly a species can vanish and how it can be brought back."],
g:[["Syrinx","The voice organ of a bird"],["Mimic","An animal that copies sounds or looks"],["Flock","A group of birds that live and travel together"],["Pet trade","Buying and selling animals as pets"]],
f:["A parrot has two toes facing forward and two facing back.","The kākāpō is the world’s heaviest parrot and cannot fly.","Some large parrots live for more than 80 years."],
fun:"Hanging parrots of the Philippines sleep upside down, like bats.",
tl:"Ang loro ay makulay at matalinong ibon na kayang gayahin ang mga salita at tunog. Ang Philippine cockatoo o katala ay lubhang nanganganib at matatagpuan pangunahin sa Palawan."});

ADDART({id:"wshark",t:"The Whale Shark",i:"🦈",w:"Whale shark",r:["shark","whale","reef"],cons:["EN","Whale shark"],
p:[["The biggest fish","The whale shark is the largest fish in the world, growing longer than a bus."],["A gentle giant","Despite its size, it eats tiny plankton and small fish and is harmless to people."],["Where to see them","Divers can swim with whale sharks at gathering sites in Australia, Mexico, the Maldives, the Philippines and elsewhere."]],
l:"The whale shark is the largest fish in the sea, a slow-moving giant that feeds on some of the smallest creatures in the ocean. It is a shark, not a whale, and its spotted back makes every individual unique. It roams warm seas around the world, gathering at a few places where food is plentiful.",
s:[["Shark, not whale","A whale shark breathes through gills, like all fish, and does not need to come up for air. It is called a whale shark because of its enormous size and because it feeds by filtering water, like some whales. It is a cartilaginous fish, with a skeleton of flexible cartilage instead of bone."],["Size","Whale sharks commonly grow to about 12 meters, and the largest may reach around 18 meters. Their mouths can be about 1.5 meters wide. Even so, their throats are narrow, and they eat only small food. They swim slowly, at about 5 kilometers per hour."],["Filter feeding","A whale shark gulps or sucks in water full of plankton, fish eggs and small fish, then pushes it out through its gills. Filter pads catch the food. It has about 3,000 tiny teeth, but it does not use them to eat. A whale shark may filter thousands of liters of water an hour."],["Spots and stripes","The whale shark’s back is gray-blue with white spots and pale stripes. The pattern behind each gill area is different on every animal. Scientists photograph these spots and use computer programs, first designed to map stars, to identify individual sharks across the oceans."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Rhincodon typus"],["Length","Commonly about 12 m, up to about 18 m"],["Weight","Up to about 20 metric tons or more"],["Diet","Plankton, fish eggs and small fish"],["Status","Endangered since 2016"]],
d:[["1828","Whale shark first described by scientists, from a fish caught near Cape Town"],["2003","Whale sharks are added to CITES trade controls"],["2016","The whale shark is listed as Endangered by the IUCN"],["2012","International Whale Shark Day first held on 30 August"],["2016","Whale shark listed as Endangered on the IUCN Red List"]],
s2:[["Where they live","Whale sharks live in warm tropical seas around the world. They travel thousands of kilometers, gathering at places and seasons where food is plentiful, such as plankton blooms or the spawning of fish and corals. Gathering sites include Ningaloo Reef in Western Australia, the Yucatán coast of Mexico, the Maldives, Djibouti, Belize and the Philippines."],["Babies","Whale sharks give birth to live young. In 1995 a female caught off Taiwan was carrying about 300 babies at different stages, each about 40 to 60 centimeters long. Very few young whale sharks are ever seen, so scientists still know little about where they grow up."],["Swimming with whale sharks","At gathering sites such as Ningaloo Reef in Australia, Isla Holbox in Mexico and Donsol in the Philippines, visitors swim with whale sharks under strict rules: small groups, no touching, no flash cameras and keeping several meters away. In many places, fishers who once hunted the sharks now work as guides, earning more from tourism than they ever did from fishing."],["Where sharks are fed","In a few places, such as Oslob in the Philippines, whale sharks are fed by boatmen so tourists can see them every day. Scientists worry that feeding changes their natural behavior and keeps them in shallow water near boats, where they can be hit by propellers. Guidelines elsewhere ask tourists not to feed or touch the sharks and to keep their distance."],["Threats","Whale sharks were once hunted for their meat and fins, and they can be killed by boat strikes or trapped in fishing nets. Because they grow slowly and have few young, their numbers recover very slowly. Worldwide, numbers are thought to have fallen by about half over the last 75 years."],["Protection","Many countries ban catching whale sharks, and since 2003 international trade in them has been controlled under the CITES agreement. Tourism that follows good rules, as at Ningaloo Reef in Australia, gives coastal towns a reason to keep whale sharks alive."]],
sp:["Swimming with a giant","Seeing a whale shark up close is unforgettable: a spotted shape the size of a bus gliding past, its huge mouth open wide. Good guides keep groups small and make sure swimmers stay at least 3 to 4 meters away and never touch the shark, which can harm its skin and stress the animal."],
g:[["Plankton","Tiny living things that drift in water"],["Filter feeder","An animal that strains food from water"],["Cartilage","Strong, bendy tissue that forms a shark’s skeleton"],["Gills","Organs fish use to take oxygen from water"]],
f:["The whale shark is the largest fish in the world.","Every whale shark has a unique pattern of spots.","A whale shark has about 3,000 tiny teeth it does not use to eat."],
fun:"Scientists identify whale sharks using software first made to map star patterns.",
tl:"Ang butanding o whale shark ang pinakamalaking isda sa mundo, ngunit maliliit na plankton lamang ang kinakain nito. Makikita ito sa Donsol, Sorsogon, at protektado ito ng batas sa Pilipinas mula 1998."});

ADDART({id:"jelly",t:"Jellyfish",i:"🪼",w:"Jellyfish",r:["octopus","reef","pacific"],
p:[["Not really fish","Jellyfish are not fish at all. They have no brain, heart, blood or bones."],["Mostly water","A jellyfish’s body is about 95 percent water."],["Stingers","Their tentacles carry tiny stinging cells that trap prey and defend against enemies."]],
l:"Jellyfish are soft, drifting sea animals that have lived in the oceans for more than 500 million years, long before dinosaurs or even fish. They have no brain, heart or bones, yet they catch food with stinging tentacles. Some glow in the dark, and a few have stings that can be deadly.",
s:[["A simple body","A jellyfish has a bell-shaped body called a medusa, with a mouth underneath and tentacles hanging down. It swims by squeezing its bell to push out water. A simple net of nerves, instead of a brain, lets it sense light, touch and which way is up."],["Stinging cells","Jellyfish belong to a group called cnidarians, along with corals and sea anemones. Their tentacles hold thousands of stinging cells called nematocysts. When touched, each fires a tiny harpoon that injects venom, stunning small fish and plankton."],["Life cycle","Most jellyfish begin life as tiny larvae that settle on the seabed and grow into polyps, which look like small anemones. The polyps then bud off baby jellyfish that swim away. Adult jellyfish produce eggs and sperm, starting the cycle again."],["Glowing jellies","Many jellyfish produce their own light, called bioluminescence, especially in the deep sea. The crystal jelly of the Pacific contains a green fluorescent protein. Scientists now use this protein to make cells glow so they can watch how genes and diseases work."]],
k:[["Age of the group","More than 500 million years"],["Water content","About 95 percent"],["Largest","Lion’s mane jellyfish, tentacles over 30 m"],["Brain","None"],["True jellyfish species","About 200"]],
d:[["c. 500 million years ago","Jellyfish fossils from the Cambrian period"],["1870","A lion’s mane jellyfish with 36-meter tentacles washes ashore in Massachusetts"],["1962","Osamu Shimomura isolates green fluorescent protein from a jellyfish"],["1988","The immortal jellyfish’s ability to grow young again is discovered"],["2008","Nobel Prize in Chemistry awarded for green fluorescent protein"]],
s2:[["Box jellyfish","Box jellyfish have cube-shaped bells and some of the most dangerous venom of any animal. Unlike most jellyfish, they swim actively and have eyes. They are most common in northern Australia and the tropical Indo-Pacific, where some beaches use nets or warning signs during jellyfish season."],["Stings and first aid","Most jellyfish stings cause pain and redness that fade in a few hours. For box jellyfish stings, first aiders pour plenty of vinegar on the area to stop more stinging cells firing and get medical help at once. Rubbing the sting or using fresh water can make it worse."],["The immortal jellyfish","A tiny jellyfish called Turritopsis dohrnii can turn back into a polyp when it is injured or old, then grow into a new adult. In theory it could repeat this many times. It is sometimes called the immortal jellyfish, though most are eaten or die of disease."],["Jellyfish blooms","Sometimes huge numbers of jellyfish appear together, called a bloom. Blooms can clog fishing nets and block the water pipes of power stations. Overfishing, warmer seas and pollution may help jellyfish by removing their predators and competitors."],["Who eats jellyfish?","Sea turtles, especially leatherbacks, eat large amounts of jellyfish, as do some fish and seabirds. Turtles often mistake floating plastic bags for jellyfish and swallow them. In China, Japan and other parts of Asia, some kinds of jellyfish are dried and eaten by people."],["Jellyfish Lake","In Palau, in the western Pacific, a saltwater lake is home to millions of golden jellyfish that follow the Sun across the lake each day. With no predators, their stings became very weak, and snorkelers can swim among them. Their numbers fell sharply during a drought in 2016 and later recovered."]],
sp:["A glowing Nobel Prize","In 1962 Japanese scientist Osamu Shimomura studied a glowing jellyfish from the coast of Washington State and found a protein that shines green under blue light. Later scientists learned to attach it to other genes as a marker. In 2008 Shimomura, Martin Chalfie and Roger Tsien shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for this work."],
g:[["Medusa","The bell-shaped, swimming stage of a jellyfish"],["Nematocyst","A stinging cell"],["Polyp","The small, fixed stage of a jellyfish"],["Bioluminescence","Light made by a living thing"]],
f:["Jellyfish have lived in the oceans for more than 500 million years.","A jellyfish has no brain, heart or bones.","One tiny jellyfish can turn back into a young polyp."],
fun:"Jellyfish were drifting in the oceans long before the first dinosaurs.",
tl:"Ang dikya ay malambot na hayop-dagat na walang utak, puso o buto, at halos tubig ang katawan. May mga selulang nakatutusok ang galamay nito, at mapanganib ang ilang uri gaya ng box jellyfish."});

;
/* data/animals4.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 4: sea turtles, snakes, crocodiles and dinosaurs.
ADDART({id:"seaturtle",t:"Sea Turtles",i:"🐢",w:"Green sea turtle",r:["reef","jelly","sulu"],cons:["EN","Green sea turtle"],
p:[["Ancient mariners","Sea turtles have swum the oceans for more than 100 million years."],["Coming home to nest","Females crawl onto the same beaches where they hatched to lay their eggs in the sand."],["Turtles worldwide","Sea turtles swim in every ocean except the icy polar seas."]],
l:"Sea turtles are reptiles that spend almost their whole lives at sea, coming ashore only to lay eggs. Their streamlined shells and flipper-like legs make them graceful swimmers, and some travel thousands of kilometers between feeding and nesting grounds. All seven species face threats, and most are endangered.",
s:[["Seven species","The seven sea turtles are the green, hawksbill, leatherback, loggerhead, olive ridley, Kemp’s ridley and flatback. Most live across the world’s tropical and warm seas, but Kemp’s ridley is found mainly in the Gulf of Mexico and the flatback only around Australia. The green turtle is named for the color of its fat."],["Built for the sea","Unlike land turtles, sea turtles cannot pull their heads and legs into their shells. Their front legs have become long flippers for swimming, and their shells are flat and smooth. They breathe air but can stay underwater for hours while resting."],["Nesting","At night, a female crawls up a beach, digs a hole with her back flippers and lays about 100 eggs the size of ping-pong balls. She covers them with sand and returns to the sea. She may nest several times in one season, then not again for two to four years."],["Hatchlings","After about two months, the hatchlings dig out together, usually at night, and scramble toward the brightest horizon, normally the sea. Crabs, birds and fish eat many of them. Only about one in 1,000 hatchlings is thought to survive to become an adult."]],
k:[["Species","7"],["Largest","Leatherback, up to about 2 m and 700 kg"],["Eggs per nest","About 100"],["Incubation","About 60 days"],["Lifespan","Possibly 50–100 years"]],
d:[["c. 110 million years ago","Early sea turtles swim in Cretaceous seas"],["1975","CITES treaty begins controlling trade in sea turtle products"],["1996","Philippines and Malaysia create the Turtle Islands Heritage Protected Area"],["1999","Turtle Islands Wildlife Sanctuary established in Tawi-Tawi"],["2001","Countries around the Indian Ocean agree to protect sea turtles"]],
s2:[["Sand decides","The temperature of the sand decides whether a hatchling becomes male or female. Warmer nests produce mostly females, and cooler nests mostly males. As the climate warms, some beaches now produce almost all females, which worries scientists."],["Finding home","Female sea turtles return to nest on or near the beach where they hatched, sometimes after 20 or 30 years. They seem to remember the beach’s magnetic signature, a pattern in Earth’s magnetic field. Some turtles cross entire oceans between feeding and nesting areas."],["The leatherback","The leatherback is the largest turtle and one of the largest reptiles alive. Instead of a hard shell, it has a leathery skin over bony plates. It dives more than 1,000 meters deep and lives mainly on jellyfish, keeping their numbers in check."],["The hawksbill","The hawksbill has a narrow, pointed beak for reaching sponges in coral reefs. Its beautiful shell was once carved into combs, jewelry and ornaments called tortoiseshell. Hunting for this trade made the hawksbill critically endangered; international trade is now banned."],["Threats","Sea turtles drown in fishing nets, swallow plastic bags they mistake for jellyfish, and lose nesting beaches to buildings and bright lights that confuse hatchlings. In the past, their eggs and meat were widely collected. Today many countries ban catching, selling or eating sea turtles and their eggs."],["Helping turtles","Volunteers and communities guard nests, move eggs at risk to safe hatcheries and release hatchlings. Turtle excluder devices let turtles escape from shrimp nets. Turning off beachfront lights during nesting season and cleaning beaches of plastic also help."]],
sp:["The Turtle Islands","Some of the most important sea turtle nesting beaches are protected across borders. In 1996 the Philippines and Malaysia joined their Turtle Islands into the first protected area shared by two countries for sea turtles. At Ostional in Costa Rica and in Odisha, India, hundreds of thousands of olive ridleys come ashore together in mass nestings called arribadas."],
g:[["Reptile","A cold-blooded animal with scales that breathes air"],["Hatchling","A newly hatched young animal"],["Carapace","The top part of a turtle’s shell"],["Hatchery","A protected place where eggs are kept safe until they hatch"]],
f:["The temperature of the sand decides whether a sea turtle is male or female.","Only about one in 1,000 hatchlings may survive to adulthood.","Leatherbacks can dive more than 1,000 meters deep."],
fun:"Warm sand produces mostly female sea turtles; cooler sand, mostly males.",
tl:"Ang pawikan ay reptilya sa dagat na umaahon lamang upang mangitlog sa buhangin. Lima sa pitong uri nito ang nasa Pilipinas, at ipinagbabawal ng batas ang paghuli at pagkain sa mga ito at sa kanilang itlog."});

ADDART({id:"snake",t:"Snakes",i:"🐍",w:"Snake",r:["frog","croc","phbio"],
p:[["Legless reptiles","Snakes are reptiles with long bodies, no legs and no eyelids."],["Swallowing whole","They swallow their prey whole, stretching their jaws around animals bigger than their heads."],["Mostly harmless","Of about 4,000 species, only a minority have venom dangerous to people."]],
l:"Snakes are legless reptiles found on every continent except Antarctica. They smell with their tongues, swallow prey whole and move by bending their long bodies. Some squeeze their prey, some use venom, and many are harmless. Snakes help people by controlling rats and mice, though a few can be very dangerous.",
s:[["How snakes move","Snakes move in several ways. Most push against rocks and plants in S-shaped waves. Heavy snakes such as pythons creep forward in a straight line using their belly scales. Sidewinders of the desert loop sideways over loose sand, and some tree snakes even glide between branches."],["Smelling with the tongue","A snake flicks its forked tongue to collect scent particles from the air and ground. It then presses the tongue into a special organ in the roof of its mouth that analyzes the smells. The two tips help it tell which direction a scent is coming from."],["Eating","Snakes eat only animals, from insects and eggs to rats, birds and even deer. Their lower jaw has two halves joined by stretchy tissue, and their skin stretches too, so they can swallow very large meals. A big python may not need to eat again for months."],["Shedding skin","As snakes grow, they shed their outer skin in one piece, often several times a year. Before shedding, their eyes turn cloudy because the clear scale covering each eye also comes off. Snakes have no eyelids, so they sleep with their eyes open."]],
k:[["Species","About 4,000"],["Longest","Reticulated python, over 6 m"],["Heaviest","Green anaconda, up to about 250 kg"],["Longest venomous","King cobra, up to about 5.5 m"],["Smallest","Barbados threadsnake, about 10 cm"]],
d:[["c. 100 million years ago","Early snakes with tiny back legs live in the Cretaceous"],["1801","The reticulated python is described by scientists"],["1895","Albert Calmette develops one of the first antivenoms"],["2009","Fossils of Titanoboa, a 13-meter snake, are announced"],["2017","World Health Organization lists snakebite as a neglected tropical disease"]],
s2:[["Constrictors","Pythons, boas and anacondas are constrictors. They grab prey with their teeth, wrap their coils around it and squeeze each time it breathes out, stopping its blood flow. The reticulated python of Southeast Asia is the longest snake in the world."],["Venomous snakes","Venomous snakes inject venom through hollow or grooved fangs. Cobras, kraits and sea snakes have venom that affects the nerves, while vipers have venom that damages blood and tissue. Spitting cobras of Africa and Asia can spray venom into the eyes of an attacker."],["Snakes around the world","About 4,000 kinds of snake live on every continent except Antarctica. Ireland, Iceland, New Zealand and Greenland have no native land snakes. Australia is famous for venomous snakes such as the inland taipan, while South America has the green anaconda and Asia the king cobra, the longest venomous snake."],["Snakebite","Snakebites kill tens of thousands of people each year, mostly in rural parts of Asia and Africa. If someone is bitten, keep them calm and still, remove tight rings or watches and get them to a hospital quickly. Cutting the bite, sucking out venom or using a tight tourniquet does more harm than good."],["Giant snakes of the past","About 60 million years ago, a snake called Titanoboa lived in the hot swamps of what is now Colombia. It was about 13 meters long and may have weighed more than a metric ton. Its fossils show that the climate there was then even hotter than today’s tropics."],["Snakes and people","Snakes appear in myths around the world, from the serpent in the Garden of Eden to the naga of South and Southeast Asia and Quetzalcoatl, the feathered serpent of the Aztecs. In many cultures, a serpent was said to swallow the Moon during eclipses. In real life, snakes help farmers by eating rats that damage crops."]],
sp:["The Rainbow Serpent","In the stories of many Aboriginal Australian peoples, the Rainbow Serpent is a great snake that shaped the land in the Dreaming, carving rivers and waterholes as it moved and bringing rain. It is one of the oldest continuing religious beliefs in the world, appearing in rock paintings thousands of years old. Serpents also feature in the myths of many other peoples, from the Aztecs’ feathered Quetzalcoatl to the sea serpent bakunawa of the Philippines."],
g:[["Venom","A poison injected by a bite or sting"],["Fang","A long, sharp tooth"],["Constrictor","A snake that kills by squeezing"],["Antivenom","A medicine that blocks the effects of venom"]],
f:["Snakes smell with their tongues.","Snakes have no eyelids and sleep with their eyes open.","The reticulated python is the world’s longest snake."],
fun:"Snakes smell with their tongues, flicking them to taste the air.",
tl:"Ang ahas ay reptilyang walang paa na umaamoy gamit ang dila at lumulunok ng buong biktima. Kaunti lamang sa mga 4,000 uri nito ang may kamandag na mapanganib sa tao; sa Pilipinas ay may sawa at Philippine cobra."});

ADDART({id:"croc",t:"Crocodiles",i:"🐊",w:"Saltwater crocodile",r:["snake","mindanao","dino"],cons:["CR","Philippine crocodile"],
p:[["Ancient survivors","Crocodiles and their relatives have survived for more than 200 million years, outlasting the dinosaurs."],["Powerful hunters","They lie in wait in the water, then burst out to seize prey with jaws that have the strongest bite ever measured."],["Across the tropics","Crocodilians live in warm rivers, swamps and coasts of Africa, Asia, Australia and the Americas."]],
l:"Crocodiles are large, armored reptiles that hunt in rivers, swamps and coastal waters across the tropics. With their relatives the alligators, caimans and gharials, they form a group called crocodilians, whose ancestors lived alongside the dinosaurs. They include the world’s largest reptile, the saltwater crocodile, and some of its rarest.",
s:[["Crocodilians","There are about 26 species of crocodilian. True crocodiles live in Africa, Asia, Australia and the Americas. Alligators live in the United States and China, caimans in Central and South America, and the gharial, with its long, thin snout for catching fish, in India and Nepal."],["Built to ambush","A crocodile’s eyes, ears and nostrils sit on top of its head, so it can watch and breathe while almost completely hidden underwater. A flap at the back of its throat lets it open its mouth underwater without drowning. It waits patiently, then lunges at animals that come to drink."],["The bite","Saltwater crocodiles have the strongest bite measured for any living animal, more than 16,000 newtons. Yet the muscles that open their jaws are weak, so a person can hold a crocodile’s mouth shut with their hands or tape. Crocodiles cannot chew; they tear and swallow large chunks."],["Caring parents","Unlike most reptiles, crocodiles care for their young. The mother guards her nest of eggs for months. When the babies call from inside the eggs, she digs them out and gently carries them to the water in her mouth, then protects them for weeks."]],
k:[["Species","About 26 crocodilians"],["Largest","Saltwater crocodile, males up to about 6 m"],["Bite force","Over 16,000 newtons"],["Lifespan","Often 50–70 years"],["Rarest","Gharial, Chinese alligator and others, critically endangered"]],
d:[["c. 80 million years ago","Deinosuchus, a 10-meter crocodile relative, hunts dinosaurs"],["1987","The American alligator is declared recovered"],["2011","Lolong is captured in Bunawan, Agusan del Sur"],["2012","Guinness World Records names Lolong the largest crocodile in captivity"],["2013","Lolong dies"]],
s2:[["The saltwater crocodile","The saltwater crocodile is the largest living reptile. Big males can grow to about 6 meters and weigh more than 1,000 kg. It lives in rivers, mangroves and coasts from India to northern Australia and can swim far out to sea, which is how it reached many distant islands."],["Lolong","In 2011 villagers in Bunawan, Agusan del Sur, captured a saltwater crocodile they named Lolong. It measured 6.17 meters, and Guinness World Records named it the largest crocodile ever held in captivity. Lolong lived in an eco-park in Bunawan until it died in 2013."],["Rare crocodilians","Several crocodilians are critically endangered. The gharial of India and Nepal, with its long, thin snout, has lost most of its rivers. The Chinese alligator, the Orinoco crocodile of Venezuela and Colombia, and the Philippine crocodile, a small freshwater species usually under 3 meters long, each survive in the wild in very small numbers."],["Saving crocodilians","Crocodilians were long feared and killed, and their wetlands drained. The American alligator, hunted almost to extinction, was protected in 1967 and declared recovered in 1987. Today breeding centers in India, Nepal, China and the Philippines raise young gharials, alligators and crocodiles and release them into protected rivers and lakes."],["Survivors","Crocodilians survived the asteroid impact that wiped out the dinosaurs 66 million years ago. They can go months without food, slowing their bodies down when times are hard. Their closest living relatives are not lizards but birds; both are archosaurs, like the dinosaurs."],["Staying safe","Crocodile attacks on people happen where people fish, wash or swim in crocodile waters. Local warning signs should always be obeyed. Never swim at dusk or night in rivers, estuaries or mangroves where crocodiles live, and stay several meters back from the water’s edge."]],
sp:["Lolong of Bunawan","Lolong took about three weeks and dozens of people to capture in 2011, after reports of attacks on people and farm animals in the area. The crocodile became a national celebrity and tourist attraction. After it died in 2013, its skeleton and preserved skin were given to the National Museum of the Philippines."],
g:[["Crocodilian","A group of reptiles that includes crocodiles and alligators"],["Ambush predator","A hunter that waits hidden and attacks by surprise"],["Archosaur","A group that includes crocodiles, dinosaurs and birds"],["Estuary","Where a river meets the sea"]],
f:["Crocodiles carry their babies to the water in their mouths.","The saltwater crocodile has the strongest bite ever measured.","Crocodiles are more closely related to birds than to lizards."],
fun:"Crocodiles are more closely related to birds than to lizards.",
tl:"Ang buwaya ay malaking reptilya na nangangaso sa ilog, latian at baybayin. Dalawang uri ang nasa Pilipinas: ang malaking saltwater crocodile, gaya ni Lolong, at ang lubhang nanganganib na Philippine crocodile."});

ADDART({id:"dino",t:"Dinosaurs",i:"🦕",w:"Dinosaur",r:["evo","birds","tect"],
p:[["Rulers of the Mesozoic","Dinosaurs dominated life on land for about 165 million years, from about 230 to 66 million years ago."],["Giants and small fry","They ranged from chicken-sized hunters to long-necked giants longer than a basketball court."],["Still with us","Birds are living dinosaurs, descended from small feathered meat eaters."]],
l:"Dinosaurs were a group of reptiles that ruled the land for about 165 million years during the Mesozoic Era. They included the largest animals ever to walk the Earth as well as small, fast, feathered hunters. Most died out 66 million years ago after an asteroid struck Earth, but one branch survives today as the birds.",
s:[["The age of dinosaurs","The Mesozoic Era is divided into three periods. The first dinosaurs appeared in the Triassic, about 230 million years ago. Giant long-necked sauropods spread in the Jurassic, and the Cretaceous saw Tyrannosaurus, Triceratops and the first flowering plants. Dinosaurs lived on every continent, including Antarctica."],["Two great groups","Scientists divide dinosaurs by their hip bones. Saurischians, or lizard-hipped dinosaurs, include the long-necked sauropods and the two-legged theropods, the group that gave rise to birds. Ornithischians, or bird-hipped dinosaurs, include plant eaters such as Stegosaurus, Triceratops and duck-billed hadrosaurs."],["Giants","Sauropods such as Argentinosaurus and Patagotitan may have reached more than 30 meters long and weighed over 50 metric tons, the largest land animals ever. They had long necks to reach leaves and small heads, and they swallowed plants without chewing much."],["The end of the dinosaurs","About 66 million years ago, an asteroid about 10 kilometers wide hit what is now Mexico, making the Chicxulub crater. Dust and soot blocked sunlight, plants died and temperatures crashed. About three quarters of all species died out, including all dinosaurs except birds."]],
k:[["Lived","About 230 to 66 million years ago"],["Largest","Sauropods over 30 m long"],["Tyrannosaurus rex","About 12 m long, about 8 metric tons"],["Named","1842, by Richard Owen"],["Survivors","Birds"]],
d:[["1824","Megalosaurus becomes the first dinosaur to be scientifically named"],["1842","Richard Owen coins the word dinosaur"],["1902","Barnum Brown discovers the first Tyrannosaurus rex skeleton"],["1980","Luis and Walter Alvarez propose that an asteroid killed the dinosaurs"],["1996","Sinosauropteryx, the first feathered dinosaur, is found in China"]],
s2:[["Terrible lizards","The word dinosaur, meaning terrible lizard, was coined in 1842 by the British scientist Richard Owen. Dinosaurs were not true lizards, though. They stood with their legs straight beneath their bodies, like mammals and birds, instead of sprawling out to the sides."],["Tyrannosaurus rex","Tyrannosaurus rex lived in western North America at the very end of the Cretaceous. It was about 12 meters long, with banana-sized teeth and one of the strongest bites of any land animal. Its arms were short but muscular. A famous skeleton called Sue, found in 1990, is about 90 percent complete."],["Feathered dinosaurs","Since 1996, fossils from China and elsewhere have shown that many dinosaurs had feathers, from simple fuzz to full wings. Velociraptor, a turkey-sized hunter, had feathered arms. Feathers probably first helped with warmth and display before some dinosaurs used them to fly."],["How we know","Everything we know about dinosaurs comes from fossils: bones, teeth, eggs, footprints and even skin impressions and droppings. Paleontologists dig them out carefully and study them in laboratories. Scans and chemical tests can reveal how fast dinosaurs grew and even the colors of some feathers."],["Dinosaurs on every continent","Dinosaur fossils have been found on every continent, including Antarctica, where Cryolophosaurus was discovered in 1991. They are missing from many islands, though, because the islands rose from the sea or formed from volcanoes after the age of dinosaurs, or because their old rocks formed underwater."],["Living dinosaurs","Birds share features with theropod dinosaurs such as hollow bones, wishbones, feathers and the way they lay eggs and brood them. Scientists therefore count birds as living dinosaurs. A chicken or a sparrow is more closely related to Tyrannosaurus rex than T. rex was to Stegosaurus."]],
sp:["Closer in time to us","Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago and Tyrannosaurus about 68 million years ago. That means more time separated Stegosaurus from Tyrannosaurus than separates Tyrannosaurus from us. Dinosaurs lived so long ago that the continents were in different places and the Atlantic Ocean was still narrow."],
g:[["Paleontologist","A scientist who studies fossils"],["Mesozoic","The era of dinosaurs, divided into Triassic, Jurassic and Cretaceous"],["Sauropod","A giant, long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Fossil","Remains or traces of ancient life preserved in rock"]],
f:["Birds are living dinosaurs.","T. rex lived closer in time to us than to Stegosaurus.","Dinosaur fossils have been found on every continent, even Antarctica."],
fun:"Tyrannosaurus rex lived closer in time to us than to Stegosaurus.",
tl:"Ang mga dinosaur ay mga reptilyang namuhay sa lupa mula mga 230 hanggang 66 milyong taon na ang nakararaan. Karamihan ay nalipol nang tumama ang isang asteroid, ngunit ang mga ibon ay itinuturing na buhay na inapo nila."});

;
/* data/animals5.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 5: butterflies, bees, spiders and trees.
ADDART({id:"butterfly",t:"Butterflies and Moths",i:"🦋",w:"Butterfly",r:["insect","bee","eco"],
p:[["Scaly wings","Butterflies and moths belong to a group called Lepidoptera, which means scale wings."],["A total change","They grow through four stages: egg, caterpillar, pupa and adult."],["Flower helpers","Many butterflies and moths pollinate flowers as they sip nectar."]],
l:"Butterflies and moths are insects with wings covered in tiny, colorful scales. They begin life as caterpillars and transform completely into winged adults, one of the most remarkable changes in nature. There are about 18,000 kinds of butterfly and around 160,000 kinds of moth, found on every continent except Antarctica.",
s:[["Four stages","A butterfly starts as an egg laid on a food plant. The egg hatches into a caterpillar, or larva, which eats and grows, shedding its skin several times. It then forms a pupa, called a chrysalis in butterflies. Inside, its body is rebuilt, and an adult with wings finally emerges."],["Wings of scales","Butterfly and moth wings are covered with thousands of overlapping scales, like roof tiles. Some scales hold colored pigments. Others have tiny ridges that bend light to create shimmering blues and greens, the way a soap bubble shows rainbow colors."],["Butterfly or moth?","Most butterflies fly by day, have club-tipped antennae and rest with their wings folded upright. Most moths fly at night, have feathery or thin antennae and rest with their wings spread flat. But there are many exceptions, and scientists see butterflies as one branch of the moth family tree."],["Feeding","Adult butterflies feed through a long tube called a proboscis, which stays coiled under the head when not in use. They sip nectar, fruit juice and minerals from wet mud. They taste with sensors on their feet, which helps females find the right plants for their eggs."]],
k:[["Butterfly species","About 18,000"],["Moth species","About 160,000"],["Largest butterfly","Queen Alexandra’s birdwing, wingspan up to about 28 cm"],["Longest migration","Monarch, up to about 4,000 km"],["Most species","Tropical South America"]],
d:[["1758","Linnaeus groups butterflies and moths as Lepidoptera"],["1906","Queen Alexandra’s birdwing is discovered in Papua New Guinea"],["1975","Scientists find the monarch’s winter forests in the mountains of Mexico"],["2008","Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2023","IUCN lists the migratory monarch butterfly as Vulnerable"]],
s2:[["The monarch migration","Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies in North America fly as far as 4,000 kilometers to the same mountain forests in central Mexico, where they cluster on fir trees for the winter. No single butterfly makes the round trip; it takes several generations to return north."],["Caterpillar defenses","Caterpillars are soft and slow, so they defend themselves in clever ways. Some are green to blend in with leaves, some look like bird droppings, and some have eye spots that look like snake eyes. Others are hairy or poisonous and wear bright colors as a warning."],["Silk moths","The silkworm is the caterpillar of a moth. It spins a cocoon from a single thread of silk up to about 900 meters long. People in China began farming silkworms thousands of years ago, and silk became so valuable that the trade routes to the west were later named the Silk Road."],["Giants and gems","The largest butterfly is Queen Alexandra’s birdwing of Papua New Guinea, with wings up to about 28 centimeters across. Birdwings and swallowtails of Southeast Asia and Australia are among the most colorful. Butterfly houses around the world, from London to Singapore, breed butterflies so visitors can see them up close."],["Moths at night","Many moths find their way at night using the Moon and stars, which may be why they flutter around lamps. Male moths can smell females from kilometers away with their feathery antennae. The atlas moth of South and Southeast Asia is one of the largest moths in the world."],["Why they matter","Butterflies and moths pollinate many plants and are food for birds, bats and other animals. Because they react quickly to changes in their surroundings, scientists use them to measure the health of habitats. Many are declining because of habitat loss, pesticides and climate change."]],
sp:["Planting for butterflies","Anyone can help butterflies by growing the plants they need. Caterpillars need particular food plants, such as citrus for many swallowtails and milkweed for monarchs and their relatives, while adults need flowers rich in nectar, such as lantana and santan. Avoiding pesticides lets caterpillars grow safely."],
g:[["Lepidoptera","The insect group of butterflies and moths"],["Chrysalis","The pupa of a butterfly"],["Proboscis","A long, tube-shaped mouthpart for sipping"],["Metamorphosis","A complete change of body form during growth"]],
f:["Butterflies taste with their feet.","A silkworm’s cocoon can be one thread up to about 900 meters long.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to about 4,000 kilometers."],
fun:"Butterflies taste their food with sensors on their feet.",
tl:"Ang paruparo at gamugamo ay mga insektong may pakpak na natatakpan ng maliliit na kaliskis. Dumaraan sila sa apat na yugto, mula itlog, uod, pupa hanggang sa may pakpak na adulto, at tumutulong sa polinasyon ng mga bulaklak."});

ADDART({id:"bee",t:"Bees",i:"🐝",w:"Western honey bee",r:["insect","butterfly","agri"],
p:[["Busy pollinators","Bees carry pollen from flower to flower, helping plants make fruit and seeds."],["Honey makers","Honeybees turn flower nectar into honey and store it in wax combs."],["Many kinds","There are about 20,000 species of bee, and most live alone rather than in hives."]],
l:"Bees are flying insects that feed on nectar and pollen from flowers. As they travel from bloom to bloom, they pollinate plants, including many crops that people eat. Honeybees live in large, organized colonies and make honey, but most of the world’s 20,000 bee species live alone. Bees are vital to farms and wild ecosystems.",
s:[["Pollination","When a bee visits a flower, pollen sticks to its hairy body. At the next flower, some of it rubs off, fertilizing the plant so it can make seeds and fruit. Many fruits, nuts and vegetables depend on bees and other pollinators, from mangoes and coffee to almonds and squash."],["The honeybee colony","A honeybee colony can have tens of thousands of bees. A single queen lays all the eggs, up to about 2,000 a day. Thousands of female worker bees clean the hive, feed the young, build comb, guard the entrance and collect food. Male bees, called drones, mate with queens."],["Making honey","Worker bees suck nectar from flowers and carry it back to the hive in a special honey stomach. There they pass it from bee to bee, adding enzymes, and spread it in wax cells. Fanning with their wings dries it into thick honey. One worker makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon in her lifetime."],["The waggle dance","A worker that finds good flowers returns to the hive and performs a waggle dance. The direction of her waggling run shows the direction of the food compared with the Sun, and its length shows the distance. Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch decoded the dance and won a Nobel Prize in 1973."]],
k:[["Species","About 20,000"],["Colony size","Up to about 60,000 honeybees"],["Queen’s eggs","Up to about 2,000 a day"],["Wingbeats","About 200 per second"],["Honey per worker","About 1/12 teaspoon in a lifetime"]],
d:[["c. 6000 BCE","Cave painting in Spain shows a person collecting honey"],["1852","Lorenzo Langstroth patents the movable-frame beehive"],["1973","Karl von Frisch wins a Nobel Prize for decoding the waggle dance"],["2006","Colony collapse disorder alarms beekeepers in the United States"],["2018","First World Bee Day held on 20 May"]],
s2:[["Solitary bees","Most bees do not live in hives. Solitary bees, such as mason bees and leafcutter bees, build their own small nests in soil, wood or hollow stems. Each female lays eggs and leaves food for her young. Many solitary bees are excellent pollinators of wildflowers and crops."],["Honeybees of the world","The western honeybee, kept by beekeepers worldwide, is only one of about a dozen honeybee species. Asia has several more, including the Asian honeybee and the giant honeybee, which builds huge single combs hanging from trees and cliffs. Tropical regions also have tiny stingless bees, kept since Maya times for their tangy honey."],["Stings","Only female bees sting. A honeybee’s sting has barbs, so it stays in the skin, and the bee dies after stinging. Other bees can sting more than once. Most stings cause pain and swelling, but some people are allergic and need emergency treatment. Scraping a sting out quickly reduces the venom injected."],["Bees in danger","Many bee populations are declining. Causes include the loss of wildflowers and nesting places, pesticides, diseases, parasites such as the varroa mite and climate change. Since 2006, beekeepers in some countries have reported large losses of colonies, sometimes called colony collapse disorder."],["Beekeeping","People have collected wild honey for thousands of years, and beekeeping began at least 4,500 years ago in ancient Egypt. The movable-frame hive, patented by Lorenzo Langstroth in 1852, let beekeepers lift out combs without destroying them. Bees also give us beeswax, used for candles and polishes."],["Helping bees","People can help bees by planting flowers that bloom at different times of year, leaving some wild corners in gardens, avoiding pesticides and providing shallow water. Buying honey from local beekeepers supports their work. In many tropical countries, farmers keep stingless bees to pollinate crops."]],
sp:["The giant honeybee","The giant honeybee builds a single open comb that can be more than a meter wide, hanging from a high branch or cliff. When threatened, thousands of bees on the comb flip their bodies in a rippling wave, called shimmering, to scare off hornets. In Nepal and parts of Southeast Asia, honey hunters still climb cliffs and trees to harvest these combs."],
g:[["Pollination","Moving pollen so plants can make seeds"],["Colony","A large group of insects living together"],["Nectar","A sugary liquid made by flowers"],["Drone","A male honeybee"]],
f:["A honeybee makes only about a twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in her life.","Only female bees can sting.","Bees tell each other where flowers are by dancing."],
fun:"Honeybees tell each other where to find flowers by dancing.",
tl:"Ang bubuyog ay insektong kumukuha ng nektar at pollen sa mga bulaklak at tumutulong sa polinasyon ng maraming pananim. Ang mga pukyutan ay gumagawa ng pulot, at may mga katutubong bubuyog sa Pilipinas gaya ng kiwot."});

ADDART({id:"spider",t:"Spiders",i:"🕷️",w:"Spider",r:["insect","bee","eco"],
p:[["Not insects","Spiders are arachnids. They have eight legs and two body parts, while insects have six legs and three parts."],["Silk spinners","All spiders make silk, which they use for webs, nests, egg sacs and safety lines."],["Pest controllers","Spiders eat huge numbers of insects, including mosquitoes and crop pests."]],
l:"Spiders are eight-legged hunters found almost everywhere on land, from deserts to rainforests and homes. All of them make silk, and many spin beautiful webs to trap prey. There are about 50,000 known species. Although some people fear them, very few spiders are dangerous to humans, and they help control insects.",
s:[["Body plan","A spider’s body has two main parts: the cephalothorax, which carries the legs, eyes and fangs, and the abdomen, which holds the silk glands. Most spiders have eight eyes, though many still see poorly and rely on feeling vibrations through their legs and webs."],["Silk","Spiders make silk in glands in their abdomen and pull it out through spinnerets. Some spiders make seven different kinds, each for a different job. Spider silk is stronger than steel of the same weight and very stretchy, and scientists are trying to copy it for ropes, medical stitches and clothing."],["Webs and hunters","Orb weavers spin round, wheel-shaped webs with sticky spirals. Other spiders build sheet webs, funnels or messy tangles. Many spiders spin no webs at all: wolf spiders chase prey on the ground, jumping spiders leap on it, and trapdoor spiders wait in burrows."],["Venom","Almost all spiders use venom from their fangs to paralyze prey. They then pour digestive juices onto it and suck up the liquid. Only a few kinds, such as widow spiders and some recluses, can cause serious harm to people, and bites are rare."]],
k:[["Species","About 50,000 known"],["Legs","8"],["Largest by weight","Goliath birdeater, about 175 g"],["Widest leg span","Giant huntsman, about 30 cm"],["Silk strength","Stronger than steel of the same weight"]],
d:[["c. 380 million years ago","Early spider relatives leave fossils in Devonian rocks"],["1757","Carl Clerck publishes the first scientific book naming spiders"],["1973","Spiders Arabella and Anita spin webs in space aboard Skylab"],["2010","Darwin’s bark spider, which spins webs across rivers, is described"],["2017","Study estimates spiders eat 400 to 800 million metric tons of prey a year"]],
s2:[["Jumping spiders","Jumping spiders have two large front eyes that give them sharp, color vision. They stalk insects like tiny cats and pounce, trailing a silk safety line. Some can leap many times their own body length. Males perform colorful dances to win females."],["Spiders that help farmers","Spiders eat more insects than any other predator on land, an estimated 400 to 800 million metric tons a year. In rice fields across Asia, spiders eat planthoppers and other pests, reducing the need for pesticides. Golden orb weavers, whose webs shine yellow in the sunlight, live in warm regions around the world."],["Spider fighting","In several parts of Asia, including Japan and the Philippines, people have long kept spiders for friendly fights. Two spiders are placed on a stick, and one is pushed toward the other until one falls or is wrapped in silk. Some people worry about the harm to spiders and the forests they are taken from."],["Babies and ballooning","Female spiders wrap their eggs in silk sacs, sometimes carrying them around. When the spiderlings hatch, many release fine threads into the wind and float away, a trick called ballooning. Ballooning spiders have been found kilometers high in the air and far out at sea."],["Space spiders","In 1973 two garden spiders named Arabella and Anita flew to the Skylab space station as part of a student’s experiment. At first their webs were messy, but they soon adjusted to weightlessness and spun webs much like those on Earth, though with thinner threads."],["Fear of spiders","Fear of spiders, called arachnophobia, is one of the most common fears. Yet spiders are useful neighbors: one study estimated that the world’s spiders eat 400 to 800 million metric tons of insects and other prey each year, about as much as all the meat and fish people eat."]],
sp:["Darwin’s bark spider","Darwin’s bark spider of Madagascar spins the largest known orb webs, stretched across rivers and lakes on anchor lines up to about 25 meters long. Its silk is the toughest biological material ever tested, about ten times tougher than Kevlar, the material used in bulletproof vests. It was described by scientists in 2010."],
g:[["Arachnid","An eight-legged animal such as a spider or scorpion"],["Spinneret","The organ a spider uses to spin silk"],["Orb web","A round, wheel-shaped web"],["Ballooning","Floating through the air on silk threads"]],
f:["Spider silk is stronger than steel of the same weight.","Spiders flew to space on Skylab in 1973.","Spiders may eat as much prey each year as all people eat meat and fish."],
fun:"Two spiders spun webs in space aboard Skylab in 1973.",
tl:"Ang gagamba ay arachnid na may walong paa at gumagawa ng sutla para sa sapot nito. Kumakain ito ng maraming insekto, at kaunti lamang sa mga 50,000 uri nito ang mapanganib sa tao."});

ADDART({id:"trees",t:"Trees and Forests",i:"🌳",w:"Tree",r:["photo","eco","amazon"],
p:[["Giants of the plant world","Trees are the largest and oldest living things on Earth."],["Lungs of the planet","Through photosynthesis, trees take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen."],["Homes for life","Forests shelter more than half of all land animals and plants."]],
l:"Trees are tall, long-lived plants with woody trunks that hold their leaves high toward the Sun. Together they form forests that cover nearly a third of Earth’s land, store huge amounts of carbon, protect soil and water and shelter most of the world’s land species. People depend on trees for wood, food, medicine and clean air.",
s:[["How a tree works","A tree’s roots take up water and minerals from the soil. Tubes in the wood carry them up to the leaves, where photosynthesis turns sunlight, water and carbon dioxide into sugar and oxygen. Other tubes just under the bark carry the sugar to every part of the tree."],["Growth rings","Each year, many trees add a new layer of wood under the bark. In places with seasons, this makes a ring that is light in the growing season and darker later. Counting rings tells a tree’s age, and their widths record past rainfall and temperature, a science called dendrochronology."],["Kinds of trees","Broadleaf trees, such as narra, mahogany and oak, have flat leaves and flowers. Conifers, such as pines, have needles and cones. Many broadleaf trees in cold places drop their leaves in autumn, while most tropical trees and conifers stay green all year. Palms and tree ferns look like trees but grow differently."],["Forests of the world","Tropical rainforests near the equator have the most species. Temperate forests grow in milder climates, and vast boreal forests of conifers stretch across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia. Mangrove forests grow along tropical coasts, with roots that stand in salty water."]],
k:[["Trees on Earth","About 3 trillion"],["Tree species","About 73,000"],["Tallest","Hyperion, a coast redwood, about 116 m"],["Oldest","Bristlecone pines over 4,800 years"],["Forest cover","About 31 percent of Earth’s land"]],
d:[["1872","The first Arbor Day is held in Nebraska, United States"],["1977","Wangari Maathai founds the Green Belt Movement in Kenya to plant trees"],["2004","Wangari Maathai wins the Nobel Peace Prize for her tree-planting movement"],["2006","Hyperion, the world’s tallest known tree, is found in California"],["2015","Study estimates there are about 3 trillion trees on Earth"]],
s2:[["Record breakers","The tallest known tree is Hyperion, a coast redwood in California, about 116 meters high. The largest by volume is General Sherman, a giant sequoia. Some Great Basin bristlecone pines are more than 4,800 years old, and clonal tree colonies, sharing one root system, may be far older."],["Tropical forests of Asia","The rainforests of Southeast Asia, on Borneo, Sumatra, the Malay Peninsula and the Philippines, are dominated by tall dipterocarp trees, some more than 80 meters high. Logging, plantations and farming have cleared much of them. The remaining forests shelter orangutans, hornbills, tarsiers and thousands of plants found nowhere else."],["National trees","Many countries honor a national tree. The oak is a symbol of England, Germany and the United States, the maple leaf appears on Canada’s flag, the cedar on Lebanon’s, and the banyan is India’s national tree. The narra, with hard reddish wood, is the Philippines’ national tree. Some, such as the cedar of Lebanon and the narra, became rare from heavy cutting and are now protected."],["Mangroves","Mangrove trees grow in the salty mud of tropical coasts. Their tangled roots shelter young fish, crabs and shrimp and protect coasts from waves and storm surges. Indonesia has the largest area of mangroves of any country. Replanting them is one way coastal towns prepare for storms."],["Trees and climate","Trees store carbon in their wood, roots and soil. When forests are burned or cut down, much of that carbon returns to the air as carbon dioxide, adding to climate change. Protecting old forests and restoring damaged ones are among the most effective ways to slow global warming."],["Talking trees?","Trees are linked underground by fungi that wrap around their roots. Through these networks, trees exchange water and nutrients with the fungi, and some scientists think trees can share resources with each other. Researchers still debate how much trees really communicate."]],
sp:["Wangari Maathai","In 1977 Kenyan scientist Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement, which helped women plant trees to restore damaged land, provide firewood and protect water supplies. The movement has planted tens of millions of trees. In 2004 she became the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize."],
g:[["Dendrochronology","Dating events using tree rings"],["Conifer","A tree with needles and cones"],["Dipterocarp","A family of tall tropical trees common in Southeast Asian rainforests"],["Mangrove","A tree that grows in salty coastal water"]],
f:["There are about 3 trillion trees on Earth.","The tallest known tree is about 116 meters tall.","Some bristlecone pines are more than 4,800 years old."],
fun:"There are about 3 trillion trees on Earth, roughly 400 for every person.",
tl:"Ang puno ang pinakamalaki at pinakamatandang buhay na bagay sa mundo. Nagbibigay ito ng oxygen, tirahan ng mga hayop at kahoy, at ang narra ang pambansang puno ng Pilipinas mula 1934."});

;
