ADDART({id:"japancountry",t:"Japan",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗾",w:"Japan",r:["japan","quake","volc","ww2","china","rail"],
p:[["An island nation","Japan is an island country in East Asia made up of about 6,800 islands. Four main islands, Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu and Shikoku, hold nearly all of its people."],["Tradition and technology","Japan blends old traditions such as the tea ceremony, Shinto shrines and sumo wrestling with advanced industries, including cars, electronics and robots."],["Living with earthquakes","Japan lies where several tectonic plates meet, so it has frequent earthquakes and many volcanoes. Strict building rules and early-warning systems help protect its people."]],
l:"Japan is an island country in East Asia, stretching in an arc along the western Pacific from Hokkaido in the north to Okinawa in the south. About 124 million people live there, most of them on the coastal plains of Honshu, the largest island, where the capital, Tokyo, anchors one of the biggest urban areas in the world. Japan is known for its long history, its distinctive culture and its high-tech economy.",
s:[["Land and islands","Japan has about 6,800 islands, but the four main ones hold nearly everyone. About three quarters of the country is mountainous and forested, so most people live on narrow coastal plains. Mount Fuji, at 3,776 m, is the highest peak and a national symbol."],["Earthquakes and volcanoes","Japan lies on the Pacific Ring of Fire. It has more than 100 active volcanoes and experiences earthquakes almost daily, most of them small. In March 2011 a magnitude 9.0 earthquake off the northeast coast set off a huge tsunami that killed about 18,000 people and damaged the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant."],["Cities","Greater Tokyo is home to roughly 37 million people and was long counted as the largest metropolitan area in the world; a 2025 United Nations report, using a new method, ranked it third after Jakarta and Dhaka. Other major cities include Osaka, Nagoya, Yokohama, Sapporo and Kyoto, the old imperial capital known for its temples and gardens. Fast, punctual trains link the main cities."],["Language and religion","Japanese is written with three scripts used together: kanji, characters borrowed from Chinese, and the syllable scripts hiragana and katakana. Shinto, the native religion, and Buddhism both shape festivals and daily life, and many Japanese people take part in both."]],
k:[["Capital","Tokyo"],["Population","About 124 million (2024)"],["Area","About 378,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Fuji, 3,776 m"],["Currency","Japanese yen"]],
d:[["794","Capital moves to Heian-kyō, now Kyoto"],["1603","Tokugawa shogunate begins; Edo, now Tokyo, becomes the political center"],["1853","American ships under Commodore Perry arrive, ending centuries of seclusion"],["1868","Meiji Restoration begins rapid modernization"],["1945","Japan surrenders at the end of World War II"],["1964","Tokyo Olympics and the first Shinkansen bullet train"],["2011","Great East Japan earthquake and tsunami"]],
s2:[["Samurai and shoguns","From the late 1100s, military rulers called shoguns governed Japan in the emperor’s name, supported by samurai, a warrior class that followed a code of loyalty and honor. The Tokugawa shoguns kept the peace from 1603 to 1868, a time named Edo after the capital, when cities, trade and arts such as kabuki theater and woodblock prints flourished."],["Opening to the world","For over two centuries Japan strictly limited contact with foreigners. In 1853 American warships under Commodore Matthew Perry demanded trade, and the shogunate soon collapsed. In 1868 the Meiji Restoration returned power to the emperor and launched rapid change: railways, factories, a modern army, a national school system and, in 1889, a constitution."],["War and recovery","Japan built an empire in the early 1900s, fought a long war in China from 1937 and entered World War II against the United States and its allies in 1941. The war ended in August 1945, after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Under a new constitution in 1947, Japan became a democracy and renounced war as a means of settling disputes, and the emperor became a symbol of the state."],["The economic miracle","After the war Japan rebuilt with astonishing speed. By the late 1960s it had the world’s second-largest economy, behind only the United States, and brands such as Toyota, Sony and Nintendo became global names. Japan was overtaken by China in 2010 and Germany in 2023, and India has been catching up with it, but Japan remains a leader in cars, machinery, electronics and robotics."],["An aging society","Japanese people have among the longest lives in the world, with an average life expectancy of about 84 years. Together with a low birth rate, this means the population is aging and shrinking: it peaked at about 128 million around 2008, and nearly three in ten people are now over 65."],["Food and culture","Japanese cuisine, built on rice, fish, vegetables and soy foods, was added to UNESCO’s intangible heritage list in 2013 as washoku. Sushi, ramen and tempura are known worldwide, as are manga, anime and video games. In spring, families gather under the cherry trees for hanami, flower viewing, a beloved national tradition."]],
sp:["The Shinkansen","In October 1964, just before the Tokyo Olympics, Japan opened the Tokaido Shinkansen between Tokyo and Osaka, the world’s first high-speed railway. Its trains ran at 210 km/h and cut the journey from about six and a half hours to four. The network has since grown to thousands of kilometers, and its trains are famous for their punctuality and safety. The Shinkansen inspired high-speed rail systems in France, China and many other countries."],
g:[["Archipelago","A group or chain of islands"],["Shogun","A military ruler of Japan before 1868"],["Samurai","A warrior of old Japan"],["Shinto","Japan’s native religion, which honors spirits called kami"],["Tsunami","A giant sea wave caused by an earthquake or eruption"],["Kanji","Chinese characters used to write Japanese"]],
f:["Japan has more than 100 active volcanoes.","Vending machines in Japan sell everything from hot drinks to umbrellas.","The Japanese name for the country, Nihon or Nippon, means origin of the sun."],
fun:"Japan is made up of about 6,800 islands.",
tl:"Ang Hapon ay bansang pulo sa Silangang Asya na may halos 6,800 pulo. Kilala ito sa Tokyo, sa Bundok Fuji, sa mga bullet train at sa pinagsamang tradisyon at makabagong teknolohiya."});

;
