ADDART({id:"brazilcountry",t:"Brazil",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇧🇷",w:"Brazil",r:["amazon","sugar","coffee","soccer","explor","urban"],
p:[["The giant of South America","Brazil is the largest country in South America and the fifth largest in the world, with about 212 million people. It is the only country in the Americas where Portuguese is the main language."],["Rainforest and wetlands","Brazil holds about 60 percent of the Amazon rainforest, the Pantanal, the largest tropical wetland on Earth, and a coastline of about 7,400 km on the Atlantic."],["A blend of peoples","Brazilians are descended from Indigenous peoples, Portuguese settlers, Africans brought as enslaved workers and later immigrants from Europe, the Middle East and Asia."]],
l:"Brazil is the largest country in South America, covering nearly half of the continent. About 212 million people live there, most of them in cities along the Atlantic coast, such as São Paulo and Rio de Janeiro. Brazil is home to most of the Amazon rainforest, one of the richest natural regions on Earth, and is known for its music, its football and its lively mix of cultures.",
s:[["Land and climate","Brazil’s north is covered by the Amazon basin, whose rivers carry more water than any others on Earth. Inland lie the Pantanal wetland and the Brazilian Highlands, a vast plateau of farms and savanna called the cerrado. Most of the country is tropical, but the far south has a mild climate with occasional frost."],["Cities","São Paulo is the largest city in the Americas, with more than 12 million people in the city itself. Rio de Janeiro is famous for its beaches, its Carnival and the statue of Christ the Redeemer. The capital, Brasília, was built from scratch in the interior and opened in 1960."],["People","Brazil’s population is among the most varied in the world. It has the largest African-descended population outside Africa, about two million people of Japanese descent, the largest Japanese community outside Japan, and large communities of Italian, German, Lebanese and Indigenous heritage."],["Language and religion","Portuguese is spoken everywhere, though Brazilian Portuguese differs from the European form in sound and vocabulary. Brazil has more Roman Catholics than any other country, alongside fast-growing Protestant churches and Afro-Brazilian traditions such as Candomblé."]],
k:[["Capital","Brasília"],["Largest city","São Paulo"],["Population","About 212 million (2024)"],["Area","About 8.5 million km²"],["Language","Portuguese"]],
d:[["1500","Pedro Álvares Cabral reaches the coast; Portugal claims Brazil"],["1822","Independence from Portugal declared on 7 September"],["1888","Slavery abolished, the last country in the Americas to do so"],["1889","Brazil becomes a republic"],["1960","Brasília becomes the capital"],["1985","Military rule ends"],["1988","Current democratic constitution adopted"]],
s2:[["The Portuguese colony","After Cabral’s arrival in 1500, Portugal built sugar plantations along the northeastern coast, which needed huge numbers of workers. Gold and diamonds found in the south in the 1690s drew thousands more settlers. Indigenous peoples, who numbered in the millions, were driven from their lands or died of disease and violence."],["Slavery and its end","About five million enslaved Africans were taken to Brazil, far more than to any other country. They worked on sugar, gold, coffee and cotton. Slavery was abolished only in 1888, and its legacy still shapes questions of race and inequality in Brazil today."],["Empire and republic","When Napoleon invaded Portugal, the Portuguese royal family fled to Brazil in 1808. Pedro, the king’s son, declared independence in 1822 and became emperor. In 1889 army officers overthrew his son and proclaimed a republic."],["Dictatorship and democracy","The army ruled from 1964 to 1985, jailing and exiling opponents. Democracy returned in 1985, and a new constitution in 1988 guaranteed wide rights. Since then the country has held regular elections, and social programs have lifted tens of millions of people out of poverty."],["Farms, factories and forests","Brazil is the world’s largest producer of coffee and one of the largest exporters of soybeans, beef, sugar and iron ore. It also builds aircraft, through the company Embraer. Clearing forest for farming and ranching has destroyed large parts of the Amazon, and protecting it is among the country’s greatest challenges."],["Music and football","Samba, born in Afro-Brazilian communities, and bossa nova, which emerged in the 1950s, have shaped world music. Football is a national passion: Brazil has won the men’s World Cup five times, in 1958, 1962, 1970, 1994 and 2002, and produced stars such as Pelé."]],
sp:["Brasília","Brazil’s capital was planned and built in about four years, from 1956 to 1960, on an empty plateau in the interior, to draw people and development away from the coast. Urban planner Lúcio Costa laid out the city in a shape often compared to an airplane or a bird, and architect Oscar Niemeyer designed its landmark buildings, including the National Congress and the cathedral. UNESCO added Brasília to its World Heritage list in 1987 as a landmark of modern city planning."],
g:[["Favela","A densely built informal neighborhood in a Brazilian city"],["Samba","Brazilian music and dance rooted in Afro-Brazilian traditions"],["Carnival","A festival of parades and music held before Lent"],["Pantanal","The world’s largest tropical wetland"],["Plantation","A large farm growing one crop for sale"],["Deforestation","The clearing of forests"]],
f:["Brazil is the only country in the Americas where Portuguese is the main language.","Brazil has won the men’s football World Cup five times, more than any other country.","Brazil is home to more plant species than any other country."],
fun:"Brazil has won the men’s football World Cup five times.",
tl:"Ang Brazil ay pinakamalaking bansa sa Timog Amerika. Hawak nito ang malaking bahagi ng gubat ng Amazon, at Portuges ang wika ng halos lahat ng mamamayan."});
ADDART({id:"nigeriacountry",t:"Nigeria",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇳🇬",w:"Nigeria",r:["sahara","islam","christ","trade","urban"],
p:[["Africa’s most populous country","Nigeria, in West Africa, has more than 230 million people, more than any other African country and the sixth most in the world. It is home to hundreds of ethnic groups."],["Rivers and savanna","The Niger and Benue rivers meet in the middle of the country and flow south to a large delta on the Atlantic. Rainforest covers the south, and dry savanna the north."],["A creative nation","Nigeria is known for its writers, its film industry, called Nollywood, and Afrobeats, a popular music style that has spread around the world."]],
l:"Nigeria is a country in West Africa on the Gulf of Guinea, and with more than 230 million people it is the most populous country in Africa. It has more than 500 languages, a large oil industry and a fast-growing economy, and its cities, especially Lagos, are among the busiest on the continent. Nigerian writers, filmmakers and musicians have a worldwide audience.",
s:[["Land and climate","Southern Nigeria is hot and wet, with mangrove swamps along the coast and rainforest inland. The north is drier, and from December to February the dusty harmattan wind blows in from the Sahara. The Niger Delta, where the Niger River splits into many channels before reaching the sea, is one of the largest wetlands in the world."],["Cities","Lagos, on the coast, is Nigeria’s largest city and one of the biggest in Africa, with a metropolitan area of well over 15 million people. The capital is Abuja, a planned city in the center of the country, which replaced Lagos as capital in 1991. Kano and Ibadan are other large, ancient cities."],["Peoples and languages","Nigeria has more than 500 languages. The three largest groups are the Hausa and Fulani in the north, the Yoruba in the southwest and the Igbo in the southeast. English is the official language and a language of schooling, and Nigerian Pidgin is widely spoken in everyday life."],["Religion","About half of Nigerians are Muslim, mostly in the north, and about half are Christian, mostly in the south. Traditional beliefs also remain important, and festivals and music often draw on all of these."]],
k:[["Capital","Abuja"],["Largest city","Lagos"],["Population","About 230 million (2024)"],["Area","About 924,000 km²"],["Official language","English"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","Nok culture makes terracotta sculptures"],["1804","Usman dan Fodio founds the Sokoto Caliphate"],["1914","British combine north and south into one colony"],["1960","Independence from Britain on 1 October"],["1967","Civil war begins; it ends in 1970"],["1999","Return to civilian rule"]],
s2:[["Ancient kingdoms","The Nok people of central Nigeria made fine terracotta sculptures more than 2,000 years ago. Later the Yoruba city of Ife, the Hausa city-states of the north and the Kingdom of Benin grew rich on trade. Benin’s craftsmen cast famous bronze plaques and heads, many of which were taken by British troops in 1897 and are now in museums around the world."],["Empires and colonial rule","In 1804 the scholar Usman dan Fodio led a movement that created the Sokoto Caliphate, one of the largest states in Africa. Britain took control of Lagos in 1861 and gradually of the whole region, and in 1914 joined the northern and southern territories into a single colony called Nigeria."],["Independence and civil war","Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960. Military coups followed, and in 1967 the southeastern region declared itself the independent state of Biafra, starting a civil war that ended in 1970 with great loss of life. The military governed for much of the next three decades before civilian rule returned in 1999."],["Oil and the economy","Nigeria has been one of Africa’s largest oil producers since the 1950s, and oil provides most of the country’s export earnings. The Niger Delta has suffered pollution and conflict. The economy also includes farming, a large technology sector centered on Lagos, and banking and telecommunications."],["Literature and music","Chinua Achebe’s novel Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, is one of the most widely read African books. Wole Soyinka won the 1986 Nobel Prize in Literature, the first African writer to do so. Fela Kuti created Afrobeat in the 1970s, and today Afrobeats stars fill stadiums around the world."],["Food and festivals","Jollof rice, a spicy tomato rice dish, is celebrated across West Africa, and Nigerians take pride in their version. Other favorites include suya, spiced grilled meat, pounded yam with egusi soup, and fried plantains. Festivals such as the Durbar in the north, with horse parades, and Lagos Carnival show the country’s variety."]],
sp:["Nollywood","Nigeria’s film industry, nicknamed Nollywood, began in the early 1990s when filmmakers started making low-budget movies directly on video. The film Living in Bondage (1992) is often credited as the start of the modern industry. By the late 2000s UNESCO ranked Nigeria second only to India in the number of films produced each year. Nollywood stories, about family, love, faith, crime and comedy, are watched across Africa and by Nigerians abroad, and streaming services now commission Nigerian films for audiences worldwide."],
g:[["Nollywood","Nigeria’s film industry"],["Afrobeats","West African popular music inspired by local and global styles"],["Harmattan","A dry, dusty wind from the Sahara that blows over West Africa in winter"],["Federation","A country made of states that share power with a central government"],["Delta","A landform where a river meets the sea in many channels"],["Pidgin","A simplified language used between groups"]],
f:["Nigeria has more than 500 languages.","Nigerian Pidgin is spoken by tens of millions of people.","Lagos is one of the fastest-growing cities in Africa."],
fun:"Nigeria is the most populous country in Africa.",
tl:"Ang Nigeria ay bansa sa Kanlurang Aprika at pinakamarami ang mamamayan sa buong kontinente. May mahigit 500 wika ito at kilala sa Nollywood at Afrobeats."});

;
