ADDART({id:"germanycountry",t:"Germany",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇩🇪",w:"Germany",r:["wwi","ww2","coldwar","reform","print","renew"],
p:[["The heart of Europe","Germany lies in central Europe, between the North Sea and the Alps, and has about 84 million people, more than any other country in the European Union."],["A federal republic","Germany is a democracy made up of sixteen states, called Länder. Its capital and largest city is Berlin, and Frankfurt is its financial center."],["Industry and ideas","Germany is Europe’s largest economy and a leader in cars, machinery and chemicals. It is also the home of Bach, Beethoven, Goethe and Einstein."]],
l:"Germany is a country in central Europe with about 84 million people, making it the most populous member of the European Union. It stretches from the North Sea and the Baltic coast in the north to the Alps in the south, and its capital is Berlin. Germany has the largest economy in Europe, a long tradition in science, music and philosophy, and a modern history marked by war, division and reunification.",
s:[["Land and rivers","Northern Germany is a low plain, central Germany has forested hills such as the Harz and the Black Forest, and in the south the land rises to the Bavarian Alps, where the Zugspitze, at 2,962 m, is the highest peak. The Rhine, Elbe and Danube are major rivers and important trade routes."],["Cities","Berlin is the capital, with about 3.7 million people. Hamburg is a great port, Munich is the capital of Bavaria, and Frankfurt hosts the European Central Bank and a major stock exchange. Cologne is known for its cathedral. Germany has no single dominant city, so economic and cultural life is spread across the country."],["Government","Under the Basic Law of 1949 Germany is a federal parliamentary republic. The Bundestag, the national parliament, elects the chancellor, who leads the government, while the president is mostly a ceremonial head of state. The states have wide powers over education and policing."],["People and language","German is the main language and is spoken in Austria and Switzerland as well. Germany has welcomed millions of immigrants since the 1950s, and nearly three in ten of its people have an immigrant background. Christianity is the largest religion, though many Germans have no religious affiliation, and Islam is the second largest."]],
k:[["Capital","Berlin"],["Population","About 84 million (2024)"],["Area","About 357,000 km²"],["Highest point","Zugspitze, 2,962 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["962","Otto I crowned emperor, founding the Holy Roman Empire"],["1517","Martin Luther begins the Reformation"],["1871","Germany unified as an empire under Bismarck"],["1945","Nazi regime falls; Germany is divided into occupation zones"],["1949","West Germany and East Germany founded"],["1961","Berlin Wall built"],["1990","Germany reunified on 3 October"]],
s2:[["The Holy Roman Empire","From 962 until 1806 a loose union of German-speaking states, the Holy Roman Empire, dominated central Europe. Its hundreds of principalities and free cities made Germany a patchwork of different rulers, which encouraged rivalry and creativity, with universities, printing presses and courts all competing for talent."],["Reformation and printing","Around 1450 Johannes Gutenberg developed movable-type printing in Mainz. In 1517 the monk Martin Luther published his arguments against church practices, and his German translation of the Bible helped standardize the language. The Reformation split western Christianity and led to a century and more of religious conflict."],["Unification and empire","In 1871, after wars led by Prussia under Chancellor Otto von Bismarck, the German states joined in a single empire. Rapid industrialization made Germany one of Europe’s strongest economies, but rivalries with other powers helped lead to World War I, which ended in German defeat in 1918."],["Nazi rule and the Second World War","After the Weimar Republic of 1919 to 1933 collapsed, Adolf Hitler’s Nazi regime ruled from 1933 to 1945. It started World War II in 1939 and murdered six million Jews and millions of other people in the Holocaust. Germany was defeated in 1945, and its cities lay in ruins."],["Division and reunification","After the war Germany was split into democratic West Germany and communist East Germany, and Berlin was divided by a wall from 1961. The wall fell on 9 November 1989, and the two Germanys were reunified on 3 October 1990, which is now a national holiday."],["Industry and green energy","Germany’s success rests on exports of cars, machinery, chemicals and medical equipment, and on many family-owned mid-sized firms known as the Mittelstand. Its Energiewende, or energy transition, aims to replace fossil fuels and nuclear power with renewables, which supplied about half of the country’s electricity in 2023. The last nuclear plants closed in April 2023."]],
sp:["The Berlin Wall","East Germany began building the Berlin Wall on 13 August 1961 to stop its citizens fleeing to the West. In all, the barrier ran for about 155 km around West Berlin, with watchtowers, guards and a strip of open ground. At least 140 people died trying to cross. On 9 November 1989, after weeks of peaceful protest, East Germany announced that its borders were open, and crowds climbed on the wall and began to take it apart. Within a year Germany was reunified, and pieces of the wall can still be seen in Berlin."],
g:[["Bundestag","Germany’s federal parliament"],["Länder","The sixteen states of Germany"],["Reunification","The joining of East and West Germany in 1990"],["Autobahn","A German motorway, parts of which have no general speed limit"],["Mittelstand","Germany’s many small and mid-sized family firms"],["Energiewende","Germany’s shift to renewable energy"]],
f:["Parts of Germany’s autobahn network have no general speed limit.","Johannes Gutenberg developed movable-type printing in Mainz around 1450.","Germany has won the men’s football World Cup four times."],
fun:"Germany is the most populous country in the European Union.",
tl:"Ang Alemanya ay bansa sa gitnang Europa na may humigit-kumulang 84 milyong tao. Ito ang pinakamalaking ekonomiya sa Europa at nagkaisang muli ang Silangan at Kanlurang Alemanya noong 1990."});
ADDART({id:"indiacountry",t:"India",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇮🇳",w:"India",r:["zero","hindu","buddh","islam","chess","cricket"],
p:[["The most populous country","India, in South Asia, has about 1.45 billion people, more than any other country. It is also the seventh largest country in the world by area."],["The largest democracy","India is a federal republic of 28 states and 8 union territories. Its capital is New Delhi, and its elections are the largest in the world."],["Many faiths and languages","Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism began in India, which is also home to hundreds of millions of Muslims and Christians. Hindi and English are the official languages of the national government, and the constitution lists 22 languages in all."]],
l:"India is a country in South Asia and, with about 1.45 billion people, the most populous in the world. It stretches from the Himalayas in the north to tropical beaches in the south, and it is the world’s largest democracy. India has one of the oldest civilizations on Earth, a huge variety of languages and religions, and one of the fastest-growing major economies.",
s:[["Land and climate","India’s north is walled by the Himalayas, whose Kanchenjunga, at 8,586 m, is its highest peak. South of them lie the vast plain of the Ganges and the Indus, the dry Thar Desert in the northwest and the Deccan Plateau in the center. Most rain falls during the summer monsoon, on which millions of farmers depend."],["Cities","Mumbai is the financial capital and home of the Hindi film industry, called Bollywood. Delhi, with New Delhi as the capital within it, is one of the largest cities on Earth. Kolkata, Chennai and Bengaluru, a center of software, are other great cities, but about two thirds of Indians still live in villages and small towns."],["Government","India has been a democracy since independence, and its constitution came into force on 26 January 1950. Voters choose the lower house of parliament, the Lok Sabha, and the prime minister leads the government, while the president is head of state. States have their own elected governments."],["Languages and religions","India has 22 scheduled languages, including Hindi, Bengali, Tamil, Telugu and Urdu, and hundreds of dialects. About four in five Indians are Hindu and about one in seven are Muslim, with large Christian, Sikh, Buddhist and Jain communities. Festivals such as Diwali, Eid and Holi are celebrated across communities."]],
k:[["Capital","New Delhi"],["Population","About 1.45 billion (2024)"],["Area","About 3.29 million km²"],["Highest point","Kanchenjunga, 8,586 m"],["Currency","Indian rupee"]],
d:[["c. 2600 BCE","Cities of the Indus Valley civilization flourish"],["c. 268 BCE","Ashoka begins his reign over the Mauryan Empire"],["1526","Babur founds the Mughal Empire"],["1858","British Crown takes direct control of India"],["1947","Independence and partition on 15 August"],["1950","Constitution takes effect on 26 January"],["2023","India becomes the world’s most populous country"]],
s2:[["The Indus Valley","Around 2600 BCE people in the valley of the Indus River built planned cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, with straight streets, brick houses and drains. The civilization traded as far as Mesopotamia, but its writing has never been fully deciphered, and it declined about 1900 BCE."],["Empires and ideas","Over the next two thousand years India produced great empires, including the Mauryan Empire under Ashoka, who spread Buddhism, and the Gupta Empire, a golden age of science and art. Indian mathematicians developed the decimal system and the use of zero, and the game of chess began in India."],["The Mughals","From 1526 the Mughal Empire ruled much of the subcontinent, and it combined Persian, Central Asian and Indian traditions in art, architecture and cuisine. The emperor Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal in the 1600s. The empire weakened in the 1700s as European trading companies grew powerful."],["British rule","The British East India Company gradually took control from the 1750s, and after a rebellion in 1857 the British Crown ruled directly until 1947, an era known as the Raj. Famines, high taxes and the destruction of local industries caused widespread suffering, and resistance grew."],["Independence and partition","Led by Mohandas Gandhi, who used nonviolent protest, including the Salt March of 1930, and by Jawaharlal Nehru, India won independence on 15 August 1947. British India was divided into India and Pakistan, and millions of people were displaced in one of the largest migrations in history."],["Economy and space","After the 1990s India opened its economy, and it became a world leader in software services and in producing low-cost medicines. It is now one of the five largest economies in the world and among the fastest-growing. Its Mars orbiter reached Mars in 2014, and in 2023 Chandrayaan-3 became the first spacecraft to land near the Moon’s south pole."]],
sp:["The Taj Mahal","The Taj Mahal, in Agra, was built between about 1632 and 1653 by the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died in childbirth. Built of white marble and inlaid with semiprecious stones, it stands on a platform beside the Yamuna River, with four tall minarets at its corners. Thousands of craftsmen and laborers worked on it. UNESCO added the Taj Mahal to its World Heritage list in 1983, and millions of people visit it every year."],
g:[["Raj","British rule in India, from 1858 to 1947"],["Partition","The 1947 division of British India into India and Pakistan"],["Monsoon","A seasonal wind that brings heavy rain"],["Caste","A traditional hereditary social class in South Asia"],["Bollywood","The Hindi-language film industry based in Mumbai"],["Yoga","A system of physical and mental exercises that began in India"]],
f:["India became the world’s most populous country in 2023.","The game of chess began in India.","India has one of the largest postal networks in the world."],
fun:"India has the world’s largest democracy.",
tl:"Ang India ay bansa sa Timog Asya at pinakamarami ang mamamayan sa buong mundo, humigit-kumulang 1.45 bilyon. Ito ang pinakamalaking demokrasya at pinagmulan ng zero at ng laro ng chess."});
ADDART({id:"usacountry",t:"United States",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇺🇸",w:"United States",r:["usrev","coldwar","space","jazz","bball","urban"],
p:[["A continent-sized country","The United States is made up of 50 states, mostly in North America, and has about 340 million people. It is the third most populous country in the world."],["A federal republic","The Constitution divides power between the national government and the states, and among a president, Congress and the courts. The capital is Washington, D.C."],["A nation of immigrants","People from every part of the world have settled in the United States, and its culture blends many traditions, from jazz and blues to Hollywood and hip hop."]],
l:"The United States of America is a country of 50 states, mostly in central North America, with Alaska to the northwest and Hawaii in the Pacific. About 340 million people live there, and its economy is the largest in the world. The United States is a federal republic whose Constitution, written in 1787, is the oldest written national constitution still in use.",
s:[["Land and climate","The country includes the Appalachian Mountains in the east, the Great Plains in the center, the Rocky Mountains in the west and deserts in the southwest. The Mississippi and Missouri rivers drain the interior, and the Great Lakes are the largest group of freshwater lakes on Earth. Denali, in Alaska, at 6,190 m, is the highest peak, and the climate ranges from Arctic to tropical."],["Cities","New York City, with about 8 million people in the city itself, is the largest city, and its metropolitan area is among the biggest in the world. Los Angeles, Chicago and Houston are the next largest, and Washington, D.C., is the capital. About four in five Americans live in urban areas."],["Government","The Constitution creates three branches of government: Congress, made up of the Senate and the House of Representatives, the president and the courts. Each of the fifty states has its own government and constitution. The Bill of Rights, added in 1791, protects freedoms such as speech, religion and a fair trial."],["People and language","The United States has more immigrants than any other country, and its people include descendants of Native Americans, Europeans, Africans brought as enslaved people, and Asians and Latin Americans. English is the most widely spoken language, and Spanish is the second most common."]],
k:[["Capital","Washington, D.C."],["Population","About 340 million (2024)"],["Area","About 9.8 million km²"],["Highest point","Denali, 6,190 m"],["Currency","US dollar"]],
d:[["1776","Declaration of Independence adopted on 4 July"],["1787","Constitution written in Philadelphia"],["1861","Civil War begins; it ends in 1865"],["1865","13th Amendment abolishes slavery"],["1920","19th Amendment guarantees women the vote"],["1964","Civil Rights Act ends legal segregation"],["1969","Apollo 11 astronauts land on the Moon"]],
s2:[["Native peoples and colonies","Native American peoples lived across North America for thousands of years before Europeans arrived. English colonies were founded on the Atlantic coast from 1607, and by 1776 there were thirteen. Colonists, enslaved Africans and immigrants all shaped the new society, and Native nations lost most of their lands over the following centuries."],["Independence and the Constitution","The thirteen colonies broke from Britain in the American Revolution, declaring independence in 1776 and winning the war in 1783. The Constitution, written in 1787 and in force from 1789, set up a strong federal government, and George Washington became the first president."],["Expansion and civil war","The United States expanded across the continent in the 1800s by purchase, treaty and conquest. Disagreement over slavery led to the Civil War of 1861 to 1865, in which the Union defeated the Confederacy. Slavery was abolished, but the struggle for equal rights for Black Americans continued for another century."],["Rise to world power","Industry, railways and immigration made the United States the world’s biggest economy by 1900. It helped decide both world wars, and after 1945 it led the Western alliance in the Cold War against the Soviet Union. In 1969 Apollo 11 astronauts became the first people to walk on the Moon."],["The civil rights movement","In the 1950s and 1960s Black Americans, led by figures such as Martin Luther King Jr. and Rosa Parks, protested against segregation with marches, boycotts and sit-ins. Their movement won the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965, changing American law and society."],["Economy and innovation","The United States has the world’s largest economy, with strong sectors in technology, finance, health care, entertainment and agriculture. Its companies in Silicon Valley and elsewhere have created many of the world’s leading software and internet services. The US dollar is the main currency used in global trade and reserves."]],
sp:["Yellowstone, the first national park","In 1872 the United States set aside Yellowstone, in the northwest corner of Wyoming and parts of Montana and Idaho, as the world’s first national park. It covers about 9,000 km² and contains roughly half of the world’s geysers, including Old Faithful, as well as hot springs, canyons, herds of bison and grizzly bears. The park sits on a huge volcanic hot spot. The idea spread, and there are now thousands of national parks around the world."],
g:[["Constitution","The written basis of the nation’s laws"],["Congress","The US legislature, made up of the Senate and the House of Representatives"],["Federalism","A system dividing power between national and state governments"],["Prairie","A large area of flat grassland"],["Immigrant","A person who moves to live permanently in another country"],["Silicon Valley","A California region known for technology companies"]],
f:["Yellowstone, set aside in 1872, was the world’s first national park.","The United States has 50 states and one federal district.","The US Constitution is the oldest written national constitution still in use."],
fun:"The United States has more immigrants than any other country.",
tl:"Ang Estados Unidos ay bansang binubuo ng 50 estado at may humigit-kumulang 340 milyong tao. Ito ang may pinakamalaking ekonomiya sa mundo at ang Konstitusyon nito noong 1787 ang pinakamatandang nakasulat na pambansang konstitusyon na ginagamit pa."});

;
