ADDART({id:"egyptcountry",t:"Egypt",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇪🇬",w:"Egypt",r:["egypt","nile","sahara","islam","christ","med"],
p:[["A gift of the Nile","Egypt lies in the northeast corner of Africa, with a part, the Sinai Peninsula, in Asia. Nearly all of its roughly 116 million people live in the narrow Nile valley and delta, which make up only a small part of the country."],["Desert and sea","Most of Egypt is desert, including the Sahara in the west. The country has coasts on both the Mediterranean and the Red Sea."],["Old and new","Egypt is home to some of the oldest monuments on Earth, such as the pyramids, and to Cairo, the largest city in the Arab world and in Africa."]],
l:"Egypt is a country in northeast Africa, linked to Asia by the Sinai Peninsula. About 116 million people live there, almost all of them along the Nile, whose waters have fed one of the world’s oldest civilizations for five thousand years. Today Egypt is a major country of the Arab world, with a capital, Cairo, that is among the largest cities on Earth, and the Suez Canal, one of the world’s most important waterways.",
s:[["Land and river","More than nine tenths of Egypt is desert. The Nile flows north through the country for about 1,500 km and fans out into a green delta before reaching the Mediterranean. Farmers have always depended on the river, and the Aswan High Dam, completed in 1970, now controls its flow and supplies electricity."],["Cities","Cairo and its suburbs have more than 20 million people. Alexandria, on the Mediterranean, was founded by Alexander the Great in 331 BCE and was home to a great ancient library and lighthouse. Luxor and Aswan in the south are known for their temples and tombs."],["People and language","Arabic is the official language, though Egyptian Arabic differs in sound and vocabulary from the formal written form. About nine in ten Egyptians are Muslim and about one in ten are Christian, most of them Copts, whose church traces its roots to the first century."],["Government","Egypt has been a republic since 1953. It has a president, a parliament and a constitution, which has been revised several times. After the uprising of January 2011 forced President Hosni Mubarak to resign, the country went through a period of political change that is still debated."]],
k:[["Capital","Cairo"],["Population","About 116 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1 million km²"],["Longest river","The Nile"],["Official language","Arabic"]],
d:[["c. 3100 BCE","Upper and Lower Egypt are united"],["30 BCE","Rome annexes Egypt after Cleopatra’s death"],["641","Arab armies conquer Egypt"],["1869","Suez Canal opens"],["1952","Army officers overthrow the monarchy"],["1979","Peace treaty with Israel is signed"],["2011","Uprising ends Mubarak’s rule"]],
s2:[["Pharaohs and pyramids","Egyptian civilization began around 3100 BCE, when a king united the country. During the Old Kingdom, from about 2600 BCE, pharaohs built the pyramids at Giza as royal tombs. Egyptians developed hieroglyphic writing, a 365-day calendar, advanced medicine and architecture, and a religion centered on the afterlife."],["Foreign rulers","Persians, Greeks under Alexander and his successors, and then Romans ruled Egypt in turn. The last Greek queen, Cleopatra VII, died in 30 BCE, and Egypt became a Roman province. Christianity spread there in the first centuries, and in 641 CE Arab armies brought Islam and the Arabic language."],["Medieval Cairo","In 969 the Fatimids founded Cairo, which became a center of learning. Al-Azhar, founded there in 970, is one of the oldest universities in the world. Later the Mamluks and, from 1517, the Ottomans ruled Egypt, and Cairo remained one of the greatest cities of the Islamic world."],["Modern Egypt","Napoleon invaded in 1798, and soon afterward Muhammad Ali took power and began modernizing the country. The Suez Canal opened in 1869, and Britain occupied Egypt from 1882. Egypt gained formal independence in 1922, and in 1952 officers led by Gamal Abdel Nasser ended the monarchy."],["Wars and peace","Nasser nationalized the Suez Canal in 1956, which led to a war with Britain, France and Israel. Egypt fought further wars with Israel in 1967 and 1973. In 1979 President Anwar Sadat signed a peace treaty with Israel, the first between Israel and an Arab state, and was assassinated two years later."],["Economy","Egypt earns money from tourism, Suez Canal fees, natural gas, farming, including its famous long-staple cotton, and money sent home by Egyptians working abroad. The government has built new cities and roads, but rapid population growth and rising prices are major challenges."]],
sp:["The Suez Canal","The Suez Canal, opened on 17 November 1869, links the Mediterranean to the Red Sea and saves ships a voyage of thousands of kilometers around Africa. The French diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps led the company that built it, using the labor of many thousands of Egyptian workers. The canal is about 193 km long and in 2022 handled about 23,000 ships, roughly one eighth of world trade by volume. Egypt nationalized it in 1956 and widened it in 2015, and its revenue is one of the country’s main sources of foreign money."],
g:[["Pharaoh","A ruler of ancient Egypt"],["Delta","A fan-shaped area where the Nile reaches the sea"],["Copts","Egyptian Christians, whose church dates to the first century"],["Papyrus","A reed used to make paper in ancient Egypt"],["Hieroglyphs","Picture signs used in ancient Egyptian writing"],["Suez Canal","A canal linking the Mediterranean and the Red Sea"]],
f:["Egypt has one of the world’s oldest universities, Al-Azhar, founded in 970.","About nine tenths of Egyptians live in the Nile valley and delta.","Egyptian cotton is prized for its long, soft fibers."],
fun:"Egypt is the most populous Arab country.",
tl:"Ang Ehipto ay bansa sa hilagang-silangang Aprika. Halos lahat ng mga taga-roon ay nakatira sa tabi ng Ilog Nilo, at dito matatagpuan ang mga piramide at ang Suez Canal."});
ADDART({id:"chinacountry",t:"China",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇨🇳",w:"China",r:["china","silk","print","mandarin","confu","buddh"],
p:[["A huge and ancient country","China, in East Asia, has about 1.41 billion people and is the second most populous country in the world. It is the third or fourth largest country by area."],["A civilization of 4,000 years","Chinese civilization is among the oldest in the world, and gave humanity paper, printing, gunpowder and the compass."],["A modern economic power","Since the late 1970s China has grown from a poor country into the world’s second-largest economy and its largest exporter."]],
l:"China is a country in East Asia with about 1.41 billion people, and its capital is Beijing. It covers a vast area, from the Himalayas and the deserts of the west to the crowded plains and coasts of the east. One of the world’s oldest civilizations, China is now the world’s second-largest economy and a leader in manufacturing, trade and technology.",
s:[["Land and rivers","China’s west is a high plateau and mountain land, including Tibet, which borders Mount Everest. The Gobi and Taklamakan deserts lie in the north and west. The Yangtze, about 6,300 km, is the longest river in Asia, and the Yellow River cradled early Chinese civilization. Most people live in the eastern third of the country."],["Cities","Shanghai, with about 25 million people, is the largest city and a great financial and port center. Beijing, the capital, has about 22 million, and Guangzhou, Shenzhen and Chongqing are also huge. China has more than 100 cities with over a million people."],["People and languages","About nine in ten Chinese belong to the Han ethnic group, and the government recognizes 55 minority groups, including Tibetans, Uyghurs and Mongols. Standard Mandarin is the national language, and many regional languages, such as Cantonese and Shanghainese, are widely spoken. All are written with Chinese characters."],["Government","China is a one-party state ruled by the Communist Party, founded in 1921. The Party leader is also normally the president and head of the military. Hong Kong and Macau are special administrative regions with their own legal and economic systems."]],
k:[["Capital","Beijing"],["Population","About 1.41 billion (2024)"],["Area","About 9.6 million km²"],["Longest river","Yangtze, about 6,300 km"],["Official language","Mandarin"]],
d:[["221 BCE","Qin unifies China under the first emperor"],["1271","Kublai Khan founds the Yuan dynasty"],["1644","Qing dynasty begins; it ends in 1912"],["1949","People’s Republic of China proclaimed"],["1978","Deng Xiaoping launches economic reforms"],["2008","Beijing hosts the Summer Olympics"],["2010","China becomes the world’s second-largest economy"]],
s2:[["Dynasties","Chinese history is traditionally told as a succession of dynasties, from the Shang, about 1600 BCE, through the Qin, who unified the country in 221 BCE, to the Han, Tang, Song, Ming and Qing. Rulers built canals, roads and the Great Wall, and a civil service chosen by examination ran the empire for centuries."],["Inventions","Chinese inventors made paper by about 100 CE, printing with blocks and, by the 1040s, with movable type, gunpowder and the magnetic compass. During the Song dynasty China had paper money, giant river ships and the world’s largest cities."],["Decline and revolution","In the 1800s the Qing empire was weakened by the Opium Wars with Britain, by foreign concessions and by rebellions such as the Taiping Rebellion, which killed millions. In 1912 a revolution ended the empire, and China became a republic, though it was soon divided among warlords."],["The People’s Republic","After a civil war, Mao Zedong proclaimed the People’s Republic on 1 October 1949. The Great Leap Forward of 1958 to 1962 led to a terrible famine, and the Cultural Revolution of 1966 to 1976 brought years of chaos and persecution. The Communist government, under Deng Xiaoping from 1978, then opened the economy to trade and private business."],["Economic rise","China became the world’s factory, producing electronics, clothing, machinery and much more. It joined the World Trade Organization in 2001 and passed Japan to become the second-largest economy in 2010. Hundreds of millions of people were lifted out of poverty. Its population began to shrink in 2022, and it is aging fast."],["Science and technology","China has built the world’s largest high-speed rail network, with more than 45,000 km of track. It has landed spacecraft on the Moon, including on the far side in 2019, and has its own space station, Tiangong. It leads the world in solar panels, electric vehicles and batteries."]],
sp:["The Great Wall","The Great Wall is not one wall but many, built and rebuilt over more than two thousand years to guard China’s northern frontier. The first emperor linked older walls in the 200s BCE, but most of what survives was built under the Ming dynasty, between the 1300s and 1600s, of brick and stone. A government survey measured all its sections at about 21,000 km. Contrary to a popular belief, the wall cannot be seen from space with the naked eye. UNESCO listed it as a World Heritage site in 1987."],
g:[["Dynasty","A line of rulers from one family"],["Han","China’s largest ethnic group"],["Pinyin","A system for writing Chinese sounds in Latin letters"],["Renminbi","China’s currency, also called the yuan"],["Special economic zone","A region with special rules to attract business, such as Shenzhen"],["Mandarin","China’s standard spoken language"]],
f:["China’s Yangtze is the longest river in Asia.","China’s Three Gorges Dam is the world’s largest hydroelectric power station.","China’s high-speed rail network is the largest in the world."],
fun:"China has the world’s longest high-speed rail network.",
tl:"Ang Tsina ay bansa sa Silangang Asya na may humigit-kumulang 1.41 bilyong tao. Isa ito sa pinakamatandang sibilisasyon at ngayon ay ikalawang pinakamalaking ekonomiya sa mundo."});
ADDART({id:"mexicocountry",t:"Mexico",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇲🇽",w:"Mexico",r:["aztec","maya","maize","choco","explor","usacountry"],
p:[["The largest Spanish-speaking country","Mexico, in southern North America, has about 130 million people, more than any other Spanish-speaking country. It borders the United States to the north and Guatemala and Belize to the south."],["Mountains and coasts","Mexico has coastlines on the Pacific Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico, high volcanic mountains, deserts in the north and tropical forest in the south."],["Ancient and modern","Mexico was home to the Olmec, Maya and Aztec civilizations, and its culture blends Indigenous and Spanish traditions."]],
l:"Mexico is a country in southern North America with about 130 million people, making it the most populous Spanish-speaking country in the world. Its capital, Mexico City, is one of the largest cities on Earth. Mexico was the home of great ancient civilizations, was ruled by Spain for 300 years, and today is a major economy, famous for its food, art and festivals.",
s:[["Land and climate","Two great mountain ranges, the Sierra Madre Occidental and the Sierra Madre Oriental, run down the country and enclose a central plateau. Mexico’s highest peak, Pico de Orizaba, a volcano, rises to 5,636 m. The north is dry, the center temperate and the south and the Yucatán Peninsula hot and humid. Earthquakes and volcanoes are common."],["Cities","Mexico City, built on the site of the Aztec capital, has about 9 million people in the city and more than 20 million in its metropolitan area. Guadalajara, Monterrey and Puebla are other major cities. Mexico City lies on an old lakebed, and parts of it are sinking."],["People and languages","Most Mexicans are mestizo, of mixed Indigenous and European ancestry. Spanish is the main language, and the government recognizes 68 Indigenous languages, including Nahuatl, the Aztec language, and several Maya languages."],["Government","Mexico is a federal republic of 31 states and Mexico City, with a president elected for a single six-year term. The Constitution of 1917, which came out of the Mexican Revolution, guarantees land reform, workers’ rights and free education."]],
k:[["Capital","Mexico City"],["Population","About 130 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.96 million km²"],["Highest point","Pico de Orizaba, 5,636 m"],["Currency","Mexican peso"]],
d:[["c. 1200 BCE","Olmec civilization flourishes"],["1325","Aztecs found Tenochtitlan"],["1521","Spanish conquer the Aztec Empire"],["1821","Independence from Spain"],["1910","Mexican Revolution begins"],["1917","Present constitution adopted"],["1994","North American Free Trade Agreement takes effect"]],
s2:[["Ancient civilizations","The Olmec, who flourished from about 1200 BCE, built Mexico’s first great cities and carved huge stone heads. Later came the Maya, with their temples, calendar and writing, and the city of Teotihuacan, with its Pyramids of the Sun and the Moon, which had well over 100,000 people. The Aztecs founded their capital, Tenochtitlan, in 1325."],["Conquest and New Spain","Hernán Cortés, helped by Indigenous allies who resented Aztec rule, took Tenochtitlan in 1521. Spain ruled Mexico as the Viceroyalty of New Spain for three hundred years. Silver mines made it rich, but diseases killed millions of Indigenous people, and a rigid class system favored those born in Spain."],["Independence","In 1810 the priest Miguel Hidalgo called for independence in the Grito de Dolores, still celebrated each 16 September. After eleven years of war Mexico became independent in 1821. In the 1840s it lost about half of its territory to the United States after the Mexican–American War."],["Revolution","In 1910 Mexicans rose against the long dictatorship of Porfirio Díaz. The revolution, with leaders such as Francisco Madero, Emiliano Zapata and Pancho Villa, lasted about a decade and killed hundreds of thousands. Out of it came the 1917 constitution and a new political order."],["Economy","Mexico is one of the 15 largest economies in the world. It makes cars, electronics and machinery, mostly for sale in the United States, with which it has a long border and a free-trade agreement. It is also a major exporter of oil, silver and farm goods, and the world’s largest producer of avocados."],["Art and food","Muralists Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros painted giant public works of history and politics, and Frida Kahlo became one of the most famous painters of the 1900s. UNESCO has honored traditional Mexican cuisine, based on maize, beans and chili, as intangible heritage since 2010."]],
sp:["The Day of the Dead","The Day of the Dead, observed on 1 and 2 November, is a Mexican celebration to remember family and friends who have died. Families build altars called ofrendas with photographs, candles, orange marigolds, sugar skulls and the favorite foods of the departed, and visit cemeteries to share meals and stories. The festival blends ancient Indigenous beliefs about death with the Catholic observance of All Saints and All Souls. It is a joyful and loving remembrance, not a Mexican Halloween. UNESCO recognized it as intangible cultural heritage in 2008."],
g:[["Mestizo","A person of mixed Indigenous and European descent"],["Conquistador","A Spanish conqueror of the Americas"],["Ejido","Land farmed communally under Mexico’s land reform"],["Mural","A painting made directly on a wall"],["Peso","Mexico’s currency"],["Cenote","A natural sinkhole filled with water, common in the Yucatán"]],
f:["Mexico City was built on the ruins of the Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan.","Mexico is the world’s largest producer of avocados.","Mexico recognizes 68 Indigenous languages."],
fun:"Mexico has more Spanish speakers than any other country.",
tl:"Ang Mexico ay bansa sa timog ng Hilagang Amerika na may humigit-kumulang 130 milyong tao. Ito ang may pinakamaraming nagsasalita ng Espanyol sa buong mundo at tahanan ng mga sibilisasyong Maya at Aztec."});

