ADDART({id:"francecountry",t:"France",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇫🇷",w:"France",r:["frrev","ww2","wwi","renais","impress","cinema"],
p:[["The largest country in the European Union","France, in western Europe, has about 68 million people and is the largest country in the European Union by area. Its capital is Paris."],["Coasts, mountains and countryside","France has coasts on the Atlantic and the Mediterranean, the Alps and Pyrenees mountains, and rolling farmland and vineyards in between."],["Culture and cuisine","France is famous for its art, fashion, wine and food, and is the most visited country in the world."]],
l:"France is a country in western Europe, with about 68 million people, and its capital is Paris. It has coasts on the Atlantic Ocean, the English Channel and the Mediterranean Sea, mountains in the east and south, and some of the world’s most productive farmland. France has long been a leader in art, science, food and fashion, and is one of the world’s most visited countries.",
s:[["Land and rivers","Mainland France is shaped roughly like a hexagon. Mont Blanc, on the Italian border at 4,805 m, is the highest peak in western Europe. The Seine flows through Paris, the Loire is the longest river, and the Rhône and Garonne drain the south. Overseas, France also includes places such as French Guiana, Guadeloupe and Réunion."],["Cities","Paris has about 2 million people in the city itself and some 12 million in its region. Marseille is the great Mediterranean port, Lyon is known for its food and silk history, and Toulouse for aircraft. The Eiffel Tower, the Louvre and Notre-Dame make Paris one of the most visited cities on Earth."],["Government","France has been a republic, in five versions, since 1792. The current Fifth Republic dates from 1958 and was designed by Charles de Gaulle. The president, elected for five years, shares power with a prime minister and a parliament of two houses."],["People and language","French is the national language and is spoken by about 300 million people worldwide, in Canada, Belgium, Switzerland and much of Africa. The Académie française, founded in 1635, guards the language. France is mostly Catholic by tradition, but it is officially secular and many French people have no religion."]],
k:[["Capital","Paris"],["Population","About 68 million (2024)"],["Area","About 551,000 km² on the mainland"],["Highest point","Mont Blanc, 4,805 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["58 BCE","Julius Caesar begins the conquest of Gaul"],["800","Charlemagne crowned emperor"],["1789","French Revolution begins"],["1804","Napoleon crowned emperor"],["1940","Germany occupies northern France"],["1957","France helps found the European Economic Community"],["1958","Fifth Republic founded"]],
s2:[["Gaul and the Franks","The ancient Gauls, a Celtic people, were conquered by Julius Caesar between 58 and 50 BCE, and the region became a Roman province. In the 400s the Franks, a Germanic people, took over and gave their name to France. Their king Clovis became Christian around 496, and Charlemagne, crowned emperor in 800, ruled much of western Europe."],["Medieval and royal France","The Hundred Years’ War with England, from 1337 to 1453, ended in French victory, helped by Joan of Arc, a peasant girl who led armies in the 1420s and was burned at the stake in 1431. Under Louis XIV, who reigned from 1643 to 1715, France was the strongest power in Europe, and his palace at Versailles was copied across the continent."],["Revolution and Napoleon","In 1789 the French Revolution overthrew the monarchy and proclaimed liberty, equality and fraternity. The general Napoleon Bonaparte took power in 1799, crowned himself emperor in 1804 and conquered much of Europe before his final defeat at Waterloo in 1815. His Civil Code, issued in 1804, still influences law in many countries."],["Wars and recovery","France suffered heavy losses in World War I and was occupied by Germany from 1940 to 1944 in World War II. After the war it rebuilt its economy, gave up most of its colonial empire, and with Germany became the core of the movement that created the European Union, beginning with the Treaty of Rome in 1957."],["Economy","France is the world’s seventh-largest economy. It is a leader in aircraft, with Airbus, in luxury goods and fashion, in tourism, and in nuclear power, which generates about two thirds of its electricity. It is also the largest farm producer in the European Union."],["Art and food","French painters such as Monet and Renoir launched Impressionism, and the Lumière brothers held the first public film screening in Paris in 1895. UNESCO has listed the French gastronomic meal as heritage, and France makes more than a thousand kinds of cheese. The Tour de France bicycle race has been held since 1903."]],
sp:["The Eiffel Tower","The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World’s Fair in Paris, held to mark the centenary of the French Revolution. It was designed by engineers at the company of Gustave Eiffel and put together from about 18,000 iron parts and 2.5 million rivets in just over two years. At 330 m with its antennas, it was the tallest structure in the world until 1930. It was meant to be taken down after twenty years but was kept because it was useful as a radio antenna. About 7 million people visit it every year."],
g:[["Gaul","The ancient land of the Celts that became France"],["Bastille","The Paris fortress prison stormed on 14 July 1789"],["Fifth Republic","France’s current system of government, since 1958"],["Haute couture","High-fashion clothing made to order"],["Napoleonic Code","The French legal code of 1804"],["Impressionism","A painting style that began in France in the 1870s"]],
f:["France is the most visited country in the world.","France makes more than a thousand kinds of cheese.","The Louvre in Paris is the world’s most visited art museum."],
fun:"France is the largest country in the European Union by area.",
tl:"Ang Pransya ay bansa sa kanlurang Europa na may humigit-kumulang 68 milyong tao. Kilala ito sa Paris, sa Eiffel Tower, sa sining, pagkain at moda, at ito ang pinakabinibisitang bansa sa mundo."});
ADDART({id:"russiacountry",t:"Russia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇷🇺",w:"Russia",r:["coldwar","ww2","space","frrev","novel","musicw"],
p:[["The largest country on Earth","Russia covers about 17 million square kilometers, more than a tenth of the world’s land, and spans eleven time zones from eastern Europe to the Pacific."],["Forests, steppe and tundra","Most of Russia is cold. Vast forests called taiga cover the center, treeless tundra lies in the north and steppe grassland in the south."],["A land of writers and composers","Russia has given the world authors such as Tolstoy and Dostoevsky, composers such as Tchaikovsky and a great ballet tradition."]],
l:"Russia is the largest country in the world, stretching across eastern Europe and northern Asia from the Baltic Sea to the Pacific Ocean. About 144 million people live there, most of them in the European west, and the capital is Moscow. Russia has vast reserves of oil, gas and minerals, a rich culture of literature and music, and a long and eventful history.",
s:[["Land and climate","The Ural Mountains divide Russia’s European part from Siberia. Europe’s longest river, the Volga, and its highest peak, Mount Elbrus at 5,642 m, are both in Russia. Winters are long and very cold, and in parts of Siberia the ground stays frozen all year, a condition called permafrost."],["Cities","Moscow, the capital, has about 13 million people and is the largest city in Europe. Saint Petersburg, built by Peter the Great from 1703, is famous for its palaces and canals. Novosibirsk, Yekaterinburg and Kazan are other major cities, and most Russians live in cities."],["People and language","Russian is the main language and is written in the Cyrillic alphabet, named after Saint Cyril, a ninth-century missionary. Russia is home to more than 190 ethnic groups, including Tatars, Ukrainians and Chechens. Russian Orthodox Christianity is the largest religion, and Islam the second."],["Government","Russia is a federal republic with a strong president, who is elected for six years, and a two-house parliament. It is divided into republics, regions and other federal subjects. Russia is one of the five permanent members of the UN Security Council."]],
k:[["Capital","Moscow"],["Population","About 144 million (2024)"],["Area","About 17.1 million km²"],["Highest point","Mount Elbrus, 5,642 m"],["Language","Russian"]],
d:[["988","Prince Vladimir adopts Christianity in Kyivan Rus"],["1240","Mongols conquer the Russian lands"],["1703","Peter the Great founds Saint Petersburg"],["1861","Serfdom abolished"],["1917","Revolutions end the tsarist system"],["1922","Soviet Union founded"],["1991","Soviet Union dissolves"]],
s2:[["Kyivan Rus and the Mongols","The first Russian state, Kyivan Rus, grew in the 800s around Kyiv and adopted Orthodox Christianity in 988. In 1240 the Mongols invaded and ruled for over two centuries. Moscow grew powerful as a collector of tribute for the Mongols and, by the late 1400s, threw off their rule."],["The tsars","Ivan IV, called the Terrible, was crowned the first tsar of Russia in 1547. Peter the Great, who ruled from 1682, modernized the army and the state and built Saint Petersburg, and Catherine the Great extended the empire in the late 1700s. Serfs, peasants bound to the land, were freed only in 1861."],["Revolution and the Soviet Union","Defeat in World War I and food shortages led to revolution in 1917, when the tsar was overthrown and the Bolsheviks under Vladimir Lenin took power. In 1922 they created the Soviet Union. Under Joseph Stalin, industry grew quickly, but millions died in famines, forced labor camps and political purges."],["The Second World War","Germany invaded the Soviet Union in 1941. The war, which Russians call the Great Patriotic War, killed an estimated 27 million Soviet people, more than any other country. Soviet forces took Berlin in 1945, and after the war the USSR became one of two superpowers in the Cold War with the United States."],["Space and science","The Soviet Union launched the first artificial satellite, Sputnik 1, in 1957, and in 1961 Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space. Russian scientists include Dmitri Mendeleev, who created the periodic table, and Ivan Pavlov, who studied conditioned reflexes. Russia still takes part in the International Space Station."],["After the Soviet Union","The Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991 into fifteen independent states, of which Russia was the largest. The 1990s were a time of economic hardship and rapid change, and the economy later grew strongly on exports of oil and gas. In February 2022 Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, and the United Nations General Assembly voted by a large majority to demand that it withdraw."]],
sp:["Lake Baikal","Lake Baikal, in southern Siberia, is the deepest lake in the world, reaching 1,642 m, and the oldest, at about 25 million years. It is about 636 km long and holds roughly a fifth of the world’s liquid surface fresh water, more than all the North American Great Lakes combined. Its water is so clear that a diver can see to a depth of 40 m. Baikal is home to more than 1,000 animal species found nowhere else, including the nerpa, the world’s only freshwater seal. UNESCO listed it as a World Heritage site in 1996."],
g:[["Tsar","A Russian emperor before 1917"],["Steppe","A vast grassland"],["Taiga","The northern forest of conifers"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen all year"],["Soviet Union","The communist state that existed from 1922 to 1991"],["Cyrillic","The alphabet used to write Russian"]],
f:["Russia spans eleven time zones.","Lake Baikal holds about a fifth of the world’s liquid surface fresh water.","The Trans-Siberian Railway is the longest railway line in the world."],
fun:"Russia is the largest country in the world by area.",
tl:"Ang Russia ay pinakamalaking bansa sa mundo at sumasaklaw sa labing-isang time zone. Kilala ito sa mga manunulat, kompositor at sa Lawa ng Baikal, ang pinakamalalim na lawa sa mundo."});
ADDART({id:"southafricacountry",t:"South Africa",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇿🇦",w:"South Africa",r:["nigeriacountry","trade","urban","soccer"],
p:[["At the southern tip of Africa","South Africa lies at the southern end of Africa, between the Atlantic and Indian oceans, and has about 64 million people. The small kingdom of Lesotho lies entirely within its borders."],["Three capitals","Uniquely, South Africa has three capital cities: Pretoria for the government, Cape Town for the parliament and Bloemfontein for the courts."],["The rainbow nation","South Africa has eleven spoken official languages, plus sign language, and many cultures. After the end of apartheid in the 1990s it was called the rainbow nation."]],
l:"South Africa is a country at the southern tip of Africa, with about 64 million people and eleven spoken official languages. It has coasts on two oceans, a high inland plateau rich in gold and diamonds, and a diverse population. South Africa is known for its wildlife, its mineral wealth and its history of apartheid, the system of racial segregation that ended in the 1990s under Nelson Mandela.",
s:[["Land and wildlife","Much of South Africa is a high plateau called the veld, ringed by mountains such as the Drakensberg, whose Mafadi peak, at 3,450 m, is the highest in the country. Table Mountain towers over Cape Town. Kruger National Park is one of Africa’s great wildlife reserves, home to lions, elephants and rhinoceroses, and the Cape region has one of the richest plant kingdoms on Earth."],["Cities","Johannesburg, built on gold, is the largest city, and its surrounding province, Gauteng, is the economic heart of the country. Cape Town is famous for its harbor and mountain, and Durban is a major port on the Indian Ocean. Pretoria is the administrative capital."],["People and languages","South Africa’s eleven spoken official languages include isiZulu, isiXhosa, Afrikaans, English and Sesotho, and since 2023 South African Sign Language is a twelfth official language. The majority of people are Black South Africans, including the Zulu and Xhosa, and there are also large communities of European, mixed and Asian descent. Most South Africans are Christian."],["Government","South Africa is a parliamentary republic with a president elected by the National Assembly. Its 1996 constitution, one of the world’s most progressive, guarantees equality and wide rights. Elections since 1994 have been held under universal suffrage."]],
k:[["Capitals","Pretoria, Cape Town and Bloemfontein"],["Population","About 64 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.22 million km²"],["Highest point","Mafadi, 3,450 m"],["Currency","Rand"]],
d:[["1652","Dutch settlement founded at the Cape"],["1886","Gold discovered on the Witwatersrand"],["1910","Union of South Africa formed"],["1948","National Party begins formal apartheid"],["1990","Nelson Mandela freed on 11 February"],["1994","First democratic election on 27 April"],["2010","South Africa hosts the football World Cup"]],
s2:[["First peoples and settlers","The Khoisan peoples lived in the south for tens of thousands of years, and Bantu-speaking farmers arrived from the north over the last 1,500 years. In 1652 the Dutch East India Company set up a supply station at the Cape, and settlers, later called Afrikaners or Boers, moved inland, taking land from African peoples."],["British rule and the mineral revolution","Britain took the Cape in 1806. Diamonds were found at Kimberley in 1867 and gold on the Witwatersrand in 1886, drawing thousands of workers and fortune seekers and starting the country’s industrial economy. Two wars between Britain and the Boer republics, the second from 1899 to 1902, ended in British victory."],["Apartheid","In 1948 the National Party made racial segregation law. Under apartheid, meaning apartness, people were classified by race, and Black South Africans were denied the vote, forced into separate areas and given inferior schooling. Protests such as the Sharpeville killings in 1960 and the Soweto uprising of 1976 were met with violence."],["The end of apartheid","Nelson Mandela and other leaders were imprisoned, and the African National Congress was banned. International sanctions and internal resistance eventually forced change. Mandela was freed on 11 February 1990, and in 1994 millions of South Africans of every race voted in the first democratic election, which made him president."],["Truth and reconciliation","The new government set up a Truth and Reconciliation Commission, led by Archbishop Desmond Tutu, in 1995. Victims described their suffering, and perpetrators who confessed fully could apply for amnesty. It aimed to heal the nation by uncovering the truth rather than seeking revenge."],["Economy and challenges","South Africa has Africa’s most industrialized economy, with mining of gold, platinum and coal, manufacturing, finance and wine. It is the world’s largest producer of platinum. Inequality remains among the highest in the world, unemployment is high, and power shortages in recent years have strained the economy."]],
sp:["Nelson Mandela","Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born in 1918 in a village in the Eastern Cape, and trained as a lawyer. He joined the African National Congress and led the struggle against apartheid, and in 1964 was sentenced to life imprisonment. He spent 27 years in prison, most of them on Robben Island, and was freed on 11 February 1990. He shared the 1993 Nobel Peace Prize with the president, F. W. de Klerk, and in 1994 became the first president elected in free elections, serving until 1999. He died on 5 December 2013, and his birthday, 18 July, is marked as Nelson Mandela International Day."],
g:[["Apartheid","South Africa’s system of legal racial segregation from 1948 to the early 1990s"],["Rainbow nation","A name for South Africa’s diverse, democratic society"],["Veld","South Africa’s open grassland"],["Springbok","A South African antelope and the national rugby team"],["Braai","A South African barbecue"],["Township","A residential area created for Black South Africans under apartheid"]],
f:["South Africa has three capital cities.","South Africa is the world’s largest producer of platinum.","The Springboks have won the men’s rugby World Cup four times."],
fun:"South Africa has eleven spoken official languages, plus a sign language.",
tl:"Ang Timog Aprika ay bansa sa katimugang dulo ng Aprika na may tatlong kabisera at labing-isang sinasalitang opisyal na wika. Kilala ito sa apartheid at kay Nelson Mandela, na naging unang pangulo na inihalal ng lahat ng lahi noong 1994."});
ADDART({id:"australiacountry",t:"Australia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🇦🇺",w:"Australia",r:["reef","pacific","cricket","swimming","explor"],
p:[["Country and continent","Australia is both a country and a continent. It is the sixth largest country in the world, with about 27 million people, most living near the coast."],["The outback and the coast","The center of Australia is a dry, thinly populated region called the outback. The Great Barrier Reef, the world’s largest coral reef system, lies off the northeast coast."],["Unique wildlife","Australia’s long isolation produced animals found nowhere else, such as kangaroos, koalas, wombats and the egg-laying platypus."]],
l:"Australia is a country and a continent in the Southern Hemisphere, between the Indian and Pacific oceans. About 27 million people live there, most in cities along the east and southeast coasts, while the vast interior, the outback, is very dry and sparsely settled. Australia is known for its unique wildlife, its Indigenous cultures, which are the oldest continuous cultures on Earth, and its high standard of living.",
s:[["Land and climate","Australia is the flattest and, after Antarctica, the driest inhabited continent. The Great Dividing Range runs down the east, and Mount Kosciuszko, at 2,228 m, is the highest peak on the mainland. The north is tropical, the center desert and the south and southeast temperate. Droughts, bushfires and floods are regular dangers."],["Cities","More than eight in ten Australians live in cities, mostly near the coast. Sydney, with its harbor and Opera House, and Melbourne each have more than 5 million people. Canberra, the capital, was designed in the early 1900s as a compromise between those two rival cities."],["People and languages","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for more than 60,000 years and speak hundreds of languages. English is the main language, and about three in ten Australians were born overseas, including large communities from England, India, China and New Zealand."],["Government","Australia is a federation of six states and two mainland territories, with a parliament modeled on the British one. The head of state is the British monarch, represented by a governor-general, and the prime minister leads the government. Voting is compulsory."]],
k:[["Capital","Canberra"],["Population","About 27 million (2024)"],["Area","About 7.69 million km²"],["Highest point","Mount Kosciuszko, 2,228 m"],["Currency","Australian dollar"]],
d:[["c. 65,000 years ago","Aboriginal peoples settle Australia"],["1770","James Cook claims the east coast for Britain"],["1788","First Fleet arrives at Sydney Cove on 26 January"],["1851","Gold rush begins"],["1901","Six colonies unite as the Commonwealth of Australia"],["1967","Referendum includes Aboriginal people in the census"],["2008","Government apologizes to the Stolen Generations"]],
s2:[["The First Australians","Aboriginal peoples reached Australia at least 60,000 years ago and developed hundreds of nations with their own languages and laws. They shaped the land with fire and passed down knowledge, art and stories, including the Dreaming, which explains how the world was made. Torres Strait Islanders live on the islands between Australia and New Guinea."],["British settlement","James Cook claimed the east coast for Britain in 1770. In January 1788 the First Fleet, carrying about 1,400 people, more than half of them convicts, landed at what became Sydney. Britain sent about 160,000 convicts over the next 80 years, while free settlers followed. The spread of settlement brought disease, violence and the loss of land for Aboriginal peoples."],["Gold and federation","Gold found in Victoria in 1851 brought a rush of immigrants and wealth. In 1901 the six colonies agreed to federate as the Commonwealth of Australia. Most women won the right to vote nationally in 1902, among the first in the world."],["War and nation","Thousands of Australians fought in World War I, and the failed landing at Gallipoli in 1915 became a defining national memory, marked each year on Anzac Day, 25 April. After World War II Australia welcomed millions of immigrants from Europe and later Asia, and it ended its restrictive immigration policy in the 1970s."],["Recognition and reconciliation","A 1967 referendum counted Aboriginal people in the census. In 1992 the High Court’s Mabo decision recognized native title, rejecting the idea that Australia had been empty land. In 2008 the prime minister apologized to the Stolen Generations, Aboriginal children who were removed from their families. A 2023 referendum on an Indigenous Voice to Parliament was rejected."],["Economy and nature","Australia is a leading exporter of iron ore, coal and natural gas, and its economy is among the 15 largest. It also exports wool, beef, wheat and wine. About 84 percent of its mammals are found nowhere else, and its universities draw students from across Asia. Protecting the Great Barrier Reef and other ecosystems from warming oceans is a major challenge."]],
sp:["Uluru","Uluru, a huge sandstone rock in the middle of Australia, rises 348 m above the plain and is about 9.4 km around, and much of it lies underground. It is sacred to the Anangu, the Aboriginal people of the area, who have lived there for tens of thousands of years and whose stories describe how its shape and caves were formed. Climbing Uluru was banned on 26 October 2019 out of respect for Anangu wishes. Uluru-Kata Tjuta National Park, which includes the rock, was listed as a World Heritage site in 1987 and is jointly managed by the Anangu."],
g:[["Outback","The vast, dry, thinly populated interior of Australia"],["Marsupial","A mammal whose young develop in a pouch"],["Dreaming","Aboriginal accounts of creation and law, also called Dreamtime"],["Billabong","A pond left behind by a river"],["Federation","The 1901 union of the six colonies into one nation"],["Native title","Recognition of Indigenous peoples’ rights to land"]],
f:["Australia is the only country that is also a continent.","Australia has more kangaroos than people.","Australia’s Aboriginal cultures are among the oldest continuous cultures on Earth."],
fun:"Australia is both a country and a continent.",
tl:"Ang Australia ay bansa at kontinente sa Katimugang Hemispero na may humigit-kumulang 27 milyong tao. Kilala ito sa Great Barrier Reef, sa Uluru, sa mga kangaroo at sa mga katutubong Aboriginal na may pinakamatandang patuloy na kultura sa daigdig."});

