ADDART({id:"ukcountry",t:"United Kingdom",c:"Countries and Places",i:"💂",w:"United Kingdom",r:["indrev","english","shakes","ww2","newtonp","beatles"],
p:[["Four nations","The United Kingdom joins four nations, England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland, under one government and monarch."],["Islands in the Atlantic","It occupies the island of Great Britain, the northeast of Ireland and many smaller islands off northwestern Europe."],["A global influence","Britain was the birthplace of the Industrial Revolution and once ruled the largest empire in history, spreading the English language worldwide."]],
l:"The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland is an island country off the northwest coast of Europe. About 69 million people live there, most of them in England, and its capital, London, is one of the world’s great centers of finance, culture and learning. A constitutional monarchy with one of the oldest parliaments, it led the Industrial Revolution and built a vast empire whose legacy, including the English language, reaches every continent.",
s:[["Land and climate","Great Britain has rolling lowlands in the south and east and rugged uplands in Scotland, Wales and northern England. Ben Nevis in Scotland, at 1,345 m, is the highest peak. Warm currents from the Atlantic give the islands a mild, damp climate, with cool summers and few extremes."],["Four nations","England is the largest and most populous nation, with about 84 percent of the people. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have their own parliaments or assemblies, created in 1998 and 1999, which run matters such as health and education. Each has its own traditions, and Welsh and Scottish Gaelic are spoken alongside English."],["Government","The UK is a constitutional monarchy: the king or queen is head of state, but power lies with Parliament, made up of the elected House of Commons and the appointed House of Lords. The prime minister leads the government. Britain has no single written constitution; its rules come from laws, court decisions and long tradition."],["Cities and people","London, with about 9 million people, is one of the world’s most diverse cities, where hundreds of languages are spoken. Other major cities include Birmingham, Manchester, Glasgow, Edinburgh, Cardiff and Belfast. Migration from former colonies and from Europe has made modern Britain multicultural."]],
k:[["Capital","London"],["Population","About 69 million (2024)"],["Area","About 243,000 km²"],["Highest point","Ben Nevis, 1,345 m"],["Currency","Pound sterling"]],
d:[["1066","The Normans conquer England"],["1215","Magna Carta limits the king’s power"],["1707","England and Scotland unite as Great Britain"],["1760s","The Industrial Revolution begins"],["1940","The Battle of Britain"],["1948","The National Health Service is founded"],["2020","The UK leaves the European Union"]],
s2:[["From Romans to Normans","Romans ruled much of Britain from 43 CE to about 410. Anglo-Saxon kingdoms followed, and Viking raiders settled in the north and east. In 1066 William of Normandy won the Battle of Hastings and became king of England, bringing French language and customs that reshaped English."],["Parliament and rights","In 1215 rebel barons forced King John to seal Magna Carta, which said even the king must obey the law. Parliament grew in power over the centuries, and after a civil war in the 1640s and the Glorious Revolution of 1688, the monarch had to rule with it. These ideas influenced democracies worldwide."],["Workshop of the world","From the 1760s, inventions such as the steam engine, spinning machines and iron-making transformed Britain into the first industrial nation. Factories, canals and railways spread, and cities such as Manchester and Birmingham boomed. By 1850 Britain produced about half the world’s iron and cotton cloth."],["Empire and Commonwealth","At its height around 1920, the British Empire covered about a quarter of the world’s land and people, from Canada and India to Australia and much of Africa. It brought trade and railways but also conquest, exploitation and the Atlantic slave trade, abolished in 1807. After 1945 most colonies became independent, and many joined the Commonwealth of Nations."],["The world wars","Britain fought in both world wars. In 1940, after France fell, it stood almost alone against Nazi Germany, and the Royal Air Force won the Battle of Britain. Under Winston Churchill it fought on until victory in 1945, though the wars left it exhausted and in debt."],["Culture and sport","Britain has given the world William Shakespeare, Jane Austen, the Beatles and Harry Potter, and its universities, Oxford and Cambridge, are among the oldest in the world. Football, rugby, cricket, tennis and golf were codified in Britain in the 1800s and spread around the globe."]],
sp:["The National Health Service","In 1948 Britain launched the National Health Service, offering medical care to everyone, free at the point of use and paid for by taxes. Its founder, health minister Aneurin Bevan, wanted care based on need, not wealth. The NHS became one of the world’s largest employers and a model studied by many countries, and it remains deeply loved by Britons, despite long waiting lists and constant debate about its funding."],
g:[["Constitutional monarchy","A system in which a king or queen reigns but an elected parliament governs"],["Magna Carta","A charter of 1215 that limited the English king’s power"],["Commonwealth","An association of 56 countries, most of them former British colonies"],["Devolution","Handing some powers from a central government to regions"],["Industrial Revolution","The shift from hand production to machines and factories"]],
f:["Nowhere in the United Kingdom is more than about 115 km from the sea.","London’s Underground, opened in 1863, was the world’s first underground railway.","The UK has had a woman as monarch for about 130 of the last 200 years."],
fun:"London’s Underground was the world’s first underground railway, opened in 1863.",
tl:"Ang United Kingdom ay bansang pulo sa hilagang-kanluran ng Europa na binubuo ng Inglatera, Scotland, Wales at Hilagang Ireland. Dito nagsimula ang Rebolusyong Industriyal at ang wikang Ingles."});

ADDART({id:"italycountry",t:"Italy",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🍕",w:"Italy",r:["rome","renais","davinci","michel","opera","volc"],
p:[["The boot of Europe","Italy is a boot-shaped peninsula reaching into the Mediterranean Sea, with the large islands of Sicily and Sardinia."],["Cradle of civilizations","Ancient Rome and the Renaissance both began in Italy, which has more UNESCO World Heritage Sites than almost any other country."],["Art, food and style","Italy is famous for its art, architecture, opera, fashion, cars and cooking, from pasta and pizza to espresso."]],
l:"Italy is a country in southern Europe made up of a long peninsula shaped like a boot and the islands of Sicily and Sardinia. About 59 million people live there. Its capital, Rome, was the heart of the Roman Empire and is home to Vatican City, center of the Catholic Church. Birthplace of the Renaissance, Italy has given the world extraordinary art, music and food, and today it is one of Europe’s largest economies.",
s:[["Land","The Alps form Italy’s northern border, and the Apennine Mountains run down the peninsula like a spine. Between them lies the fertile Po Valley, the country’s farming and industrial heartland. Italy has active volcanoes, including Vesuvius near Naples, Etna on Sicily and Stromboli, and frequent earthquakes."],["Regions and cities","Italy has 20 regions, each with its own dialects, dishes and traditions. Rome is the capital, Milan the center of finance and fashion, Turin of carmaking, and Naples a busy southern port. Venice, built on more than 100 islands in a lagoon, and Florence, the city of the Renaissance, draw millions of visitors."],["North and south","Italy was united only in 1861, and differences remain. The industrial north is wealthier, while the south, called the Mezzogiorno, has higher unemployment. Many southern Italians moved north or emigrated, to the Americas and Australia, in the 1800s and 1900s."],["Religion and language","Most Italians are Roman Catholic, and the pope lives in Vatican City, an independent state inside Rome. Italian grew from the Tuscan dialect of Florence, the language of Dante’s Divine Comedy, and became the national standard after unification."]],
k:[["Capital","Rome"],["Population","About 59 million (2024)"],["Area","About 301,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mont Blanc (Monte Bianco), about 4,806 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["753 BCE","Traditional date of the founding of Rome"],["79 CE","Vesuvius buries Pompeii"],["1300s","The Renaissance begins in Florence"],["1861","The Kingdom of Italy is proclaimed"],["1922","Mussolini’s Fascists take power"],["1946","Italians vote to become a republic"],["1957","Italy helps found the European Economic Community"]],
s2:[["Ancient Rome","From a small city by the Tiber, Rome grew into an empire that by 117 CE stretched from Britain to Mesopotamia. Romans built roads, aqueducts and cities, and their language, Latin, and their laws shaped much of Europe. Ruins such as the Colosseum and the Forum still stand in the heart of Rome."],["Cities and the Renaissance","In the Middle Ages, Italian city-states such as Venice, Genoa and Florence grew rich from trade and banking. In the 1300s to 1500s, they led the Renaissance, a rebirth of art and learning. Painters and sculptors such as Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo, Raphael and Botticelli worked for wealthy patrons such as the Medici family."],["Unification","For centuries Italy was divided among small states and foreign powers. In the movement called the Risorgimento, led by figures such as Giuseppe Garibaldi, Camillo Cavour and Giuseppe Mazzini, Italy was united as a kingdom in 1861, and Rome became its capital in 1871."],["Fascism and war","In 1922 Benito Mussolini’s Fascist party took power and created a dictatorship. Italy allied with Nazi Germany and entered World War II in 1940. Mussolini was overthrown in 1943, and Italian partisans fought the German occupation. In 1946 Italians voted to abolish the monarchy and become a republic."],["Economic miracle","After the war Italy rebuilt quickly. In the 1950s and 1960s, its economic miracle turned it into an industrial power known for cars such as Fiat and Ferrari, design, fashion houses in Milan and machinery. Italy was a founding member of the European Economic Community in 1957 and adopted the euro in 1999."],["Food and culture","Italian food varies by region, from the risottos of the north to the pizzas of Naples and the seafood of Sicily. Italian cooking, along with Mediterranean diets, is admired worldwide. Italy gave the world opera, through composers such as Verdi and Puccini, and its films, by directors such as Federico Fellini, shaped modern cinema."]],
sp:["Venice, the floating city","Venice was built from the 400s CE on more than 100 small islands in a lagoon, on millions of wooden piles driven into the mud. Canals serve as streets, crossed by about 400 bridges. For centuries the Republic of Venice was a great sea power, trading with the Byzantine and Ottoman empires. Today rising seas and floods, called acqua alta, threaten the city, and huge mobile flood barriers, called MOSE, began operating in 2020."],
g:[["Peninsula","Land surrounded by water on three sides"],["Renaissance","A rebirth of art and learning in Europe from the 1300s to 1500s"],["Risorgimento","The movement that united Italy in the 1800s"],["Fascism","A dictatorship based on extreme nationalism and the power of one leader"],["City-state","An independent city that governs its own territory"]],
f:["Italy and China have the most UNESCO World Heritage Sites of any countries.","Two independent countries, Vatican City and San Marino, lie entirely within Italy.","Pizza Margherita is said to be named after Queen Margherita of Italy, in 1889."],
fun:"Two whole countries, Vatican City and San Marino, lie inside Italy.",
tl:"Ang Italya ay bansang hugis-bota sa timog Europa. Dito nagsimula ang Imperyong Romano at ang Renasimyento, at kilala ito sa sining, opera, moda at pagkain gaya ng pasta at pizza."});

ADDART({id:"spaincountry",t:"Spain",c:"Countries and Places",i:"💃",w:"Spain",r:["spanish","explor","columbus","picasso","soccer","islam"],
p:[["The Iberian Peninsula","Spain covers most of the Iberian Peninsula in southwestern Europe, as well as the Balearic and Canary Islands and two cities in North Africa."],["A meeting of cultures","Romans, Visigoths, Muslims and Christians all shaped Spain, leaving Roman bridges, Moorish palaces and Gothic cathedrals."],["Spanish around the world","Spain’s empire carried the Spanish language to the Americas and beyond, making it one of the most widely spoken languages on Earth."]],
l:"Spain is a country in southwestern Europe that covers most of the Iberian Peninsula, along with the Balearic Islands in the Mediterranean, the Canary Islands in the Atlantic and the cities of Ceuta and Melilla in North Africa. About 49 million people live there, and its capital is Madrid. Once the center of a global empire, Spain is today a democratic monarchy famous for its art, architecture, festivals, food and football.",
s:[["Land and climate","Much of Spain is a high central plateau, the Meseta, ringed by mountains, including the Pyrenees on the French border and the Sierra Nevada in the south. The interior has hot summers and cold winters, the Mediterranean coast is warm and sunny, and the green north is rainier. The highest point is Mount Teide on Tenerife in the Canary Islands."],["Regions and languages","Spain has 17 autonomous communities, each with its own government. Spanish, or Castilian, is spoken everywhere, while Catalan, Galician and Basque are official in their regions. Catalonia and the Basque Country have strong regional identities, and some people there want independence."],["Cities","Madrid, in the center, is the capital and largest city. Barcelona, on the Mediterranean, is famous for Antoni Gaudí’s buildings, including the still-unfinished Sagrada Família. Seville, Granada and Córdoba in Andalusia hold great monuments of Islamic Spain, and Valencia is known for paella."],["Economy","Spain has one of Europe’s largest economies. Tourism is huge: more than 90 million foreign visitors came in 2024, among the most of any country. Spain is also a leading producer of olive oil, wine, fruit and vegetables, cars and renewable energy from wind and sun."]],
k:[["Capital","Madrid"],["Population","About 49 million (2024)"],["Area","About 506,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Teide, 3,715 m"],["Currency","Euro"]],
d:[["711","Muslim armies cross from North Africa and conquer most of Iberia"],["1492","Granada falls; Columbus reaches the Americas"],["1588","The Spanish Armada is defeated by England"],["1936","The Spanish Civil War begins"],["1975","Franco dies; the transition to democracy begins"],["1978","A democratic constitution is approved"],["1986","Spain joins the European Community"]],
s2:[["Romans and Visigoths","Romans conquered Iberia over two centuries and ruled it for about 600 years, building roads, aqueducts such as the one at Segovia, and cities. Spain gave Rome the emperor Trajan, born near Seville. After Rome fell, the Visigoths, a Germanic people, ruled from Toledo."],["Al-Andalus","In 711 Muslim forces from North Africa conquered most of the peninsula, which they called al-Andalus. Córdoba became one of the largest and most learned cities in Europe, where Muslims, Christians and Jews lived and worked. The Alhambra palace in Granada and the great mosque of Córdoba survive from this time."],["Reconquest and unity","Christian kingdoms in the north slowly pushed south over centuries. The marriage of Isabella of Castile and Ferdinand of Aragon in 1469 united much of Spain, and in 1492 they captured Granada, the last Muslim state. That same year they expelled Spain’s Jews and sponsored Columbus’s voyage across the Atlantic."],["A global empire","In the 1500s and 1600s, Spain built an empire stretching from Mexico and Peru to the Philippines, and silver from the Americas made it Europe’s greatest power. This was Spain’s Golden Age of art and writing, of El Greco, Velázquez and Cervantes’s Don Quixote. Conquest brought devastation to Indigenous peoples. Most American colonies won independence in the early 1800s."],["Civil war and dictatorship","In 1936 a military revolt led by General Francisco Franco against the elected Republic started the Spanish Civil War, in which hundreds of thousands died. Franco won in 1939 and ruled as dictator until his death in 1975. King Juan Carlos then helped lead a peaceful transition to democracy, with a new constitution in 1978."],["Culture and festivals","Spanish culture includes flamenco music and dance from Andalusia, painters from Goya to Picasso and Dalí, and the architecture of Gaudí. Festivals such as Seville’s Holy Week, Pamplona’s San Fermín and Valencia’s Fallas draw huge crowds. Spaniards often eat late, and tapas, small shared dishes, are a national tradition. Football clubs Real Madrid and Barcelona are among the most famous in the world, the men’s national team won the World Cup in 2010 and 2026, and the women’s team won it in 2023."]],
sp:["The Sagrada Família","In 1883 the architect Antoni Gaudí took over the design of a church in Barcelona and spent the rest of his life on it, creating towers like stretched sandcastles and a forest of tree-like columns inside. When he died in 1926, it was barely a quarter finished. Funded by donations and tickets, work has continued for more than 140 years, and its tallest tower, dedicated to Jesus, is planned to make it the tallest church in the world."],
g:[["Iberian Peninsula","The peninsula in southwestern Europe shared by Spain and Portugal"],["Al-Andalus","The Arabic name for Muslim-ruled Iberia"],["Reconquista","The centuries-long Christian reconquest of Iberia"],["Autonomous community","One of Spain’s 17 self-governing regions"],["Flamenco","A passionate song, guitar and dance tradition from Andalusia"]],
f:["Spain produces about 40 percent of the world’s olive oil.","Spanish is the official language of 20 countries.","The Canary Islands are closer to Africa than to mainland Spain."],
fun:"Spain produces about 40 percent of the world’s olive oil.",
tl:"Ang Espanya ay bansa sa timog-kanlurang Europa na sumasakop sa halos buong Tangway ng Iberia. Dati itong may malawak na imperyo na nagdala ng wikang Espanyol sa Amerika at iba pang bahagi ng mundo."});

