ADDART({id:"polandcountry",t:"Poland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏰",w:"Poland",r:["ww2","holocaust","coldwar","ussrfall","curie","copern"],
p:[["Heart of Europe","Poland lies on the North European Plain, between Germany to the west and Ukraine, Belarus and Lithuania to the east, with a coast on the Baltic Sea."],["A nation that endured","Poland was divided by its neighbors and vanished from the map for 123 years, then was devastated in World War II, yet its people kept their language and culture alive."],["Solidarity","In 1980 Polish workers founded Solidarity, a trade union that grew into a movement and helped bring down communism across Eastern Europe."]],
l:"Poland is a country in Central Europe, stretching from the Baltic Sea in the north to the Carpathian Mountains in the south. About 37 million people live there, and its capital is Warsaw. Poland has a long and dramatic history: a great power in the 1500s, it was partitioned by its neighbors, suffered terribly in World War II and lived under communist rule until 1989. Since then it has become a democracy and one of Europe’s fastest-growing economies.",
s:[["Land","Most of Poland is flat lowland, part of the great North European Plain, which made it easy for armies to cross. In the north are the Baltic coast and thousands of lakes in Masuria. In the south rise the Sudetes and the Tatra Mountains, part of the Carpathians, where Rysy is the highest peak. The Vistula, Poland’s longest river, flows through Kraków and Warsaw to the sea."],["Cities","Warsaw, the capital and largest city, was almost completely destroyed in World War II and rebuilt, including its old town, now a UNESCO site. Kraków, the royal capital for centuries, has a medieval market square and the Wawel Castle. Other major cities include Łódź, Wrocław, Poznań and the port of Gdańsk."],["People and faith","Almost all Poles speak Polish, a Slavic language written in the Latin alphabet. The great majority are Roman Catholic, and the Church played a major role in keeping national identity alive under foreign rule and communism. Karol Wojtyła, archbishop of Kraków, became Pope John Paul II in 1978."],["Economy","Since 1989 Poland has changed from a planned economy to a market economy, and joined the European Union in 2004. It grew steadily for three decades, avoiding recession even in 2009. Poland makes cars, furniture, electronics and food products and is a major farming country, growing potatoes, apples and grain."]],
k:[["Capital","Warsaw"],["Population","About 37 million (2024)"],["Area","About 313,000 km²"],["Highest point","Rysy, 2,499 m"],["Currency","Polish złoty"]],
d:[["966","Mieszko I is baptized, traditionally the founding of the Polish state"],["1569","The Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth is formed"],["1795","The third partition removes Poland from the map"],["1918","Poland regains independence"],["1939","Germany and the Soviet Union invade, starting World War II"],["1980","The Solidarity trade union is founded"],["2004","Poland joins the European Union"]],
s2:[["A great commonwealth","In 1569 Poland and Lithuania joined to form the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, one of the largest states in Europe. Its nobles elected their kings and had a parliament, the Sejm, with great power. In 1791 it adopted a constitution, one of the first modern written constitutions in the world, after that of the United States."],["The partitions","Weakened by wars and quarrels among its nobles, the Commonwealth was carved up by Russia, Prussia and Austria in three partitions, in 1772, 1793 and 1795. For 123 years Poland did not exist as a state. Poles rose up again and again, and many emigrated, but they kept their language, faith and culture alive."],["Independence and invasion","Poland regained independence in 1918 at the end of World War I. On 1 September 1939 Nazi Germany invaded, starting World War II, and the Soviet Union invaded from the east weeks later. About 6 million Polish citizens were killed, half of them Jews murdered in the Holocaust, many in camps the Nazis built in occupied Poland, such as Auschwitz."],["Communist Poland","After the war, Poland’s borders were moved west, and it became a communist state under Soviet control. Workers protested repeatedly. In 1980 shipyard workers in Gdańsk, led by the electrician Lech Wałęsa, won the right to form Solidarity, the first independent trade union in the Soviet bloc. It was banned under martial law in 1981 but survived underground."],["Freedom and Europe","In 1989 round-table talks between the government and Solidarity led to partly free elections, which Solidarity won overwhelmingly. Poland became the first country in the Soviet bloc to end communist rule, setting off a wave of change across Eastern Europe. It joined NATO in 1999 and the European Union in 2004."],["Science and culture","Poland gave the world the astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus, who placed the Sun at the center of the solar system, and Marie Skłodowska-Curie, the first person to win two Nobel Prizes. The composer Frédéric Chopin wrote piano music inspired by Polish dances, and Poland’s writers have won five Nobel Prizes in Literature."]],
sp:["Rebuilding Warsaw","By January 1945 about 85 percent of Warsaw’s old center lay in ruins, deliberately destroyed by German forces after the Warsaw Uprising of 1944. After the war, Poles rebuilt the old town brick by brick, using old paintings by the Venetian artist Bernardo Bellotto, known as Canaletto, and architects’ drawings and photographs as guides. In 1980 the rebuilt old town became a UNESCO World Heritage Site, a symbol of a nation’s will to survive."],
g:[["Partition","The division of a country’s territory among other states"],["Commonwealth","Here, the union of Poland and Lithuania from 1569 to 1795"],["Solidarity","The Polish independent trade union founded in 1980"],["Sejm","The lower house of the Polish parliament"],["Communism","A system in which the state controls the economy under a single party"]],
f:["Poland’s constitution of 1791 was one of the first modern written constitutions.","Marie Curie, born in Warsaw, named the element polonium after Poland.","The Wieliczka Salt Mine near Kraków has chapels carved entirely out of salt."],
fun:"Marie Curie named the element polonium after her homeland, Poland.",
tl:"Ang Poland ay bansa sa Gitnang Europa sa baybayin ng Dagat Baltic. Ilang beses itong nawala sa mapa at nawasak sa Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig, ngunit muling bumangon at naging demokrasya noong 1989."});

ADDART({id:"canadacountry",t:"Canada",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🍁",w:"Canada",r:["arctic","trees","english","wolf","bear","usacountry"],
p:[["A giant land","Canada is the second-largest country in the world by area, stretching from the Atlantic to the Pacific and north into the Arctic Ocean."],["Few people, vast wilderness","Most of its 41 million people live within about 200 kilometers of the border with the United States, leaving huge forests, lakes and tundra almost empty."],["Two languages, many peoples","Canada is officially bilingual in English and French, home to many First Nations, Inuit and Métis peoples and to immigrants from around the world."]],
l:"Canada is a country in North America, the second largest in the world after Russia. It stretches about 5,500 kilometers from the Atlantic to the Pacific and from the United States border into the Arctic. About 41 million people live there, mostly in cities in the south, and its capital is Ottawa. A federation of ten provinces and three territories, Canada is a parliamentary democracy known for its natural beauty, its two official languages and its welcome to immigrants.",
s:[["Land","Canada has towering Rocky Mountains in the west, vast prairies in the middle and the ancient rocks of the Canadian Shield, dotted with lakes, around Hudson Bay. It has more lakes than any other country, and its forests, the boreal forest, are among the largest on Earth. In the far north, the Arctic islands are covered in tundra and ice."],["Climate","Winters are long and cold in most of Canada, especially in the interior and the north, where temperatures can fall below −40 °C. The Pacific coast around Vancouver is milder and rainy. Summers can be warm and sunny, even hot in the south."],["Provinces and cities","The ten provinces include Ontario and Quebec, where most Canadians live, British Columbia on the Pacific and the Atlantic provinces. The three northern territories are huge but have few people. Toronto is the largest city, followed by Montreal, the largest French-speaking city in the Americas, and Vancouver."],["Government","Canada is a constitutional monarchy and a parliamentary democracy. The British monarch is the head of state, but the elected prime minister leads the government. Power is shared between the federal government in Ottawa and the provinces, which run health care and education."]],
k:[["Capital","Ottawa"],["Population","About 41 million (2024)"],["Area","About 9.98 million km²"],["Highest point","Mount Logan, 5,959 m"],["Currency","Canadian dollar"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Norse sailors settle briefly at L’Anse aux Meadows, Newfoundland"],["1608","Samuel de Champlain founds Quebec City"],["1763","France gives up New France to Britain"],["1867","Confederation creates the Dominion of Canada"],["1885","The transcontinental railway is completed"],["1982","Canada gains full control of its constitution"],["1999","The territory of Nunavut is created"]],
s2:[["First peoples","People have lived in what is now Canada for at least 12,000 years. First Nations developed many cultures, from the salmon-fishing peoples of the Pacific coast with their totem poles to the buffalo hunters of the plains and the farming Haudenosaunee near the Great Lakes. The Inuit adapted to life in the Arctic, building kayaks, igloos and dog sleds."],["New France and British North America","French explorers and fur traders arrived in the 1500s and 1600s, founding Quebec in 1608 and building New France along the St. Lawrence River. Britain took control after the Seven Years’ War in 1763 but allowed French Canadians to keep their language, religion and civil law. Loyalists fleeing the American Revolution added English-speaking settlers."],["Building a nation","In 1867 three British colonies united as the Dominion of Canada, and other provinces joined later. A railway across the continent, completed in 1885, linked the country. Canada gradually gained independence from Britain without a revolution, finally bringing its constitution home in 1982 with a Charter of Rights and Freedoms."],["Indigenous peoples today","About one in twenty Canadians is First Nations, Inuit or Métis. For more than a century, many Indigenous children were taken to residential schools designed to erase their languages and cultures, where many suffered abuse. A Truth and Reconciliation Commission reported in 2015, and Canada is working, unevenly, toward reconciliation. In 1999 the Inuit territory of Nunavut was created."],["A nation of immigrants","About one in four people in Canada was born in another country, among the highest shares in the world. Immigrants come especially from India, China, the Philippines and other parts of Asia, Africa and Latin America. Canada adopted an official policy of multiculturalism in 1971, encouraging people to keep their heritage while sharing Canadian life."],["Resources and economy","Canada is rich in natural resources: oil, natural gas, minerals, timber, fresh water and farmland. It is one of the world’s largest producers of oil, uranium, potash and wheat, and it trades closely with the United States. Its cities are also centers of banking, technology, film and artificial intelligence research."]],
sp:["The longest coastline","Canada has the longest coastline of any country, more than 200,000 kilometers when all its islands are counted, touching three oceans: the Atlantic, the Pacific and the Arctic. Its border with the United States, about 8,890 kilometers long, is the world’s longest international border. Canada also holds about a fifth of the world’s fresh water in its lakes, rivers and glaciers."],
g:[["Province","One of Canada’s ten main self-governing regions"],["First Nations","Indigenous peoples of Canada other than the Inuit and Métis"],["Inuit","Indigenous peoples of the Arctic"],["Boreal forest","The great northern forest of conifers"],["Confederation","The union of colonies that formed Canada in 1867"]],
f:["Canada has more lakes than all other countries combined.","Canada’s border with the United States is the longest in the world.","Basketball was invented by a Canadian, James Naismith."],
fun:"Canada has more lakes than the rest of the world combined.",
tl:"Ang Canada ay ang ikalawang pinakamalaking bansa sa mundo, sa hilagang Amerika. Opisyal nitong wika ang Ingles at Pranses, at kilala ito sa malalawak na kagubatan, lawa at magiliw na pagtanggap sa mga dayuhan."});

ADDART({id:"argentinacountry",t:"Argentina",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐎",w:"Argentina",r:["andes","soccer","spanish","explor","decol","antarc"],
p:[["From the Andes to the sea","Argentina stretches about 3,700 kilometers from the tropics to the southern tip of South America, between the Andes and the Atlantic."],["The pampas","Its vast grasslands, the pampas, are some of the world’s richest farmland, raising cattle, wheat and soybeans."],["Tango and football","Argentina is famous for the tango, its beef and its passion for football, with three World Cup titles."]],
l:"Argentina is the second-largest country in South America, covering much of the continent’s southern cone between the Andes Mountains and the Atlantic Ocean. About 46 million people live there, more than a third of them in and around the capital, Buenos Aires. A former Spanish colony that became one of the world’s richest countries around 1900, Argentina is known for its pampas, its beef, the tango and its footballers, from Diego Maradona to Lionel Messi.",
s:[["Land","In the west, the Andes rise to Aconcagua, at 6,961 m the highest mountain outside Asia. The center is covered by the pampas, flat, fertile grasslands, while the north has hot plains and forests and the Iguazú Falls on the border with Brazil. In the south, Patagonia is a windswept land of steppes, glaciers and lakes, ending at Tierra del Fuego."],["Climate","Because Argentina is so long, its climate ranges from subtropical in the north to cold in the far south, near Antarctica. Buenos Aires has hot, humid summers and mild winters. Seasons are the reverse of the Northern Hemisphere’s, so Christmas falls in summer."],["People","Most Argentines are descended from European immigrants, especially from Italy and Spain, who arrived in huge numbers between about 1870 and 1930. There are also Indigenous peoples and immigrants from neighboring countries. Spanish is the national language, spoken with a distinctive accent influenced by Italian."],["Economy","The pampas make Argentina one of the world’s great food exporters, of beef, soybeans, maize, wheat and wine. It also has large reserves of lithium, oil and shale gas. But its economy has gone through many crises, with periods of very high inflation, debt defaults and sharp swings between growth and recession."]],
k:[["Capital","Buenos Aires"],["Population","About 46 million (2024)"],["Area","About 2.78 million km²"],["Highest point","Aconcagua, 6,961 m"],["Currency","Argentine peso"]],
d:[["1536","Spanish settlers first found Buenos Aires"],["1816","Argentina declares independence from Spain"],["1946","Juan Perón becomes president"],["1976","A military junta seizes power"],["1982","Argentina fights Britain over the Falkland Islands"],["1983","Democracy is restored"],["2022","Argentina wins its third FIFA World Cup"]],
s2:[["Colony and independence","Spanish explorers arrived in the 1500s, and the region became part of the Viceroyalty of Peru, then from 1776 the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata, ruled from Buenos Aires. Argentina declared independence on 9 July 1816. General José de San Martín led an army across the Andes to help free Chile and Peru, and he is honored as a national hero."],["Gauchos and the pampas","On the pampas, gauchos, skilled horsemen and cowboys, herded wild cattle in the 1700s and 1800s. They became symbols of Argentine freedom and appear in the national epic poem Martín Fierro. Railways, fencing and refrigerated ships turned the pampas into a giant farm that fed Europe."],["Immigration and wealth","Between about 1870 and 1930, millions of Europeans, especially Italians and Spaniards, moved to Argentina. Grain and beef exports made it one of the ten richest countries in the world around 1900, and Buenos Aires grew into a grand city of boulevards, cafés and theaters, nicknamed the Paris of South America."],["Perón and the military","Juan Perón, president from 1946 to 1955 and again in 1973 to 1974, won strong support from workers, and his wife Eva Perón, known as Evita, became an icon. Political conflict led to repeated military coups. From 1976 to 1983, a military junta ruled with terror: up to 30,000 people disappeared, kidnapped and killed. The Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo marched every week demanding to know their children’s fate."],["War and democracy","In 1982 the junta invaded the Falkland Islands, which Argentina calls the Malvinas, a British territory it claims. Britain retook them after a 10-week war. The defeat brought down the junta, and democracy returned in 1983. Leaders of the junta were later put on trial, a landmark for human rights in Latin America."],["Culture","The tango was born in the poor neighborhoods of Buenos Aires around 1900, blending African, European and local rhythms, and became a worldwide dance. Argentine writers include Jorge Luis Borges and Julio Cortázar. Football is a passion, and Argentina has won the World Cup in 1978, 1986 and 2022. Pope Francis, the first pope from the Americas, was born in Buenos Aires."]],
sp:["Iguazú Falls","On the border between Argentina and Brazil, the Iguazú River plunges over a horseshoe-shaped cliff nearly 3 kilometers wide, broken into about 275 separate waterfalls. The most powerful, the Devil’s Throat, sends water roaring down about 80 meters into a misty gorge. The falls lie in protected rainforest, home to toucans, coatis and jaguars, and both countries’ national parks are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."],
g:[["Pampas","The vast, fertile grasslands of central Argentina"],["Gaucho","A horseman and cowboy of the South American plains"],["Patagonia","The cold, windy southern region of Argentina and Chile"],["Junta","A group of military officers ruling a country"],["Tango","A dance and music born in Buenos Aires"]],
f:["Aconcagua is the highest mountain in the world outside Asia.","Argentina has won the FIFA World Cup three times.","Ushuaia in Tierra del Fuego calls itself the southernmost city in the world."],
fun:"Aconcagua, in Argentina, is the highest mountain outside Asia.",
tl:"Ang Argentina ay malaking bansa sa timog ng Timog Amerika, sa pagitan ng Andes at Karagatang Atlantiko. Kilala ito sa malawak na damuhang pampas, sa tango at sa husay nito sa futbol."});

