ADDART({id:"perucountry",t:"Peru",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦙",w:"Peru",r:["andes","amazon","potato","aztec","maya","explor"],
p:[["Three worlds in one","Peru has a dry desert coast, the high Andes Mountains and the Amazon rainforest, which covers more than half the country."],["Land of the Inca","Peru was the heart of the Inca Empire, the largest state in the Americas before Europeans arrived, and is home to Machu Picchu."],["A gift of food","Potatoes, tomatoes and many other crops were first grown in the Andes, and Peruvian cooking is now admired worldwide."]],
l:"Peru is a country on the Pacific coast of South America. About 34 million people live there, a third of them in and around the capital, Lima. Its land rises from a narrow coastal desert to the snow-capped Andes and falls again into the Amazon rainforest. Peru was the center of the Inca Empire and of earlier civilizations stretching back thousands of years, and it still has large Indigenous populations who speak Quechua and Aymara alongside Spanish.",
s:[["Coast, mountains and jungle","Along the Pacific lies one of the driest deserts on Earth, crossed by rivers that make green oases. The Andes run down the middle of the country, with peaks such as Huascarán at 6,768 m. To the east, the Amazon rainforest covers about 60 percent of Peru, and the great Amazon River begins in its mountains."],["Lake Titicaca","On the border with Bolivia, at about 3,800 m, lies Lake Titicaca, often called the highest navigable lake in the world. The Uros people live on floating islands they build from totora reeds, which they also use to make houses and boats."],["People and languages","Peru’s people are of Indigenous, European, African and Asian descent, and many have mixed ancestry. Spanish, Quechua and Aymara are official languages, and Quechua, the language of the Inca, is spoken by several million people. Peru also has large communities descended from Chinese and Japanese immigrants."],["Economy","Peru is one of the world’s largest producers of copper, silver, gold and zinc. Its coastal waters, rich in nutrients from the cold Humboldt Current, support one of the biggest fisheries on Earth, mainly of anchovies. It also exports coffee, avocados, blueberries and asparagus, and tourism to Machu Picchu and Cusco is important."]],
k:[["Capital","Lima"],["Population","About 34 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.29 million km²"],["Highest point","Huascarán, 6,768 m"],["Currency","Peruvian sol"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Caral, one of the oldest cities in the Americas, is built"],["c. 1438","Pachacuti begins expanding the Inca Empire"],["1532","Francisco Pizarro captures the Inca ruler Atahualpa"],["1821","Peru declares independence from Spain"],["1911","Hiram Bingham brings Machu Picchu to world attention"],["2007","Machu Picchu is voted one of the New Seven Wonders of the World"]],
s2:[["Ancient civilizations","Long before the Inca, the coast and mountains of Peru were home to great cultures. Caral, built about 5,000 years ago, is among the oldest cities in the Americas. The Nazca people drew giant lines and figures in the desert, the Moche built adobe pyramids and made lifelike pottery, and the Chimú ruled from Chan Chan, once the largest adobe city in the world."],["The Inca Empire","In the 1400s the Inca, from their capital Cusco, built an empire called Tawantinsuyu that stretched about 4,000 kilometers along the Andes, from Ecuador to Chile. They connected it with roads over 30,000 kilometers long, with rope bridges and relay runners, and kept records with knotted cords called quipu. They built terraces and stone walls so precise that no mortar was needed."],["Conquest","In 1532 Spanish conquistadors led by Francisco Pizarro captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa, who was caught in a civil war against his brother. Despite a huge ransom of gold and silver, Atahualpa was executed. European diseases, war and forced labor in silver mines killed a great part of the Indigenous population. Lima, founded in 1535, became the capital of Spanish South America."],["Independence","Peru declared independence on 28 July 1821 under José de San Martín, and the final Spanish defeat came at the Battle of Ayacucho in 1824. The new republic was shaken by wars with its neighbors, coups and inequality between the coast and the Andes."],["Modern Peru","From 1980 the Shining Path, a violent Maoist group, waged a war that, with the army’s response, killed about 70,000 people before its leader was captured in 1992. Peru has since grown quickly, cutting poverty, but its politics are unstable, with many presidents removed or prosecuted."],["Food","Peru has more than 3,000 kinds of potato and gave the world the tomato, chili peppers, quinoa and lima beans. Its national dish, ceviche, is raw fish cured in lime juice with chili and onion. Chinese and Japanese immigrants added their own styles, creating chifa and Nikkei cooking, and Lima has become one of the world’s great food cities."]],
sp:["Machu Picchu","High on a ridge above the Urubamba River, the Inca built Machu Picchu in the mid-1400s, probably as a royal estate for the emperor Pachacuti. Its temples, terraces and houses of fitted stone were never found by the Spanish. Local farmers knew of it, and in 1911 the American explorer Hiram Bingham brought it to world attention. Today it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site visited by more than a million people a year."],
g:[["Inca","The empire and people that ruled the Andes in the 1400s and early 1500s"],["Quechua","The language of the Inca, still spoken by millions"],["Quipu","Knotted cords used by the Inca to keep records"],["Conquistador","A Spanish conqueror in the Americas"],["Ceviche","Raw fish cured in citrus juice, Peru’s national dish"]],
f:["Peru has more than 3,000 varieties of potato.","The Inca kept records with knotted strings, not writing.","Lake Titicaca is often called the highest navigable lake in the world."],
fun:"Peru grows more than 3,000 kinds of potato.",
tl:"Ang Peru ay bansa sa baybaying Pasipiko ng Timog Amerika, na may disyerto, kabundukang Andes at gubat ng Amazon. Dito ang puso ng Imperyong Inca at ang tanyag na Machu Picchu."});

ADDART({id:"kenyacountry",t:"Kenya",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦁",w:"Kenya",r:["rift","lion","giraffe","elephant","athletics","decol"],
p:[["On the equator","Kenya lies on the equator in East Africa, with a coast on the Indian Ocean and Lake Victoria in the west."],["Land of safaris","Its savannas and national parks are home to lions, elephants, giraffes and the great wildebeest migration."],["Cradle of humankind","Some of the oldest fossils of human ancestors have been found around Lake Turkana in northern Kenya."]],
l:"Kenya is a country in East Africa, crossed by the equator and the Great Rift Valley. About 56 million people live there, and its capital, Nairobi, is one of Africa’s leading cities for business and technology. Kenya is famous for its wildlife and safaris, its long-distance runners and its many peoples and cultures. A British colony until 1963, it is today one of the largest economies in East Africa.",
s:[["Land","Kenya’s land rises from a hot, humid coast to high central highlands, where Mount Kenya, at 5,199 m, is the second-highest mountain in Africa. The Great Rift Valley cuts through the country from north to south, dotted with lakes such as Nakuru and Naivasha. Much of the north and east is dry scrubland and desert."],["Wildlife","Kenya has more than 50 national parks and reserves. In the Maasai Mara, millions of wildebeest and zebras arrive each year from Tanzania’s Serengeti in the great migration, crossing crocodile-filled rivers. Lake Nakuru is famous for flamingos, and Amboseli for elephants with Kilimanjaro behind them."],["Peoples and languages","Kenya has more than 40 ethnic groups, including the Kikuyu, Luhya, Kalenjin, Luo, Kamba and Maasai. Swahili and English are the official languages, and Swahili, a Bantu language with many Arabic words, is a common language across East Africa. Most Kenyans are Christian, with a Muslim minority, especially on the coast."],["Economy","Agriculture employs many Kenyans, and the country is one of the world’s top exporters of tea and cut flowers, which are flown fresh to Europe. Tourism, services and technology are growing. Nairobi is nicknamed the Silicon Savannah, and Kenya’s mobile money service, M-Pesa, launched in 2007, transformed how people pay and save."]],
k:[["Capital","Nairobi"],["Population","About 56 million (2024)"],["Area","About 580,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Kenya, 5,199 m"],["Currency","Kenyan shilling"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Pastoralists spread into the region"],["1498","Vasco da Gama visits the coast at Malindi"],["1895","Britain declares a protectorate over the region"],["1952","The Mau Mau uprising begins"],["1963","Kenya becomes independent"],["2004","Wangari Maathai wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2010","Kenyans approve a new constitution"]],
s2:[["Our oldest ancestors","Fossils found around Lake Turkana include some of the most important evidence of human evolution, such as the Turkana Boy, a nearly complete skeleton of Homo erectus about 1.5 million years old, found in 1984. Stone tools from the area are about 3.3 million years old, among the oldest known."],["The Swahili coast","From about the 800s, trading towns grew along the coast, such as Mombasa, Malindi and Lamu. Swahili culture mixed African, Arab, Persian and Indian influences, and merchants traded ivory, gold and slaves for cloth, porcelain and beads across the Indian Ocean. Portuguese ships arrived in 1498, and later the Omani Arabs ruled the coast."],["British rule","In the late 1800s Britain took control and built a railway from Mombasa to Lake Victoria, and Nairobi grew up as a railway depot in 1899. White settlers took fertile highlands, pushing Africans off their land. From 1952 the Mau Mau uprising, mainly among the Kikuyu, fought British rule, and British forces held tens of thousands of people in detention camps."],["Independence","Kenya became independent on 12 December 1963, with Jomo Kenyatta as its first leader. For decades one party dominated, but multiparty elections returned in 1991. A disputed election in 2007 led to violence that killed more than 1,000 people. In 2010 Kenyans approved a new constitution that shared power with 47 county governments."],["Champions of the track","Kenyan runners, many from the highlands of the Rift Valley, have dominated middle- and long-distance running for decades. Eliud Kipchoge won two Olympic marathon titles and in 2019 became the first person to run a marathon in under two hours, in a special event. Faith Kipyegon has set world records and won three Olympic 1,500 m titles."],["Wangari Maathai","In 1977 the Kenyan biologist Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement, which organized women to plant more than 30 million trees to fight deforestation and poverty. She campaigned for democracy and was beaten and jailed. In 2004 she became the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize."]],
sp:["The great migration","Every year, about 1.5 million wildebeest, along with hundreds of thousands of zebras and gazelles, travel in a great loop through Tanzania’s Serengeti and Kenya’s Maasai Mara, following the rains and fresh grass. Around July they reach the Mara River, where they plunge across in huge herds while crocodiles wait in the water. It is one of the greatest wildlife spectacles on Earth and a mainstay of Kenya’s tourism."],
g:[["Savanna","A tropical grassland with scattered trees"],["Rift valley","A long valley formed where Earth’s crust pulls apart"],["Swahili","A Bantu language and culture of the East African coast"],["Safari","A journey to watch or photograph wild animals, from the Swahili for journey"],["Mau Mau","An uprising against British rule in Kenya in the 1950s"]],
f:["Kenya is one of the world’s largest exporters of black tea.","The word safari comes from Swahili and means journey.","Mount Kenya, on the equator, has glaciers."],
fun:"The word safari comes from Swahili, meaning journey.",
tl:"Ang Kenya ay bansa sa Silangang Aprika na tinatawid ng ekwador at ng Great Rift Valley. Kilala ito sa mga hayop sa safari, sa mga mahuhusay na mananakbo at sa kabiserang Nairobi."});

ADDART({id:"ethiopiacountry",t:"Ethiopia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"☕",w:"Ethiopia",r:["rift","coffee","christ","nile","decol","evo"],
p:[["The roof of Africa","Ethiopia is a landlocked country in the Horn of Africa, built around high plateaus and mountains split by the Great Rift Valley."],["Never colonized","Apart from an Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941, Ethiopia kept its independence when nearly all of Africa was colonized."],["Home of coffee","Coffee is believed to have first been used in Ethiopia, where the coffee ceremony is still a cherished tradition."]],
l:"Ethiopia is a country in the Horn of Africa, the continent’s second most populous nation after Nigeria, with about 132 million people. Its capital, Addis Ababa, is home to the African Union. Ethiopia has one of the world’s oldest Christian churches, ancient kingdoms and its own script and calendar. Fossils found there, such as the famous Lucy, show that it was one of the places where humankind began.",
s:[["Land","Much of Ethiopia is a high plateau, the Ethiopian Highlands, where many peaks rise above 4,000 m; Ras Dashen is the highest, at about 4,550 m. The Great Rift Valley cuts through the middle, with lakes and hot springs. In the northeast, the Danakil Depression, one of the hottest places on Earth, lies more than 100 m below sea level."],["Water and climate","The highlands are cool and rainy, while the lowlands are hot and dry. The Blue Nile rises at Lake Tana and supplies most of the water of the Nile in Egypt. Ethiopia has suffered severe droughts, and the famine of 1983 to 1985 killed hundreds of thousands of people."],["Peoples and languages","Ethiopia has more than 80 ethnic groups and languages. The largest groups are the Oromo, Amhara, Somali and Tigray. Amharic, written in the ancient Ge’ez script, is the working language of the federal government, and Afaan Oromo is the most widely spoken first language. About two thirds of Ethiopians are Christian, mostly Ethiopian Orthodox, and about a third are Muslim."],["Economy","Most Ethiopians work in farming, and coffee is the most important export. Ethiopia grew very fast in the 2000s and 2010s, building roads, railways, industrial parks and the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile, the largest hydroelectric plant in Africa. Ethiopian Airlines is the largest airline in Africa."]],
k:[["Capital","Addis Ababa"],["Population","About 132 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.1 million km²"],["Highest point","Ras Dashen, about 4,550 m"],["Currency","Ethiopian birr"]],
d:[["c. 3.2 million years ago","Lucy, an early human ancestor, lives in the Afar region"],["c. 330 CE","King Ezana of Aksum adopts Christianity"],["1100s–1200s","The rock-hewn churches of Lalibela are carved"],["1896","Ethiopia defeats Italy at the Battle of Adwa"],["1936–1941","Italy occupies Ethiopia"],["1974","Emperor Haile Selassie is overthrown"],["2019","Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["Lucy and our ancestors","In 1974 scientists in the Afar region found much of the skeleton of a 3.2-million-year-old human ancestor, an Australopithecus afarensis they named Lucy, after a Beatles song. Ethiopia has also yielded some of the oldest known fossils of modern humans, more than 200,000 years old, found near the Omo River."],["The kingdom of Aksum","From about the 100s CE, the kingdom of Aksum in northern Ethiopia and Eritrea traded with Rome, India and Arabia, minted its own coins and raised giant carved stone obelisks. Around 330 King Ezana became Christian, making Aksum one of the first Christian states in the world."],["Lalibela","In the 1100s and 1200s, King Lalibela had 11 churches carved downward into solid rock, each a single piece of stone, connected by trenches and tunnels. Built as a new Jerusalem for pilgrims, they are still used for worship, and they are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Adwa and independence","In 1896 the army of Emperor Menelik II defeated an invading Italian army at the Battle of Adwa, one of the greatest victories of an African state over a European power. Ethiopia stayed independent while almost all of Africa was colonized, becoming a symbol of African freedom, and its colors, green, yellow and red, inspired the flags of many new African nations."],["Emperors and revolution","Haile Selassie, emperor from 1930, led resistance to Italy’s occupation of 1936 to 1941 and helped found the Organization of African Unity in 1963 in Addis Ababa. In 1974 he was overthrown by a Marxist military regime, the Derg, which ruled with terror until 1991. Ethiopia then became a federation of ethnic regions; Eritrea became independent in 1993."],["Calendar, script and coffee","Ethiopia uses its own calendar, with 13 months, which runs about seven to eight years behind the Gregorian calendar, and its own way of counting the hours of the day. The Ge’ez script, used for Amharic and other languages, is one of the oldest in Africa. In the coffee ceremony, beans are roasted, ground and brewed in a clay pot, and guests drink three cups."]],
sp:["The legend of Kaldi","A famous legend says that a goat herder named Kaldi, in the highlands of Ethiopia, noticed his goats dancing with energy after eating red berries from a certain shrub. Curious, he tried them himself. Whether or not the story is true, wild coffee plants do grow in Ethiopia’s forests, and coffee was probably first used there before spreading to Yemen in the 1400s and from there to the rest of the world."],
g:[["Plateau","A large area of high, fairly flat land"],["Horn of Africa","The peninsula of northeastern Africa that juts into the Arabian Sea"],["Ge’ez","An ancient Ethiopian language and the script used to write Amharic"],["Orthodox church","Here, the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, one of the oldest Christian churches"],["Obelisk","A tall, four-sided stone pillar"]],
f:["Ethiopia’s calendar has 13 months.","The skeleton of Lucy, 3.2 million years old, was found in Ethiopia in 1974.","Addis Ababa means new flower in Amharic."],
fun:"Ethiopia’s calendar has 13 months.",
tl:"Ang Ethiopia ay bansa sa Sungay ng Aprika na may matataas na talampas. Hindi ito nasakop nang matagal ng mga Europeo, at dito unang nakilala ang kape at natagpuan ang fossil na si Lucy."});

