ADDART({id:"indonesiacountry",t:"Indonesia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌋",w:"Indonesia",r:["sea","volc","tsunami","islam","buddh","asean"],
p:[["The largest archipelago","Indonesia is made up of about 17,000 islands stretching more than 5,000 kilometers between the Indian and Pacific oceans."],["Many peoples, one nation","It is the world’s fourth most populous country, with hundreds of ethnic groups and languages united by the motto Unity in Diversity."],["Fire and water","Indonesia lies on the Ring of Fire and has some of the world’s most active volcanoes, as well as rainforests and coral reefs rich in life."]],
l:"Indonesia is a country in Southeast Asia made up of about 17,000 islands, the largest archipelago in the world. With about 284 million people, it is the fourth most populous country on Earth and has the largest Muslim population of any nation. Its capital, Jakarta, on the island of Java, was ranked the world’s largest urban area by the United Nations in 2025, though a new capital, Nusantara, is being built on Borneo. Indonesia is a democracy, the largest economy in Southeast Asia and a land of volcanoes, rainforests and extraordinary cultural variety.",
s:[["Islands","The largest islands are Sumatra, Java, Borneo, which Indonesia shares with Malaysia and Brunei and calls Kalimantan, Sulawesi and New Guinea, shared with Papua New Guinea. Java, about the size of England, is one of the most crowded islands on Earth, with more than half of all Indonesians. Thousands of small islands are uninhabited."],["Volcanoes and earthquakes","Indonesia has about 130 active volcanoes. The eruption of Tambora in 1815 was the largest in recorded history, and Krakatoa’s explosion in 1883 was heard thousands of kilometers away. Earthquakes and tsunamis are a constant danger: the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami killed about 170,000 people in Aceh alone."],["Wildlife","Indonesia is one of the most biodiverse countries. Its forests are home to orangutans, Sumatran tigers and rhinos, and on the island of Komodo live Komodo dragons, the largest lizards. The Wallace Line, between Bali and Lombok, divides Asian animals from Australian ones. Large areas of rainforest have been cleared for palm oil and logging."],["Peoples and languages","Indonesia has more than 600 ethnic groups and about 700 languages. The largest group is the Javanese. The national language, Indonesian, a form of Malay, was chosen in 1928 to unite the country and is spoken by almost everyone, usually alongside a local language."]],
k:[["Capital","Jakarta (Nusantara under construction)"],["Population","About 284 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.9 million km²"],["Highest point","Puncak Jaya, 4,884 m"],["Currency","Indonesian rupiah"]],
d:[["c. 800s","Borobudur is built in Java"],["1293","The Majapahit Empire is founded"],["1602","The Dutch East India Company is founded"],["1945","Sukarno declares independence on 17 August"],["1949","The Netherlands recognizes Indonesian independence"],["1998","Suharto resigns, opening the way to democracy"],["2004","An Indian Ocean tsunami devastates Aceh"]],
s2:[["Kingdoms and empires","Indian religions and trade shaped early Indonesian kingdoms. The Buddhist Srivijaya, based in Sumatra, controlled sea trade from the 600s, and in Java rulers built the Buddhist Borobudur and the Hindu Prambanan temples in the 800s. The Majapahit Empire, founded in 1293, ruled much of the archipelago from eastern Java."],["The coming of Islam","Muslim traders from India, Arabia and China brought Islam from the 1200s, and it spread through ports and sultanates over the following centuries. Today about 87 percent of Indonesians are Muslim. Bali remains mostly Hindu, and there are large Christian communities in places such as North Sulawesi and Papua."],["The Dutch East Indies","Europeans came for spices: nutmeg, cloves and pepper. The Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, gradually took control, and the Netherlands ruled the islands as the Dutch East Indies until World War II. Forced cultivation of export crops in the 1800s brought great wealth to the Netherlands and hardship to many Indonesians."],["Independence","Japan occupied the islands from 1942 to 1945. Two days after Japan surrendered, Sukarno and Mohammad Hatta declared independence on 17 August 1945. The Dutch tried to return, and four years of fighting and diplomacy followed before they recognized Indonesia in 1949. Sukarno became the first president."],["Suharto and democracy","In 1965 to 1966 an alleged coup attempt was followed by the mass killing of hundreds of thousands of people accused of being communists, and General Suharto took power. He ruled for 32 years, with fast economic growth but repression and corruption. The Asian financial crisis of 1997 and mass protests forced him out in 1998. Since then Indonesia has become a democracy, with direct presidential elections since 2004."],["Culture","Indonesian culture includes the gamelan orchestras of gongs and metallophones, wayang shadow-puppet plays that retell Hindu epics, batik cloth dyed with wax, and dances from Bali and Java. Rice is the staple, with dishes such as nasi goreng, fried rice, satay and rendang, a slow-cooked spicy beef. Badminton is a passion, and Indonesians have won many Olympic medals in it."]],
sp:["Borobudur","Built in central Java in the 800s, Borobudur is the largest Buddhist temple in the world. Pilgrims walk around nine stacked platforms, passing more than 2,600 carved relief panels that tell stories of the Buddha’s life and teachings, until they reach the top, ringed by 72 bell-shaped stupas. Buried under volcanic ash and jungle for centuries, it was restored with UNESCO’s help and is now a World Heritage Site."],
g:[["Archipelago","A large group of islands"],["Sultanate","A state ruled by a sultan, a Muslim ruler"],["Gamelan","An Indonesian orchestra of gongs, drums and metallophones"],["Wayang","Traditional Indonesian puppet theater"],["Batik","A way of dyeing cloth using wax to make patterns"]],
f:["More volcanoes have erupted in recorded history in Indonesia than in any other country.","Komodo dragons, the largest lizards, live only in Indonesia.","Indonesia’s motto, Bhinneka Tunggal Ika, means Unity in Diversity."],
fun:"Indonesia stretches more than 5,000 kilometers from east to west, wider than the United States.",
tl:"Ang Indonesia ay ang pinakamalaking kapuluan sa mundo na may halos 17,000 pulo. Ito ang ikaapat na pinakamataong bansa at may pinakamaraming Muslim sa alinmang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"vietnamcountry",t:"Vietnam",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🍜",w:"Vietnam",r:["vietnam","mekong","asean","rice","coffee","china"],
p:[["A long, narrow country","Vietnam curves in an S-shape along the eastern edge of mainland Southeast Asia, with a coastline more than 3,000 kilometers long."],["Two great deltas","The Red River in the north and the Mekong in the south form fertile deltas that grow enormous amounts of rice."],["War and renewal","After centuries of struggle and a long war that ended in 1975, Vietnam became one of the fastest-growing economies in Asia."]],
l:"Vietnam is a country in Southeast Asia, stretching in a long S-shape along the coast of the South China Sea. About 101 million people live there. Its capital is Hanoi in the north, and its largest city is Ho Chi Minh City, formerly Saigon, in the south. Vietnam has a history of more than 2,000 years, marked by long struggles for independence. Reunited in 1976 under a communist government, it opened its economy in 1986 and has become a major manufacturing and exporting nation.",
s:[["Land","Vietnam is mountainous in the north and along its western border with Laos and Cambodia, with Fansipan, at 3,143 m, the highest peak in Indochina. The coast has long beaches and the limestone islands of Ha Long Bay. In the south, the Mekong Delta is a vast maze of rivers and canals."],["Climate","The north has four seasons, with cool, damp winters, while the south is hot all year, with a rainy season from about May to November. Typhoons often strike the central coast, and the deltas are threatened by flooding and rising seas."],["People","About 85 percent of people are Kinh, ethnic Vietnamese, and there are 53 other recognized ethnic groups, many living in the highlands. Vietnamese is a tonal language written in the Latin alphabet with many accent marks, a system created by missionaries in the 1600s and made official in the 1900s."],["Economy","Vietnam is one of the world’s largest exporters of rice, coffee, cashew nuts, pepper and seafood. Since the 1990s, foreign companies have built factories there, and it now makes many of the world’s phones, electronics, clothes and shoes. Poverty has fallen dramatically, from more than half the population in the early 1990s to a few percent."]],
k:[["Capital","Hanoi"],["Population","About 101 million (2024)"],["Area","About 331,000 km²"],["Highest point","Fansipan, 3,143 m"],["Currency","Vietnamese đồng"]],
d:[["111 BCE","China’s Han dynasty conquers northern Vietnam"],["938","Ngô Quyền defeats Chinese forces at the Bạch Đằng River"],["1802","The Nguyễn dynasty unites the country"],["1887","France forms French Indochina"],["1954","The French are defeated at Điện Biên Phủ"],["1975","Saigon falls, ending the Vietnam War"],["1986","Đổi Mới economic reforms begin"]],
s2:[["A thousand years under China","Northern Vietnam was ruled by China for about a thousand years, from 111 BCE. Chinese writing, Confucian ideas and Buddhism shaped Vietnamese culture, but the Vietnamese kept their language and rebelled many times, as when the Trưng sisters led an uprising in 40 CE. In 938 Ngô Quyền won independence at the Battle of Bạch Đằng River."],["Dynasties","Independent Vietnamese dynasties ruled for centuries, fighting off further Chinese invasions and three Mongol attacks in the 1200s. They expanded southward along the coast, absorbing the kingdom of Champa and part of the Khmer lands. In 1802 the Nguyễn dynasty unified the country, with its capital at Huế."],["French rule","France conquered Vietnam step by step from 1858 and in 1887 combined it with Cambodia and Laos as French Indochina. It built railways and plantations of rubber and rice, but most Vietnamese were poor and had no say. Nationalists, including the communist leader Hồ Chí Minh, organized resistance."],["Wars for independence","Hồ Chí Minh declared independence in 1945. War with France followed, ending with the French defeat at Điện Biên Phủ in 1954. Vietnam was divided into a communist North and a South backed by the United States. The Vietnam War that followed killed millions of Vietnamese, and ended with the fall of Saigon in 1975. The country was reunited in 1976."],["Đổi Mới","After the war Vietnam was poor and isolated. In 1986 the government launched Đổi Mới, meaning renovation, allowing private business, foreign investment and market prices while the Communist Party kept political control. Vietnam joined ASEAN in 1995, restored relations with the United States the same year and joined the World Trade Organization in 2007."],["Food and culture","Vietnamese food is fresh and fragrant, with herbs, rice noodles and fish sauce. Phở, a beef or chicken noodle soup, and bánh mì, a baguette sandwich from the French era, are famous worldwide. Tết, the Lunar New Year, is the most important festival, and water puppetry, performed on a pool, is a unique Vietnamese art."]],
sp:["Ha Long Bay","In the Gulf of Tonkin, about 1,600 limestone islands and pillars rise steeply from emerald water, many topped with forest and hollowed by caves. Their name means descending dragon, from a legend that a dragon created them to protect the land from invaders. Formed by millions of years of erosion, Ha Long Bay is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of Vietnam’s most visited places."],
g:[["Delta","Flat land where a river splits and spreads out before reaching the sea"],["Tonal language","A language in which the pitch of a word changes its meaning"],["Indochina","The French colony of Vietnam, Cambodia and Laos"],["Đổi Mới","Vietnam’s economic reforms begun in 1986"],["Tết","The Vietnamese Lunar New Year"]],
f:["Vietnam is one of the world’s largest coffee producers, second only to Brazil.","Vietnamese is written with the Latin alphabet and many accent marks.","Sơn Đoòng, in Vietnam, is the largest known cave in the world."],
fun:"Sơn Đoòng in Vietnam is the largest known cave in the world.",
tl:"Ang Vietnam ay mahaba at makitid na bansa sa Timog-silangang Asya na hugis S. Matapos ang mahabang digmaan, naging isa ito sa pinakamabilis umunlad na ekonomiya sa Asya at malaking tagaluwas ng bigas at kape."});

ADDART({id:"southkoreacountry",t:"South Korea",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏙️",w:"South Korea",r:["korea","koreanwar","alphabet","smartphone","chip","coldwar"],
p:[["Half a peninsula","South Korea occupies the southern half of the Korean Peninsula in East Asia, separated from North Korea by a heavily guarded border."],["From poverty to prosperity","Devastated by war in the 1950s, it became one of the world’s richest and most technologically advanced countries."],["The Korean Wave","Korean music, dramas, films and food are now enjoyed by fans all over the world."]],
l:"South Korea, officially the Republic of Korea, is a country in East Asia on the southern half of the Korean Peninsula. About 52 million people live there, half of them in and around the capital, Seoul. After the peninsula was divided in 1945 and devastated by the Korean War, South Korea rose from poverty to become a wealthy democracy and a world leader in electronics, cars, shipbuilding and popular culture.",
s:[["Land","About 70 percent of South Korea is mountainous, so most people live in cities on the coastal plains and in river valleys. The peninsula is bordered by the Yellow Sea to the west, the East Sea, or Sea of Japan, to the east and the Korea Strait to the south. Jeju, a volcanic island in the south, has Hallasan, the highest mountain."],["The divided peninsula","Since 1953 the two Koreas have been separated by the Demilitarized Zone, a strip about 4 kilometers wide and 250 kilometers long. The war ended with an armistice, not a peace treaty, so the two countries are technically still at war. Millions of families were separated by the division."],["Cities","Seoul, on the Han River, is a huge, high-tech city with ancient palaces such as Gyeongbokgung. Its metropolitan area holds about 26 million people. Busan is the second-largest city and one of the busiest container ports in the world. Other cities include Incheon, Daegu and Gwangju."],["People and language","South Korea is one of the most ethnically uniform countries, though immigration is growing. Koreans speak Korean, written in Hangul, an alphabet created in 1443. Many people follow no religion, while others are Christian or Buddhist, and Confucian values of family, respect for elders and education remain strong."]],
k:[["Capital","Seoul"],["Population","About 52 million (2024)"],["Area","About 100,000 km²"],["Highest point","Hallasan, 1,947 m"],["Currency","South Korean won"]],
d:[["1443","King Sejong creates the Hangul alphabet"],["1910","Japan annexes Korea"],["1948","The Republic of Korea is founded in the south"],["1950–1953","The Korean War"],["1987","Mass protests win democratic elections"],["1988","Seoul hosts the Summer Olympics"],["2020","Parasite wins the Oscar for Best Picture"]],
s2:[["Kingdoms of Korea","Korea’s history includes the Three Kingdoms of Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla, the Goryeo dynasty, from which the name Korea comes, and the Joseon dynasty, which ruled from 1392 to 1910. Joseon scholars and King Sejong created Hangul so that ordinary people could read, and Korean printers used metal movable type before Europe."],["Colonial rule and division","Japan annexed Korea in 1910 and ruled harshly, suppressing Korean language and culture. After Japan’s defeat in 1945, the Soviet Union occupied the north and the United States the south. In 1948 two separate states were created: the Republic of Korea in the south and the communist Democratic People’s Republic of Korea in the north."],["The Korean War","In June 1950 North Korea invaded the South. United Nations forces led by the United States fought for the South, and China joined the North. The war killed millions, including many civilians, and destroyed most of the peninsula’s cities before an armistice in 1953 left the border near where it began."],["The Miracle on the Han River","In the 1950s South Korea was one of the poorest countries in the world. From the 1960s, under authoritarian governments, it built industries with huge family-run companies called chaebol, such as Samsung, Hyundai and LG. Hard work, education and exports drove one of the fastest economic rises in history, and today it is a leading maker of memory chips, phones, ships and cars."],["Democracy","For decades South Korea was ruled by military-backed strongmen, and protests were harshly suppressed, as in the Gwangju Uprising of 1980. In 1987 nationwide protests forced democratic elections. Since then power has changed hands peacefully between parties, and citizens have shown their strength, as in the candlelight protests of 2016 to 2017 that led to a president’s impeachment."],["The Korean Wave","Since the late 1990s, South Korean pop culture, called Hallyu, has spread worldwide. K-pop groups such as BTS and Blackpink fill stadiums on every continent, K-dramas are watched by millions on streaming services, and Parasite became the first non-English-language film to win the Oscar for Best Picture. Korean food, such as kimchi and Korean barbecue, has become popular far from home."]],
sp:["Hangul, the people’s alphabet","In 1443 King Sejong the Great announced a new alphabet so that all Koreans could learn to read and write, not just scholars who knew Chinese characters. Its letters are simple shapes, some based on the position of the mouth and tongue when making each sound, grouped into square blocks for each syllable. Linguists praise it as one of the most logical writing systems ever designed, and Koreans celebrate Hangul Day every 9 October."],
g:[["Peninsula","Land surrounded by water on three sides"],["Demilitarized Zone","The strip of land separating North and South Korea"],["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting, which is not a full peace treaty"],["Chaebol","A large family-controlled business group in South Korea"],["Hallyu","The Korean Wave of popular culture"]],
f:["South Korea has one of the fastest average internet speeds in the world.","The Demilitarized Zone has become an accidental wildlife refuge.","Hangul has 24 basic letters."],
fun:"The Korean alphabet, Hangul, was created in 1443 and has 24 basic letters.",
tl:"Ang Timog Korea ay bansa sa timog na bahagi ng Tangway ng Korea. Mula sa kahirapan pagkatapos ng digmaan, naging mayaman at makabagong bansa ito, kilala sa teknolohiya at sa K-pop at K-drama."});

