ADDART({id:"pakistancountry",t:"Pakistan",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏔️",w:"Pakistan",r:["indus","himal","islam","decol","cricket","malala"],
p:[["From mountains to the sea","Pakistan stretches from the world’s highest mountain ranges in the north to the deserts and coast of the Arabian Sea in the south."],["The Indus valley","The Indus River, which flows the length of the country, watered one of the world’s first civilizations more than 4,500 years ago."],["A young nation","Created in 1947 as a homeland for Muslims of British India, Pakistan has the world’s fifth-largest population."]],
l:"Pakistan is a country in South Asia, bordered by India, China, Afghanistan and Iran, with a coast on the Arabian Sea. With about 251 million people, it is the fifth most populous country in the world. Its capital is Islamabad, and its largest city is Karachi. Pakistan was created in 1947, when British India was partitioned, as a state for the region’s Muslims. Its land holds the ruins of the Indus Valley Civilization and some of the highest mountains on Earth.",
s:[["Mountains","In the north, three great ranges meet: the Himalayas, the Karakoram and the Hindu Kush. K2, on the border with China, is the second-highest mountain in the world at 8,611 m, and Pakistan has five of the world’s fourteen peaks over 8,000 m. Its glaciers are among the largest outside the polar regions."],["The Indus and the plains","The Indus River and its tributaries flow south through the fertile plains of Punjab and Sindh, where most Pakistanis live. One of the world’s largest irrigation systems carries their water to farms growing wheat, rice, cotton and sugarcane. To the west lie the dry plateaus of Balochistan."],["Provinces and cities","Pakistan has four provinces, Punjab, Sindh, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and Balochistan, as well as the capital territory and the regions of Gilgit-Baltistan and Azad Kashmir. Karachi, a huge port city, has more than 15 million people. Lahore, the cultural capital, has Mughal monuments such as the Badshahi Mosque and the Lahore Fort."],["People and languages","Pakistan’s main ethnic groups include the Punjabis, Pashtuns, Sindhis, Saraikis and Baloch. Urdu is the national language and English an official language, while Punjabi is the most widely spoken first language. About 96 percent of Pakistanis are Muslim, and the country has a very young population."]],
k:[["Capital","Islamabad"],["Population","About 251 million (2024)"],["Area","About 882,000 km²"],["Highest point","K2, 8,611 m"],["Currency","Pakistani rupee"]],
d:[["c. 2600 BCE","Mohenjo-daro and Harappa flourish in the Indus Valley"],["1526","The Mughal Empire is founded"],["1947","Pakistan becomes independent as British India is partitioned"],["1971","East Pakistan becomes the independent country of Bangladesh"],["1998","Pakistan tests nuclear weapons"],["2014","Malala Yousafzai wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["2022","Catastrophic floods cover about a third of the country"]],
s2:[["The Indus Valley Civilization","About 4,500 years ago, cities such as Mohenjo-daro and Harappa flourished along the Indus. They had grid-planned streets, brick houses, drains and public baths, and used a script that has never been deciphered. The civilization traded with Mesopotamia and declined after about 1900 BCE."],["Empires and faiths","The region has been ruled by Persians, Greeks under Alexander the Great, Buddhist kingdoms that created the art of Gandhara, and from the 700s onward a series of Muslim dynasties. The Mughal Empire, founded in 1526, left magnificent buildings in Lahore. Sikhism also began in the Punjab, and its founder, Guru Nanak, was born in what is now Pakistan."],["Partition","Under British rule, Muslim leaders such as Muhammad Ali Jinnah, called the Quaid-e-Azam, or Great Leader, demanded a separate homeland. When Britain left in 1947, British India was divided into India and Pakistan. About 15 million people fled across the new borders, and up to a million or more died in violence. The dispute over Kashmir began then and remains unresolved."],["Two wings to one","Pakistan was first made of two parts more than 1,500 kilometers apart, West and East Pakistan. Bengalis in the East felt neglected, and in 1971, after a brutal war, East Pakistan became the independent country of Bangladesh. Since then Pakistan has alternated between elected governments and military rule."],["Challenges","Pakistan has faced wars with India, militant violence, poverty and political instability. Its economy depends on farming, textiles and money sent home by workers abroad. It is also highly vulnerable to climate change: in 2022 record monsoon rains and melting glaciers flooded about a third of the country and affected some 33 million people."],["Culture and sport","Pakistan has rich traditions of poetry in Urdu and Persian, Sufi devotional music such as qawwali, made famous worldwide by Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan, and a love of spicy food such as biryani and kebabs. Cricket is a national passion, and Pakistan won the Cricket World Cup in 1992. The country also once dominated field hockey and squash."]],
sp:["Malala Yousafzai","Malala Yousafzai grew up in the Swat Valley, where Taliban militants banned girls from school. As a young girl she wrote a blog about her life and spoke out for girls’ education. In 2012, when she was 15, a gunman shot her on her school bus. She survived, continued her campaign from Britain and in 2014, at 17, became the youngest person ever to win the Nobel Peace Prize."],
g:[["Partition","The division of British India into India and Pakistan in 1947"],["Indus Valley Civilization","An ancient civilization of planned cities along the Indus River"],["Karakoram","A great mountain range in northern Pakistan, home to K2"],["Qawwali","A form of Sufi devotional music"],["Province","One of Pakistan’s main administrative regions"]],
f:["K2, in Pakistan, is the second-highest mountain in the world.","The Indus script of Mohenjo-daro has never been deciphered.","Pakistan has five of the world’s 14 mountains higher than 8,000 meters."],
fun:"Pakistan has five of the world’s 14 mountains over 8,000 meters, including K2.",
tl:"Ang Pakistan ay bansa sa Timog Asya na itinatag noong 1947 bilang tahanan ng mga Muslim ng British India. Narito ang matataas na bundok tulad ng K2 at ang mga guho ng sinaunang kabihasnan ng Indus."});

ADDART({id:"irancountry",t:"Iran",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Iran",r:["mesop","islam","goldage","silk","alexander","greece"],
p:[["Land of Persia","Iran, long known to outsiders as Persia, is a mountainous country between the Caspian Sea and the Persian Gulf."],["An ancient civilization","Its history stretches back thousands of years, to the Persian Empire of Cyrus the Great, one of the largest the world had seen."],["Poetry and gardens","Iranian culture is famous for its poets, carpets, miniature paintings, tiled mosques and walled gardens."]],
l:"Iran is a country in Western Asia, between the Caspian Sea to the north and the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman to the south. About 92 million people live there, and its capital is Tehran. Known as Persia for much of its history, Iran is one of the world’s oldest civilizations, the heart of great empires and a center of Islamic art and learning. Since a revolution in 1979, it has been an Islamic republic governed by Shia religious leaders.",
s:[["Land","Iran is a high plateau ringed by mountains, the Alborz in the north, where Mount Damavand, a dormant volcano, rises to about 5,610 m, and the Zagros in the west. Its interior holds two great deserts, the Dasht-e Kavir, a salt desert, and the Dasht-e Lut, where satellites have measured some of the hottest ground temperatures on Earth."],["Water","Much of Iran is dry, and for thousands of years people have used qanats, underground tunnels that carry water from mountains to villages and fields. The Caspian coast in the north is green and rainy. Today droughts, overuse of groundwater and shrinking lakes are serious problems."],["Cities","Tehran, at the foot of the Alborz, is a sprawling city of about 9 million people. Isfahan was a glorious capital in the 1600s, with a vast square, blue-tiled mosques and old bridges. Shiraz is famous for its poets and gardens, Mashhad for its great shrine, a major pilgrimage site, and Tabriz for its bazaar."],["People and faith","Persians make up the largest group, along with Azerbaijanis, Kurds, Lurs, Arabs, Baloch and others. Persian, or Farsi, is the official language. About 99 percent of Iranians are Muslim, mostly Shia, and Iran is the largest Shia Muslim country. There are also Zoroastrian, Christian, Jewish and Bahá’í minorities."]],
k:[["Capital","Tehran"],["Population","About 92 million (2024)"],["Area","About 1.65 million km²"],["Highest point","Mount Damavand, about 5,610 m"],["Currency","Iranian rial"]],
d:[["c. 550 BCE","Cyrus the Great founds the Persian Empire"],["330 BCE","Alexander the Great destroys Persepolis"],["c. 651 CE","Arab armies conquer the Sasanian Empire"],["1501","The Safavid dynasty makes Shia Islam the state religion"],["1906","A constitutional revolution creates a parliament"],["1979","The Islamic Revolution overthrows the shah"],["1980–1988","War with Iraq"]],
s2:[["The Persian Empire","Around 550 BCE Cyrus the Great founded the Achaemenid Empire, which stretched from Egypt and Greece to India. Cyrus was known for allowing conquered peoples to keep their customs and religions, and Darius I built roads, a postal system and the great palace city of Persepolis. Alexander the Great conquered the empire in 330 BCE."],["Parthians and Sasanians","Later Persian empires, the Parthian and the Sasanian, rivaled Rome and Byzantium for centuries. The Sasanians, from 224 CE, made Zoroastrianism, one of the world’s oldest religions, the state faith, and built a rich court culture of art, music and architecture."],["Islam and the golden age","Arab armies conquered Persia in the 600s, and most Persians gradually became Muslim, but they kept their language. Persian scholars led the Islamic golden age: the physician Ibn Sina, or Avicenna, the mathematician al-Khwarizmi, from whose name comes the word algorithm, and poets such as Ferdowsi, Rumi, Saadi and Hafez."],["Safavids and shahs","The Safavid dynasty, from 1501, made Shia Islam the state religion and made Isfahan one of the world’s great cities. Later dynasties lost territory to Russia and influence to Britain. In 1906 a constitutional revolution created a parliament. From 1925 the Pahlavi shahs modernized Iran but ruled as autocrats, and oil became the center of the economy and of foreign interference."],["The Islamic Revolution","In 1979 mass protests overthrew Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, and Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini founded the Islamic Republic, in which elected officials serve under a Supreme Leader, a senior cleric. Soon after, Iraq invaded, beginning a war from 1980 to 1988 that killed hundreds of thousands. Iran’s relations with the United States and its neighbors have been tense, especially over its nuclear program."],["Culture","Iranians celebrate Nowruz, the Persian New Year, at the spring equinox, a festival more than 3,000 years old shared across Central Asia. Persian carpets, miniature painting and poetry are treasured worldwide, and Iranians often quote the poems of Hafez in daily life. Iranian films, such as those of Abbas Kiarostami and Asghar Farhadi, have won top international prizes."]],
sp:["Persepolis","Around 518 BCE, King Darius I began building Persepolis, a ceremonial capital on a great stone terrace in southern Iran. Its halls of tall columns were decorated with carved reliefs showing people from across the empire, Egyptians, Babylonians, Indians and others, bringing gifts to the king, probably at the New Year festival. Alexander the Great burned it in 330 BCE, but its ruins remain, and it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],
g:[["Persia","The historical name for Iran used by outsiders"],["Qanat","An underground channel that carries water from the mountains"],["Zoroastrianism","An ancient Iranian religion founded by the prophet Zoroaster"],["Shia","The second-largest branch of Islam, followed by most Iranians"],["Nowruz","The Persian New Year, celebrated at the spring equinox"]],
f:["The word algorithm comes from the name of the Persian mathematician al-Khwarizmi.","Nowruz, the Persian New Year, has been celebrated for more than 3,000 years.","Iran’s qanat water tunnels, some thousands of years old, are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],
fun:"The word algorithm comes from the name of the Persian mathematician al-Khwarizmi.",
tl:"Ang Iran, na dating tinatawag na Persia, ay bansa sa Kanlurang Asya sa pagitan ng Dagat Caspian at Golpo ng Persia. Isa ito sa pinakamatandang kabihasnan, tahanan ng Imperyong Persiano at ng tanyag na tula at alpombra."});

ADDART({id:"saudicountry",t:"Saudi Arabia",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐪",w:"Saudi Arabia",r:["islam","desert","arabic","sahara","renew","trade"],
p:[["Arabian deserts","Saudi Arabia covers most of the Arabian Peninsula, a land of vast deserts, including the Empty Quarter, the largest sand desert in the world."],["Birthplace of Islam","Mecca and Medina, the holiest cities of Islam, are in Saudi Arabia, and millions of Muslims make the pilgrimage there each year."],["Oil kingdom","Saudi Arabia holds some of the world’s largest oil reserves, which turned it from a poor desert kingdom into a wealthy modern state."]],
l:"Saudi Arabia is a country in Western Asia covering most of the Arabian Peninsula, between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf. About 35 million people live there, and its capital is Riyadh. It is the birthplace of Islam and home to its two holiest cities, Mecca and Medina. An absolute monarchy ruled by the Al Saud family since its founding in 1932, it became immensely rich after oil was discovered in 1938, and it is now trying to build an economy beyond oil.",
s:[["Land and climate","Most of Saudi Arabia is desert or dry plateau, with no permanent rivers or lakes. The Rub’ al Khali, or Empty Quarter, in the south is the largest continuous sand desert in the world, with dunes up to about 250 meters high. Mountains run along the Red Sea coast. Summers are extremely hot, often over 45 °C, and rain is rare."],["Water","With no rivers, Saudi Arabia relies on underground water, much of it ancient and not refilled, and on desalination, removing salt from seawater. It is one of the largest producers of desalinated water in the world. In the past, desert peoples survived around oases, where springs allowed date palms and crops to grow."],["Cities","Riyadh, in the center of the country, is the capital and largest city, with about 7 million people. Jeddah, on the Red Sea, is the main port and the gateway for pilgrims to Mecca. Dammam and Dhahran on the Gulf coast are centers of the oil industry."],["People","Most Saudi citizens are Arabs and Sunni Muslims, with a Shia minority in the east. Millions of foreign workers, from South and Southeast Asia, the Middle East and Africa, make up more than a third of the population and do much of the work in construction, services and homes. Arabic is the official language."]],
k:[["Capital","Riyadh"],["Population","About 35 million (2024)"],["Area","About 2.15 million km²"],["Highest point","Jabal Sawda, about 3,000 m"],["Currency","Saudi riyal"]],
d:[["c. 570","The Prophet Muhammad is born in Mecca"],["622","The Hijra: Muhammad moves to Medina"],["1744","An alliance between Muhammad ibn Saud and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab"],["1932","Ibn Saud founds the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia"],["1938","Oil is discovered at Dammam"],["2016","The Vision 2030 plan is launched"],["2018","Women are allowed to drive"]],
s2:[["Before Islam","For thousands of years, the Arabian Peninsula was home to nomadic Bedouin herders and to trading towns along caravan routes that carried frankincense, myrrh and spices. The Nabataeans carved the rock city of Hegra, now called Al-Ula, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and Saudi Arabia’s first."],["The birth of Islam","Around 610 CE, in Mecca, the Prophet Muhammad began receiving the revelations that Muslims believe form the Quran. In 622 he moved to Medina, an event called the Hijra, which marks the start of the Islamic calendar. By his death in 632, most of Arabia had accepted Islam, which then spread rapidly across the world."],["The Saudi state","In 1744 Muhammad ibn Saud, a local ruler, allied with the religious reformer Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab, whose strict interpretation of Islam shaped the state. After two earlier Saudi states rose and fell, Abdulaziz ibn Saud captured Riyadh in 1902 and united most of the peninsula, proclaiming the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia in 1932."],["Oil","American geologists struck oil at Dammam in 1938. Saudi Aramco, now owned by the state, became one of the most valuable companies in the world, and Saudi Arabia became a leading member of OPEC, the group of oil-exporting countries. Oil wealth paid for roads, cities, schools, hospitals and generous welfare for citizens."],["Government","Saudi Arabia is an absolute monarchy with no elected parliament, ruled by kings who are sons and grandsons of Ibn Saud. The king is also Custodian of the Two Holy Mosques. Political parties are banned, and human rights groups criticize restrictions on free speech, the treatment of migrant workers and the use of the death penalty."],["Vision 2030","In 2016 Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman launched Vision 2030, a plan to reduce dependence on oil by developing tourism, entertainment, mining, manufacturing and renewable energy. Cinemas reopened in 2018, women were allowed to drive the same year, and giant projects such as the new city of NEOM are under construction, though many are delayed and debated."]],
sp:["The Hajj","Every year, in the Islamic month of Dhu al-Hijjah, about 2 million Muslims from around the world gather in Mecca for the Hajj, a pilgrimage every Muslim who is able must make at least once. Pilgrims wear simple white clothes, walk seven times around the Kaaba in the Grand Mosque and stand in prayer on the plain of Arafat. Managing such huge crowds in the heat is an enormous task, and the Grand Mosque has been greatly expanded."],
g:[["Peninsula","Land surrounded by water on three sides"],["Bedouin","Nomadic Arab herders of the desert"],["Hajj","The pilgrimage to Mecca"],["Desalination","Removing salt from seawater to make fresh water"],["OPEC","The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries"]],
f:["Saudi Arabia has no permanent rivers.","The Empty Quarter is the largest continuous sand desert in the world.","Saudi Arabia is one of the world’s largest producers of desalinated water."],
fun:"Saudi Arabia is one of the largest countries in the world with no permanent rivers.",
tl:"Ang Saudi Arabia ay sumasakop sa halos buong Tangway ng Arabia. Dito isinilang ang Islam at matatagpuan ang Mecca at Medina, at yumaman ito dahil sa langis na natuklasan noong 1938."});

