ADDART({id:"turkeycountry",t:"Türkiye",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🧿",w:"Turkey",r:["byzant","ottoman","rome","crusades","silk","quake"],
p:[["Between two continents","Türkiye spans Europe and Asia: a small part lies in southeastern Europe, and the large peninsula of Anatolia lies in Asia."],["Crossroads of empires","Hittites, Greeks, Romans, Byzantines and Ottomans all ruled here, leaving ruins and monuments across the land."],["A modern republic","Founded in 1923 by Mustafa Kemal Atatürk from the heart of the Ottoman Empire, Türkiye became a secular republic."]],
l:"Türkiye, also known as Turkey, is a country that bridges Europe and Asia, with coasts on the Black Sea, the Aegean and the Mediterranean. About 85 million people live there. Its capital is Ankara, and its largest city, Istanbul, straddles the Bosporus strait between two continents. For centuries the center of the Byzantine and Ottoman empires, Türkiye became a republic in 1923 and is today a major regional power with a large and varied economy.",
s:[["Land","Most of Türkiye is Anatolia, a high plateau ringed by mountains, the Pontic Mountains along the Black Sea and the Taurus in the south. In the far east, Mount Ararat, a dormant volcano, rises to 5,137 m. The Tigris and Euphrates rivers, which watered ancient Mesopotamia, begin in eastern Türkiye."],["Earthquakes","Türkiye sits where the Anatolian Plate is squeezed between the Eurasian, African and Arabian plates, so earthquakes are frequent. In February 2023, two huge earthquakes struck the southeast and northern Syria, killing more than 50,000 people in Türkiye, one of the deadliest disasters in its history."],["Istanbul","Istanbul, once called Byzantium and then Constantinople, has been a capital for more than 1,500 years. With about 16 million people, it is one of the largest cities in Europe. Its skyline is crowded with domes and minarets, including Hagia Sophia, the Blue Mosque and the Topkapı Palace of the Ottoman sultans."],["People and language","Turkish, the official language, is written in the Latin alphabet, adopted in 1928. Most people are Turks, and Kurds are the largest minority, mostly in the southeast. The great majority of Turks are Muslim, but the republic was founded as a secular state, separating religion from government."]],
k:[["Capital","Ankara"],["Population","About 85 million (2024)"],["Area","About 784,000 km²"],["Highest point","Mount Ararat, 5,137 m"],["Currency","Turkish lira"]],
d:[["c. 9600 BCE","Göbekli Tepe, one of the oldest known monumental sites, is built"],["330","Constantine makes Constantinople the capital of the Roman Empire"],["1071","The Seljuk Turks win the Battle of Manzikert"],["1453","The Ottomans capture Constantinople"],["1923","The Republic of Turkey is proclaimed"],["1928","The Latin alphabet replaces Arabic script"],["2023","Devastating earthquakes strike the southeast"]],
s2:[["Ancient Anatolia","Anatolia has some of the oldest human settlements. Göbekli Tepe, with carved stone pillars about 11,600 years old, is older than Stonehenge or the pyramids. Çatalhöyük was one of the first towns. The Hittites built an empire around 1600 BCE, and Greek cities lined the Aegean coast, among them Troy, Ephesus and Miletus."],["Byzantium","In 330 CE the Roman emperor Constantine made Byzantium, renamed Constantinople, the capital of the Roman Empire. After the western empire fell, the Byzantine Empire, Greek-speaking and Christian, lasted another thousand years. The emperor Justinian built Hagia Sophia in 537, for centuries the largest church in the world."],["The Turks arrive","Turkic peoples from Central Asia migrated west, and in 1071 the Seljuk Turks defeated the Byzantines at Manzikert and settled in Anatolia. In the late 1200s a small state led by Osman founded the Ottoman dynasty, which grew rapidly. In 1453 Sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople, ending the Byzantine Empire."],["The Ottoman Empire","At its height under Suleiman the Magnificent in the 1500s, the Ottoman Empire ruled southeastern Europe, the Middle East and North Africa. It was a multiethnic, multireligious empire. It slowly declined in the 1800s, lost territory, and joined the losing side in World War I. During the war, the deportations and mass killings of Armenians, recognized by many countries and scholars as a genocide, killed between about 600,000 and 1.5 million people."],["Atatürk’s republic","After World War I, Mustafa Kemal, a war hero, led a war of independence and founded the Republic of Turkey in 1923, with Ankara as capital. Later called Atatürk, father of the Turks, he abolished the sultanate and caliphate, gave women the vote, adopted the Latin alphabet and Western laws and dress, and made the state secular. His portrait still hangs in offices and schools."],["Modern Türkiye","Türkiye joined NATO in 1952 and became a candidate to join the European Union. It has a large economy, producing cars, textiles, appliances, steel and food, and it is one of the world’s most visited countries. Its politics have often been turbulent, with military coups in the past, conflict with Kurdish militants, and debate about democracy and the role of religion. In 2022 the United Nations began using the name Türkiye at the country’s request."]],
sp:["Hagia Sophia","Completed in 537 under the Byzantine emperor Justinian, Hagia Sophia, meaning holy wisdom, was the largest church in the world for nearly a thousand years. Its enormous dome, more than 30 meters across, seems to float above a ring of windows. After 1453 the Ottomans turned it into a mosque and added minarets; in 1934 the republic made it a museum, and in 2020 it became a mosque again, while remaining open to visitors."],
g:[["Anatolia","The large peninsula that makes up most of Türkiye"],["Bosporus","The narrow strait in Istanbul that links the Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara and separates Europe from Asia"],["Sultan","The ruler of the Ottoman Empire"],["Secular","Not connected with religion"],["Caliphate","A state led by a caliph, regarded as a successor to the Prophet Muhammad"]],
f:["Istanbul is the largest city in the world that lies on two continents.","Göbekli Tepe is older than Stonehenge and the Egyptian pyramids.","Tulips were grown in the Ottoman Empire before they became famous in the Netherlands."],
fun:"Istanbul lies on two continents, Europe and Asia.",
tl:"Ang Türkiye ay bansang nag-uugnay sa Europa at Asya. Dito naging sentro ang mga imperyong Bisantino at Otomano, at ang lungsod ng Istanbul ay nasa dalawang kontinente."});

ADDART({id:"nzcountry",t:"New Zealand",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🥝",w:"New Zealand",r:["pacific","volc","democ","birds","penguin","continents"],
p:[["Islands in the South Pacific","New Zealand is made up of two main islands, the North Island and the South Island, and hundreds of smaller ones, about 2,000 kilometers southeast of Australia."],["The last land settled","Polynesian voyagers, ancestors of the Māori, arrived about 750 years ago, making New Zealand one of the last large lands on Earth to be settled by people."],["Mountains, volcanoes and birds","Its landscapes range from glaciers and fjords to volcanoes and hot springs, and it is home to unusual birds such as the kiwi."]],
l:"New Zealand, called Aotearoa in Māori, is an island country in the southwestern Pacific Ocean. About 5.3 million people live there, and its capital is Wellington, while Auckland is the largest city. Settled by Polynesian voyagers, the Māori, about 750 years ago and later by British colonists, it is today a parliamentary democracy known for its spectacular scenery, its unique wildlife and its pioneering social reforms, including being the first country to give women the vote.",
s:[["Land","The South Island is dominated by the Southern Alps, with Aoraki, or Mount Cook, at 3,724 m, glaciers, lakes and the deep fjords of Fiordland, such as Milford Sound. The North Island has volcanoes, including Ruapehu and Tongariro, and the geothermal area around Rotorua, with geysers, bubbling mud pools and hot springs."],["Shaking islands","New Zealand straddles the boundary between the Pacific and Australian plates, so it has many earthquakes and active volcanoes. The Alpine Fault runs along the South Island, and an earthquake in 2011 badly damaged the city of Christchurch, killing 185 people. Most of New Zealand rises from Zealandia, a mostly drowned continent."],["Wildlife","Isolated for about 80 million years, New Zealand had no land mammals except bats before people arrived. Birds filled their roles, and many became flightless, such as the kiwi, the national symbol, the kākāpō, a heavy, flightless parrot, and the giant moa, hunted to extinction. Introduced rats, stoats and possums threaten native species, and the country aims to remove them."],["People","About 70 percent of New Zealanders have European ancestry, mostly British, and about 17 percent are Māori. Many Pacific Islanders and Asians also live there, and Auckland has the largest Polynesian population of any city. English, Māori and New Zealand Sign Language are official languages."]],
k:[["Capital","Wellington"],["Population","About 5.3 million (2024)"],["Area","About 270,000 km²"],["Highest point","Aoraki/Mount Cook, 3,724 m"],["Currency","New Zealand dollar"]],
d:[["c. 1280","Polynesian voyagers settle the islands"],["1642","Abel Tasman becomes the first European to sight New Zealand"],["1769","James Cook maps the coast"],["1840","The Treaty of Waitangi is signed"],["1893","New Zealand women win the right to vote"],["1953","Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay climb Everest"],["1987","New Zealand bans nuclear-armed and nuclear-powered ships"]],
s2:[["The Māori","Polynesian navigators sailed to New Zealand in double-hulled canoes around 1250 to 1300, guided by stars, currents and birds. Their descendants, the Māori, adapted to a cooler land, hunting moa and growing kūmara, sweet potatoes. They lived in tribes called iwi, built fortified villages called pā and developed rich traditions of carving, weaving, tattooing called tā moko and oratory."],["Europeans arrive","The Dutch explorer Abel Tasman sighted the coast in 1642, and the British captain James Cook mapped it in 1769. Whalers, traders and missionaries followed in the early 1800s. Muskets traded to Māori tribes led to deadly wars among them, the Musket Wars."],["The Treaty of Waitangi","In 1840 British officials and more than 500 Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi. The English and Māori versions differed in important ways, especially about who held sovereignty. British settlers poured in, and wars in the 1860s and land confiscations took much Māori land. Since 1975 the Waitangi Tribunal has investigated breaches of the treaty, leading to apologies and settlements."],["A pioneering democracy","New Zealand often led the world in reform. In 1893, after a campaign led by Kate Sheppard, it became the first self-governing country to give women the vote. It introduced old-age pensions in 1898 and a broad welfare state in the 1930s. In 1987 it declared itself nuclear-free, banning nuclear weapons and nuclear-powered ships."],["Economy","New Zealand’s economy depends heavily on farming and food exports: milk powder, butter, meat, wool, kiwifruit and wine. There are more than four times as many sheep as people, though their number has fallen. Tourism, drawn by its scenery and by films such as The Lord of the Rings, filmed there, is also important."],["Culture and sport","Māori culture is a central part of New Zealand’s identity, and the Māori language has been revived through schools and broadcasting. Rugby is the national passion, and the national team, the All Blacks, perform a haka, a Māori challenge dance, before matches. New Zealand’s Edmund Hillary, with Tenzing Norgay, was the first to climb Mount Everest in 1953."]],
sp:["Votes for women","In 1893 New Zealand became the first self-governing country in the world in which women won the right to vote in national elections. Campaigners led by Kate Sheppard gathered signatures on huge petitions, the largest of which, presented to Parliament in 1893, was nearly 300 meters long with about 25,000 names. Weeks later, women, including Māori women, voted. Kate Sheppard now appears on New Zealand’s 10-dollar note."],
g:[["Aotearoa","The Māori name for New Zealand, often translated as land of the long white cloud"],["Iwi","A Māori tribe"],["Fjord","A long, deep inlet of the sea between steep cliffs, carved by glaciers"],["Haka","A Māori ceremonial dance or challenge"],["Treaty of Waitangi","The 1840 agreement between the British Crown and Māori chiefs"]],
f:["New Zealand was the first country to give women the vote in national elections, in 1893.","Before humans arrived, New Zealand had no land mammals except bats.","Wellington is the southernmost capital city of any country."],
fun:"Wellington is the southernmost national capital in the world.",
tl:"Ang New Zealand o Aotearoa ay bansang pulo sa timog-kanlurang Pasipiko. Ito ang unang bansang nagbigay ng karapatang bumoto sa kababaihan, at kilala sa kamangha-manghang tanawin at mga ibong gaya ng kiwi."});

