ADDART({id:"pngcountry",t:"Papua New Guinea",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦜",w:"Papua New Guinea",r:["australiacountry","indonesiacountry","langevo","trees","butterfly"],
p:[["Land of many languages","More than 800 languages are spoken in Papua New Guinea, more than in any other country."],["Mountains and rainforest","Rugged mountains, rainforests and swamps cover much of the land, and many villages can be reached only on foot or by plane."],["Ancient farmers","People in the highlands began farming about 10,000 years ago, one of the earliest farming centers in the world."]],
l:"Papua New Guinea is a country in Oceania that occupies the eastern half of the island of New Guinea, the world’s second-largest island, along with about 600 smaller islands. About 10 million people live there, making it the most populous Pacific island nation. Its capital is Port Moresby. With more than 800 languages and hundreds of distinct cultures, it is the most linguistically diverse country on Earth. It became independent from Australia in 1975.",
s:[["Geography","A spine of high mountains runs across the mainland, rising to Mount Wilhelm at 4,509 meters. Great rivers such as the Sepik and the Fly wind through rainforests and swamps to the sea. Islands such as New Britain, New Ireland and Bougainville lie to the east, and the country sits on the Pacific Ring of Fire, with active volcanoes."],["First peoples","People reached New Guinea at least 50,000 years ago, when low sea levels joined it to Australia. At Kuk Swamp in the highlands, archaeologists have found ditches showing that people were growing plants such as bananas and taro about 7,000 to 10,000 years ago, independently of farmers elsewhere."],["Colonial history","In the 1880s Germany took the northeast of the mainland and Britain the southeast, which passed to Australia in 1906. After World War I, Australia also governed the former German part. During World War II, fierce battles between Japanese and Allied forces were fought along the Kokoda Track and on the islands."],["Independence","Papua New Guinea became independent on 16 September 1975 as a parliamentary democracy within the Commonwealth, with the British monarch as head of state. A long conflict on Bougainville, from 1988 to 1998, ended with a peace agreement, and in a 2019 referendum about 98 percent of Bougainvilleans voted for independence, a question still being negotiated."]],
k:[["Capital","Port Moresby"],["Population","About 10 million"],["Languages","Tok Pisin, English, Hiri Motu and more than 800 local languages"],["Highest point","Mount Wilhelm, 4,509 m"],["Independence","16 September 1975, from Australia"]],
d:[["c. 50,000 years ago","First people arrive"],["c. 7000 BCE","Farming develops at Kuk Swamp"],["1884","Germany and Britain divide the eastern half of New Guinea"],["1942","Kokoda Track campaign in World War II"],["1975","Independence from Australia"],["2019","Bougainville votes for independence in a referendum"]],
s2:[["Tok Pisin","With so many languages, people need a common one. Tok Pisin, a creole that grew from English, German, Malay and local languages, is spoken by millions and used in parliament and the media. Many words are built from English in clever ways: a hospital is haus sik, sick house, and thank you very much is tenkyu tru."],["Village life","Most people live in rural villages and grow much of their own food in gardens of sweet potato, taro, yams and bananas. Land is usually owned by clans rather than individuals. Pigs are valued highly and are given at weddings, funerals and peace ceremonies."],["Sing-sings","At festivals called sing-sings, groups from different areas gather to dance and sing in spectacular dress, with face paint, shells and feathers from birds of paradise. The Goroka and Mount Hagen shows in the highlands are the most famous, bringing together dozens of groups."],["Birds of paradise","New Guinea is home to most of the world’s birds of paradise, whose males have dazzling plumes and perform elaborate dances to attract females. The raggiana bird of paradise appears on the national flag. The country also has tree kangaroos, cassowaries and the Queen Alexandra’s birdwing, the world’s largest butterfly."],["Resources","Papua New Guinea has gold, copper, oil and natural gas, as well as forests and fisheries. Mines and gas projects bring in much of its export income, but they have also caused pollution and disputes over land. The country is working to share the benefits more widely and to protect its forests."],["Challenges and hope","Papua New Guinea faces challenges such as poor roads, limited health care and violence in some areas. Yet its young population, rich cultures and natural resources give it great potential. Rugby league is the national passion, and the country is set to have its own team in Australia’s top competition from 2028."]],
sp:["Over 800 languages","Papua New Guinea has more than 800 living languages, about 12 percent of all the languages in the world, though it has only about one-tenth of one percent of the world’s people. Many are spoken by only a few thousand people living in neighboring valleys. Mountains, rivers and swamps kept groups apart for thousands of years, letting their languages grow very different from each other."],
g:[["Creole","A language that develops from a mix of languages"],["Clan","A large group of families who share an ancestor"],["Sing-sing","A festival gathering with traditional dancing and song"],["Referendum","A vote by the people on a single question"]],
f:["Papua New Guinea has more languages than any other country.","Its flag shows a bird of paradise.","Farming began in its highlands thousands of years ago."],
fun:"Papua New Guinea has more than 800 languages, more than any other country.",
tl:"Ang Papua New Guinea ay bansa sa silangang bahagi ng pulo ng New Guinea. Mahigit 800 wika ang ginagamit dito, higit sa alinmang bansa. Naging malaya ito mula sa Australia noong 1975."});

ADDART({id:"fijicountry",t:"Fiji",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏝️",w:"Fiji",r:["pacific","reef","climchange","nzcountry","australiacountry"],
p:[["An island nation","Fiji is made up of more than 330 islands in the South Pacific, about a third of them inhabited."],["A meeting place","Fiji lies where Melanesian and Polynesian cultures meet, and it has a large community with roots in India."],["Rugby champions","Fiji’s rugby sevens team won Olympic gold in 2016 and again at the Tokyo Games held in 2021; the first gold was also Fiji’s first-ever Olympic medal."]],
l:"Fiji is an island country in Melanesia, in the South Pacific Ocean, about 2,000 kilometers north of New Zealand. Its more than 330 islands are home to about 900,000 people, most of whom live on the two largest islands, Viti Levu and Vanua Levu. The capital is Suva. Fiji was a British colony from 1874 to 1970. Known for coral reefs, beaches and friendly greetings of bula, it is one of the most developed Pacific island economies.",
s:[["Islands and reefs","Viti Levu and Vanua Levu are volcanic islands with mountains, rainforests and rivers. Many smaller islands are coral atolls or reef-fringed islands. Fiji’s coral reefs, including the Great Sea Reef, one of the largest barrier reef systems in the world, are home to soft corals so colorful that Fiji is called the soft coral capital of the world."],["First Fijians","People of the Lapita culture, known for their decorated pottery, reached Fiji about 3,000 years ago. Over centuries, Fijian society developed chiefdoms, great double-hulled canoes and trade with Tonga and Samoa. Chiefs still play an important role in village life and land ownership today."],["Colonial Fiji","European traders and missionaries arrived in the 1800s. In 1874 the high chief Seru Epenisa Cakobau and other chiefs gave Fiji to Britain. To work on sugar plantations, the British brought about 60,000 laborers from India between 1879 and 1916 under a system called indenture. Many stayed, and their descendants form a large part of Fiji’s people."],["Independence and coups","Fiji became independent on 10 October 1970. Tensions over political power between Indigenous Fijians and Indo-Fijians led to military coups in 1987, 2000 and 2006. A new constitution in 2013 gave all citizens the common name Fijian. In 2022 an election brought a peaceful change of government."]],
k:[["Capital","Suva"],["Population","About 900,000"],["Islands","More than 330, about 110 inhabited"],["Languages","English, iTaukei (Fijian), Fiji Hindi"],["Independence","10 October 1970, from the United Kingdom"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","Lapita settlers arrive"],["1643","Dutch explorer Abel Tasman sights the islands"],["1874","Fiji becomes a British colony"],["1879","First indentured laborers arrive from India"],["1970","Independence"],["2016","Rugby sevens team wins Fiji’s first Olympic gold"]],
s2:[["Kava","Kava, called yaqona in Fiji, is a drink made from the ground root of a pepper plant mixed with water. It is shared from a wooden bowl at ceremonies to welcome guests, settle disputes and mark important events. Visitors to villages traditionally bring a gift of kava roots for the chief."],["Indo-Fijian culture","About a third of Fiji’s people are Indo-Fijians. Hindu temples, mosques, Diwali celebrations and curries are part of everyday Fijian life. Fiji Hindi, a language developed from several Indian languages, is spoken alongside English and iTaukei."],["Rugby","Rugby is Fiji’s national passion, played in villages, schools and on beaches. The rugby sevens team, famous for fast, skillful attacking play, won Olympic gold in Rio de Janeiro in 2016 and again in Tokyo in 2021. Many Fijian players star for professional clubs in Europe, Australia and New Zealand."],["Climate change","Rising seas, stronger cyclones and coral bleaching threaten Fiji. Some coastal villages, such as Vunidogoloa, have already been moved to higher ground. In 2017 Fiji led the United Nations climate talks, COP23, and it has been a strong voice for small island nations."],["Tourism and sugar","Tourism is Fiji’s biggest industry, with visitors drawn to resorts, diving and the Mamanuca and Yasawa islands. Sugar was long the main export, and Fiji Water, bottled from an underground source on Viti Levu, is sold around the world. Fiji is also a center for regional organizations such as the University of the South Pacific."],["Fire walking","On the island of Beqa, members of the Sawau tribe perform vilavilairevo, walking barefoot over hot stones heated in a fire pit. Legend says a spirit gave the power to a warrior of the tribe in return for his freedom. Indo-Fijian Hindus also hold fire-walking ceremonies as part of their festivals."]],
sp:["Bula!","Bula is the Fijian greeting, meaning life and wishing good health. Visitors to Fiji hear it everywhere, and it is often shouted with a big smile. On Fridays, many Fijians wear bula shirts, colorful patterned shirts, to work. Vinaka means thank you, and moce is goodbye."],
g:[["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral island around a lagoon"],["Indenture","A labor contract binding workers for a fixed number of years"],["Kava","A traditional drink made from a pepper plant root"],["Coup","A sudden, illegal seizure of power"]],
f:["Fiji won its first Olympic medal, a gold, in rugby sevens in 2016.","It has more than 330 islands.","The 180th meridian passes through Fiji."],
fun:"The 180° line of longitude passes through Fiji, though the International Date Line bends around it.",
tl:"Ang Fiji ay bansang may mahigit 330 pulo sa Timog Pasipiko. Kilala ito sa mga bahura, sa rugby at sa pagbating bula. Naging malaya ito mula sa Britanya noong 1970."});

ADDART({id:"moroccocountry",t:"Morocco",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🏺",w:"Morocco",r:["sahara","islam","spaincountry","med","arabic"],
p:[["Africa’s northwest corner","Morocco lies just 14 kilometers from Europe across the Strait of Gibraltar, with coasts on both the Atlantic and the Mediterranean."],["Amazigh and Arab","Its people and culture blend Amazigh (Berber), Arab, African and Andalusian traditions."],["Old cities","Cities such as Fez and Marrakesh have walled old towns called medinas, with markets that have bustled for centuries."]],
l:"Morocco is a country in North Africa with a population of about 37 million. Its capital is Rabat and its largest city is Casablanca. The Atlas Mountains run through its center, separating fertile coastal plains from the Sahara Desert. Morocco is a kingdom ruled by the Alaouite dynasty since the 17th century. It gained independence from France and Spain in 1956. Its cuisine, crafts and historic cities make it one of Africa’s most visited countries.",
s:[["Mountains and desert","The High Atlas, Middle Atlas and Anti-Atlas mountains cross Morocco, with Jbel Toubkal reaching 4,167 meters, the highest peak in North Africa. To the north, the Rif Mountains rise above the Mediterranean. In the south and east, the land gives way to the Sahara, with its great sand dunes at Erg Chebbi."],["The Amazigh","The Amazigh, often called Berbers, are the original people of North Africa. Their languages, known as Tamazight, are spoken by millions of Moroccans, and in 2011 Tamazight became an official language beside Arabic. Their villages of mud-brick kasbahs and their weaving and jewelry are famous."],["Dynasties and empires","Arab armies brought Islam in the late 600s. The Idrisid dynasty founded Fez around 789. Later, the Almoravid and Almohad dynasties built empires that stretched from West Africa to Spain, where Muslims ruled al-Andalus for centuries. The Alaouite dynasty, which still rules, came to power in the 1660s."],["Colony and kingdom","In 1912 France made most of Morocco a protectorate, while Spain took zones in the north and south. After years of resistance, led partly by Sultan Mohammed V, Morocco became independent in 1956. King Hassan II ruled from 1961 to 1999, and King Mohammed VI has reigned since, under a constitution revised in 2011."]],
k:[["Capital","Rabat"],["Largest city","Casablanca"],["Population","About 37 million"],["Languages","Arabic and Tamazight (official); French widely used"],["Independence","1956, from France and Spain"]],
d:[["711","Tariq ibn Ziyad leads an army from Morocco into Spain"],["c. 789","Idrisid dynasty founds Fez"],["859","Al-Qarawiyyin is founded in Fez"],["1912","French and Spanish protectorates established"],["1956","Independence"],["2022","Morocco reaches the football World Cup semifinals"]],
s2:[["Fez and Marrakesh","The medina of Fez, with about 9,000 lanes, is one of the largest car-free urban areas in the world, where donkeys still carry goods. Marrakesh, the Red City, is famous for the square of Jemaa el-Fna, where storytellers, musicians, snake charmers and food stalls gather every evening."],["The oldest university","Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez was founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri, the daughter of a wealthy merchant. UNESCO and Guinness World Records describe it as the oldest existing, continually operating educational institution in the world. Its library holds ancient manuscripts, some more than 1,000 years old."],["Food","Moroccan cooking is known for tagines, stews slow-cooked in clay pots with cone-shaped lids, and couscous, traditionally served on Fridays. Dishes often combine meat with fruit such as prunes or apricots and spices such as cumin, saffron and cinnamon. Sweet mint tea, poured from high above the glass, is a sign of hospitality."],["Crafts","Moroccan artisans are famous for zellige, colorful tiles cut by hand into geometric patterns, carved cedar wood, brass lamps and leather. At the old tanneries of Fez, leather is still dyed in stone vats in colors such as red, yellow and blue, as it has been for centuries."],["Energy and economy","Morocco exports phosphates, used for fertilizer, and has the world’s largest reserves of them. It also makes cars, grows fruit and vegetables for Europe and welcomes millions of tourists. The Noor Ouarzazate complex in the desert is one of the world’s largest concentrated solar power plants."],["Football","Football is Morocco’s favorite sport. At the 2022 World Cup in Qatar, Morocco became the first African and first Arab country to reach the semifinals, beating Spain and Portugal on the way. Morocco is set to co-host the 2030 World Cup with Spain and Portugal."]],
sp:["Western Sahara","Morocco controls most of Western Sahara, a desert territory to its south that Spain left in 1975. The Polisario Front, a movement of the Sahrawi people, seeks independence for the territory and fought a war with Morocco until a ceasefire in 1991. The United Nations considers it a non-self-governing territory, and its final status remains disputed between Morocco and the Polisario Front."],
g:[["Medina","The old walled part of a North African city"],["Amazigh","The Indigenous people of North Africa, also called Berbers"],["Protectorate","A territory controlled and protected by a stronger state"],["Tagine","A slow-cooked stew and the clay pot it is cooked in"]],
f:["Al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, founded in 859, is often called the world’s oldest university.","Morocco has the world’s largest phosphate reserves.","It was the first African team to reach a World Cup semifinal."],
fun:"The oldest continually operating university in the world, Al-Qarawiyyin, was founded by a woman, Fatima al-Fihri.",
tl:"Ang Morocco ay kaharian sa hilagang-kanlurang Africa, malapit sa Espanya. Kilala ito sa kabundukang Atlas, sa disyertong Sahara, sa mga lumang lungsod gaya ng Fez at Marrakesh, at sa pagkaing tagine."});

