ADDART({id:"ghanacountry",t:"Ghana",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🥁",w:"Ghana",r:["nkrumah","decol","trade","nigeriacountry","choco"],
p:[["First to independence","In 1957 Ghana became the first colony in sub-Saharan Africa to win independence, led by Kwame Nkrumah."],["The Gold Coast","Once called the Gold Coast, it has been famous for its gold for more than a thousand years."],["Cocoa country","Ghana is one of the world’s largest producers of cocoa, the bean used to make chocolate."]],
l:"Ghana is a country on the Gulf of Guinea in West Africa, with about 34 million people. Its capital and largest city is Accra. Formerly the British colony of the Gold Coast, it became independent in 1957, the first sub-Saharan African colony to do so, and took its name from the ancient Ghana Empire. Ghana is known for its gold, cocoa, kente cloth, music and a stable democracy in which power has changed hands peacefully several times through elections.",
s:[["Land and people","Ghana stretches from sandy Atlantic beaches through tropical forest to dry savanna in the north. Lake Volta, created by the Akosombo Dam in 1965, is one of the largest artificial lakes in the world by area. Ghana’s people belong to many groups, including the Akan, Ewe, Mole-Dagbani and Ga, and speak more than 70 languages, with English as the official language."],["The Asante Kingdom","From the late 1600s, the Asante (Ashanti) Kingdom, centered on Kumasi, grew rich from gold and trade and became one of the most powerful states in West Africa. Its symbol is the Golden Stool, believed to hold the soul of the Asante nation. The Asante fought several wars against the British before their kingdom was made part of the colony in 1902."],["Forts and the slave trade","From the 1400s, Portuguese, Dutch, British, Danish and other Europeans built about 40 forts and castles along the coast to trade in gold and, later, enslaved people. Elmina Castle and Cape Coast Castle, now World Heritage Sites, held captives in dark dungeons before they were shipped across the Atlantic through the Door of No Return."],["Independence","Kwame Nkrumah led the campaign for self-rule, and on 6 March 1957 the Gold Coast became independent as Ghana. Its independence inspired movements across Africa. After Nkrumah was overthrown in 1966, Ghana went through several military governments, but since a new constitution in 1992 it has held regular, peaceful elections."]],
k:[["Capital","Accra"],["Population","About 34 million"],["Official language","English; Akan (Twi), Ewe, Dagbani and others widely spoken"],["Independence","6 March 1957, from the United Kingdom"],["Main exports","Gold, cocoa, oil"]],
d:[["1482","Portuguese build Elmina Castle"],["c. 1701","Osei Tutu unites the Asante Kingdom"],["1874","Britain declares the Gold Coast Colony"],["1957","Ghana becomes independent"],["1965","Akosombo Dam creates Lake Volta"],["1992","New constitution restores multiparty democracy"]],
s2:[["Kente cloth","Kente is a brightly colored cloth woven in narrow strips by Asante and Ewe weavers, then sewn together. Each pattern and color has a name and meaning: gold for wealth, green for growth, black for maturity. Once worn only by royalty, kente is now worn at celebrations around the world as a symbol of African heritage."],["Adinkra symbols","Adinkra are Akan symbols printed on cloth and carved on buildings, each expressing a proverb or idea. Sankofa, a bird looking backward, means go back and fetch it, reminding people to learn from the past. Gye Nyame, meaning except God, is one of the most popular and appears on Ghanaian banknotes and buildings."],["Cocoa","Cocoa was brought to the Gold Coast in the 1870s, and Ghana soon became the world’s largest producer. Today it is second only to neighboring Côte d’Ivoire. Most cocoa is grown by small family farmers. Ghana is working to process more of its own beans into chocolate and to end child labor on farms."],["Music and food","Highlife, which blends West African rhythms with jazz and guitar music, was born in Ghana in the early 1900s. Today hiplife and Afrobeats artists are popular worldwide. Favorite foods include jollof rice, fufu, a stretchy dough pounded from cassava and plantain, and kenkey, fermented corn dumplings."],["The Year of Return","In 2019, 400 years after the first enslaved Africans were recorded arriving in English-speaking North America, Ghana invited people of African descent around the world to visit and reconnect with Africa in the Year of Return. Many visited the coastal castles, and some have settled in Ghana."],["Modern Ghana","Ghana has one of West Africa’s larger economies, built on gold, cocoa and, since 2010, offshore oil. It faces challenges such as debt, illegal small-scale gold mining called galamsey, which pollutes rivers, and youth unemployment. Accra is a lively hub for business, tech start-ups and the arts."]],
sp:["Black Star of Africa","Ghana’s flag, adopted in 1957, has red, gold and green bands with a black star in the middle. The red stands for those who died in the struggle for independence, gold for the country’s mineral wealth and green for its forests and farms. The black star, the lodestar of African freedom, gives the national football team its nickname, the Black Stars."],
g:[["Kente","A colorful woven cloth from Ghana"],["Adinkra","Akan symbols that express proverbs and ideas"],["Savanna","Grassland with scattered trees"],["Galamsey","Illegal small-scale gold mining in Ghana"]],
f:["Ghana was the first sub-Saharan African colony to become independent.","Lake Volta is one of the largest artificial lakes in the world.","Ghana is the world’s second-largest cocoa producer."],
fun:"Ghana’s name comes from an ancient empire that lay hundreds of kilometers to the northwest.",
tl:"Ang Ghana sa Kanlurang Africa ang unang kolonya sa timog ng Sahara na naging malaya, noong 1957. Kilala ito sa ginto, sa kakaw na ginagawang tsokolate, at sa makukulay na telang kente."});

ADDART({id:"drccountry",t:"Democratic Republic of the Congo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦍",w:"Democratic Republic of the Congo",r:["trees","decol","nile","smartphone","phone"],
p:[["Africa’s second-largest country","The DR Congo covers about 2.3 million square kilometers in the heart of Africa, an area about the size of Western Europe."],["The mighty Congo River","The Congo River, the deepest river in the world, curves through the country to the Atlantic."],["Rich and troubled","Its land holds vast mineral wealth, yet long wars and misrule have left many of its people poor."]],
l:"The Democratic Republic of the Congo, often called the DRC or Congo-Kinshasa, is a country in Central Africa with more than 100 million people, making it the largest French-speaking country in the world by population. Its capital, Kinshasa, is one of Africa’s biggest cities. The DRC contains much of the Congo Basin rainforest, the second-largest rainforest on Earth, and huge deposits of minerals such as cobalt, copper and diamonds.",
s:[["Land and river","The Congo River and its tributaries drain most of the country. At about 4,700 kilometers, the Congo is Africa’s second-longest river and carries more water than any river except the Amazon. In places it is more than 220 meters deep. Rainforest covers the center, savanna the north and south, and the Rwenzori and Virunga mountains rise in the east."],["Ancient kingdoms","Bantu-speaking farmers spread through the region thousands of years ago. The Kingdom of Kongo, near the river’s mouth, traded with Portugal from the 1480s, and its king Afonso I wrote letters to the Portuguese king protesting the slave trade. Inland, the Luba and Lunda kingdoms and the Kuba kingdom were famous for their art and government."],["The Congo Free State","In 1885 King Leopold II of Belgium took the Congo as his own private colony, the Congo Free State. To collect rubber and ivory, his agents forced people to work, took hostages and cut off hands as punishment. Millions died from violence, hunger and disease. International outrage forced Belgium to take over the colony in 1908."],["Independence and conflict","The Congo became independent on 30 June 1960, with Patrice Lumumba as prime minister; he was overthrown and murdered in 1961. Joseph-Désiré Mobutu seized power in 1965 and ruled for 32 years, renaming the country Zaire. After his fall in 1997, wars drew in many neighboring countries and caused millions of deaths. Fighting continues in the east."]],
k:[["Capital","Kinshasa"],["Population","More than 100 million"],["Area","About 2.3 million km²"],["Languages","French (official); Lingala, Swahili, Kikongo, Tshiluba"],["Independence","30 June 1960, from Belgium"]],
d:[["c. 1390","Kingdom of Kongo founded"],["1885","Congo Free State becomes Leopold II’s private colony"],["1908","Belgium takes over the colony"],["1960","Independence; Lumumba becomes prime minister"],["1971","Mobutu renames the country Zaire"],["1997","Mobutu falls; the country becomes the DR Congo again"]],
s2:[["Wildlife","The DRC has five World Heritage natural sites. Virunga National Park, Africa’s oldest national park, founded in 1925, protects mountain gorillas. The okapi, a forest relative of the giraffe with zebra-striped legs, lives only in the DRC. Bonobos, close relatives of chimpanzees and humans, are found only south of the Congo River."],["Minerals","The DRC produces about 70 percent of the world’s cobalt, a metal used in the batteries of phones and electric cars, and is a major source of copper. Its mines also produce coltan, gold and diamonds. But much of this wealth has not reached ordinary people, and some mines use child labor or fund armed groups."],["Congo music","Congolese rumba, mixing Cuban rhythms with local styles and dazzling guitar, spread across Africa from the 1950s through stars such as Franco and Papa Wemba. In 2021 UNESCO added Congolese rumba, shared with the neighboring Republic of the Congo, to its list of the world’s intangible cultural heritage."],["Kinshasa","Kinshasa, on the south bank of the Congo River, faces Brazzaville, capital of the Republic of the Congo, on the opposite bank. They are the closest pair of capital cities in the world apart from Rome and Vatican City. With more than 15 million people, Kinshasa is one of the largest cities in Africa and is famous for its music, art and fashion."],["The Rumble in the Jungle","In 1974 Kinshasa hosted one of the most famous boxing matches in history, the Rumble in the Jungle, in which Muhammad Ali defeated George Foreman to regain the world heavyweight title. Ali’s tactic of leaning on the ropes and letting Foreman tire himself out became known as rope-a-dope."],["The rainforest","The Congo Basin rainforest absorbs huge amounts of carbon dioxide, helping to slow climate change, and its peatlands store billions of metric tons of carbon. Millions of people depend on it for food, medicine and income. Logging, farming and mining threaten it, and the DRC has asked for international support to protect it."]],
sp:["Two Congos","Two countries are named after the Congo River. The Democratic Republic of the Congo, on the south and east, was a Belgian colony and is far larger. The Republic of the Congo, on the north and west, was a French colony and has about 6 million people. To tell them apart, people often call them Congo-Kinshasa and Congo-Brazzaville, after their capitals, which face each other across the river."],
g:[["Tributary","A river or stream that flows into a larger river"],["Cobalt","A metal used in rechargeable batteries"],["Okapi","A forest animal related to the giraffe"],["Intangible heritage","Traditions, music and skills passed down through generations"]],
f:["The Congo is the deepest river in the world.","The DRC produces about 70 percent of the world’s cobalt.","The okapi lives only in the DR Congo."],
fun:"The Congo River is the only major river to cross the equator twice.",
tl:"Ang Democratic Republic of the Congo ay malawak na bansa sa gitnang Africa na dinadaanan ng Ilog Congo. Mayaman ito sa kagubatang ulan at mineral, ngunit dumanas ng mahabang digmaan at kahirapan."});

