ADDART({id:"greatwall",t:"Great Wall of China",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🧱",w:"Great Wall of China",r:["chinacountry","china","silk","mongol"],
p:[["Not one wall","The Great Wall is a network of walls, towers and forts built by many Chinese dynasties over about 2,000 years."],["Guarding the north","It was built to defend Chinese lands against raids by nomadic peoples from the northern steppes."],["A world wonder","Today it is China’s best-known landmark and a UNESCO World Heritage Site visited by millions every year."]],
l:"The Great Wall of China is a series of fortifications stretching across northern China, from the Yellow Sea toward the deserts of the west. Built and rebuilt by many dynasties from the 7th century BCE to the 17th century CE, it includes walls, trenches, watchtowers and natural barriers. A 2012 Chinese survey measured all its sections at about 21,000 kilometers in total. The best-preserved parts, near Beijing, date from the Ming dynasty.",
s:[["Early walls","Chinese states began building defensive walls of rammed earth around the 7th century BCE. After Qin Shi Huang united China in 221 BCE, he ordered many of these walls joined and extended to protect his empire from the Xiongnu, horse-riding nomads of the north. Hundreds of thousands of soldiers, prisoners and peasants did the work."],["Han and later dynasties","The Han dynasty extended the walls far west into the Gobi Desert, protecting trade along the Silk Road. Later dynasties repaired, abandoned or added sections depending on who threatened them. Under the Mongol Yuan dynasty, whose rulers came from the north themselves, the walls mattered much less."],["The Ming wall","The Ming dynasty, which ruled from 1368 to 1644, rebuilt the wall in brick and stone after Mongol raids. Ming walls stand up to about 8 meters high and are wide enough on top for soldiers to march along. Watchtowers, spaced along the wall, sent warnings by smoke signals by day and fire by night."],["Building it","Builders used whatever was nearby: rammed earth and wood in the west, stone in the mountains and fired bricks in the east. Mortar for some Ming sections is said to have included sticky rice, which made it stronger. Many workers died during construction, and legends describe the wall as the longest cemetery on Earth."]],
k:[["Location","Northern China"],["Total length","About 21,000 km including all sections (2012 survey)"],["Main builders","Qin, Han and Ming dynasties"],["Best-known sections","Badaling, Mutianyu and Jinshanling near Beijing"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1987"]],
d:[["c. 7th century BCE","Chinese states begin building defensive walls"],["221 BCE","Qin Shi Huang begins linking walls after uniting China"],["c. 120 BCE","Han dynasty extends walls into the Gobi Desert"],["1368","Ming dynasty begins; major rebuilding follows"],["1644","Manchu forces pass through Shanhai Pass and found the Qing dynasty"],["1987","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["Did it work?","The wall was never a perfect barrier. Raiders sometimes broke through or went around it, and in 1644 a Ming general opened the gates at Shanhai Pass to Manchu armies, who then conquered China. But the wall slowed attackers, controlled trade and travel, and let small garrisons watch long frontiers."],["Life on the wall","Soldiers lived in towers and forts along the wall, often with their families, and farmed the land nearby. Gates and passes worked like customs posts, where officials checked travelers and collected taxes on goods moving between China and Central Asia."],["Seen from space?","A popular myth says the Great Wall is the only human-made object visible from the Moon. It is not: the wall is narrow and similar in color to the land around it. Astronauts in low orbit have said it is very hard to spot with the naked eye, while cities and airports are easier to see."],["Wear and repair","Wind, rain and farming have worn away much of the wall, especially the rammed-earth sections in the west. Some people took bricks to build houses. China passed laws to protect it in 2006, and restoration projects now repair damaged parts while trying to keep their original look."],["Visiting today","The restored section at Badaling, northwest of Beijing, is the most visited and can be very crowded. Mutianyu is greener and quieter, and some hikers prefer wild, unrestored stretches such as Jiankou. At its eastern end, the wall meets the sea at a point known as Old Dragon’s Head."],["A symbol of China","The Great Wall appears on stamps, in poems and in China’s national anthem, which calls on people to build a new Great Wall with their flesh and blood. It is a symbol of the country’s long history and of the enormous effort of the ordinary people who built it."]],
sp:["The Great Wall in numbers","The 2012 survey by China’s State Administration of Cultural Heritage counted more than 43,000 sites linked to the wall, including walls, trenches, towers and forts, across 15 provinces and regions. The Ming wall alone measures about 8,850 kilometers. If all sections were laid end to end, they would stretch about halfway around the Earth."],
g:[["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"],["Rammed earth","Soil packed hard between wooden frames to make walls"],["Nomad","A person who moves from place to place, often with herds"],["Garrison","A group of soldiers stationed to guard a place"]],
f:["All sections of the Great Wall add up to about 21,000 kilometers.","It cannot be seen from the Moon with the naked eye.","Some Ming mortar is said to contain sticky rice."],
fun:"The Great Wall cannot be seen from the Moon with the naked eye, despite the famous myth.",
tl:"Ang Great Wall of China ay serye ng mga pader, tore at kuta sa hilagang Tsina na itinayo ng maraming dinastiya sa loob ng halos 2,000 taon upang ipagtanggol ang bansa. Isa itong World Heritage Site."});

ADDART({id:"tajmahal",t:"Taj Mahal",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🕌",w:"Taj Mahal",r:["indiacountry","islam","indus","greatwall"],
p:[["A monument to love","The Mughal emperor Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal as a tomb for his beloved wife, Mumtaz Mahal."],["White marble","Its shining white marble is inlaid with precious stones in delicate patterns of flowers and verses."],["A jewel of India","It stands on the banks of the Yamuna River in Agra and is one of the most famous buildings in the world."]],
l:"The Taj Mahal is a white marble mausoleum in Agra, in northern India, built by the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died in 1631. Completed around 1653 after more than 20 years of work, it blends Persian, Islamic and Indian styles. Its domed tomb, four minarets, gardens and reflecting pools make it one of the masterpieces of world architecture and a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
s:[["Shah Jahan and Mumtaz","Shah Jahan, the fifth Mughal emperor, married Arjumand Banu Begum, known as Mumtaz Mahal, Chosen One of the Palace. She traveled with him even on military campaigns and bore him 14 children. In 1631 she died giving birth, and the grieving emperor decided to build her a tomb unlike any other."],["Building the Taj","Work began in 1632. About 20,000 craftsmen and laborers came from across India, Persia and Central Asia, and elephants carried marble from Makrana in Rajasthan. The main tomb was finished around 1643, and the mosque, gateway and gardens around 1653. The chief architect is thought to have been Ustad Ahmad Lahauri."],["The design","The tomb stands on a raised marble platform with a minaret at each corner. Its great central dome, shaped like an onion, rises about 73 meters above the ground. The whole complex is almost perfectly symmetrical, and the minarets lean slightly outward so that they would fall away from the tomb in an earthquake."],["Stone flowers","The walls are decorated with pietra dura, or parchin kari: semi-precious stones such as jade, lapis lazuli and carnelian cut and set into the marble to form flowers and vines. Verses from the Qur’an in black marble frame the arches, and the letters grow larger higher up so they appear the same size from below."]],
k:[["Location","Agra, Uttar Pradesh, India"],["Built","c. 1632–1653"],["Commissioned by","Mughal emperor Shah Jahan"],["Height","About 73 m"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1983"]],
d:[["1631","Mumtaz Mahal dies"],["1632","Construction begins"],["c. 1643","Main tomb completed"],["c. 1653","Whole complex completed"],["1666","Shah Jahan dies and is buried beside Mumtaz"],["1983","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["A paradise garden","In front of the tomb lies a charbagh, a Persian-style garden divided into four parts by water channels, representing the four rivers of paradise in Islamic tradition. The long central pool reflects the tomb, a view that appears on countless photographs."],["Changing colors","Because white marble reflects the light around it, the Taj seems to change color through the day: pinkish at dawn, dazzling white at noon and golden at sunset. On moonlit nights it can look silvery blue, and the site opens for night viewing on a few nights each month."],["The emperor’s fate","In 1658 Shah Jahan’s son Aurangzeb seized the throne and confined his father in Agra Fort. Legend says Shah Jahan spent his last years gazing at the Taj from a window. When he died in 1666 he was buried beside Mumtaz, the only break in the building’s perfect symmetry."],["Protecting the marble","Air pollution from traffic and industry has yellowed the marble. India has banned polluting vehicles near the monument and set up a protected zone around Agra. Workers clean the marble with a special clay pack, an old beauty treatment, to draw out the stains."],["Visiting","Millions of people visit the Taj Mahal every year, making it one of India’s most visited sites. It is closed on Fridays, when the mosque is used for prayers. Visitors enter through a great red sandstone gateway, from which the tomb first appears framed in its arch."],["Mughal heritage","The Mughal Empire ruled much of South Asia from the 1500s to the 1700s. Its emperors built many great monuments, including Humayun’s Tomb in Delhi, which inspired the Taj, as well as the Red Fort and Fatehpur Sikri. Together they form one of the world’s richest traditions of architecture."]],
sp:["Tears on the cheek of time","The Bengali poet Rabindranath Tagore described the Taj Mahal as a teardrop on the cheek of time. Shah Jahan himself wrote that the sight of the tomb made people sigh with grief and that the Sun and Moon shed tears from their eyes. The building has inspired poets, painters and travelers for nearly 400 years."],
g:[["Mausoleum","A large, grand tomb"],["Minaret","A tall, slender tower beside a mosque or tomb"],["Pietra dura","Decoration made by setting cut stones into marble"],["Symmetry","Matching exactly on both sides"]],
f:["About 20,000 workers helped build the Taj Mahal.","Its minarets lean slightly outward for safety in earthquakes.","Shah Jahan’s tomb is the only object that breaks its symmetry."],
fun:"The Taj Mahal’s minarets lean slightly outward so they would fall away from the tomb in an earthquake.",
tl:"Ang Taj Mahal ay puting libingang marmol sa Agra, India, na ipinatayo ni Emperador Shah Jahan para sa kanyang asawang si Mumtaz Mahal. Natapos ito noong mga 1653 at isa sa pinakatanyag na gusali sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"pyramids",t:"Pyramids of Giza",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🔺",w:"Giza pyramid complex",r:["egypt","egyptcountry","nile","mesop"],
p:[["Tombs for kings","The pyramids were built about 4,500 years ago as tombs for the pharaohs Khufu, Khafre and Menkaure."],["Record holder","Khufu’s Great Pyramid was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for about 3,800 years."],["The last ancient wonder","It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing."]],
l:"The Pyramids of Giza stand on a desert plateau on the edge of Cairo, Egypt. Built during the Old Kingdom, around 2600 to 2500 BCE, the three great pyramids were tombs for the pharaohs Khufu, Khafre and Menkaure. Beside them lie smaller pyramids for queens, temples, tombs of officials and the Great Sphinx. The Great Pyramid of Khufu is the oldest and only surviving wonder of the ancient world.",
s:[["Why pyramids?","Ancient Egyptians believed their pharaoh was a god-king who would live on after death. His body was mummified and placed in a tomb with everything he would need in the afterlife. Pyramids grew from earlier flat tombs called mastabas, and their shape may represent the rays of the Sun or the mound from which life first rose."],["The Great Pyramid","Khufu’s pyramid was built from about 2.3 million stone blocks weighing on average more than 2 metric tons each. It was originally about 146 meters tall and covered in smooth, white limestone that shone in the Sun. Its sides line up almost exactly with north, south, east and west."],["Khafre and Menkaure","Khufu’s son Khafre built the second pyramid, which looks taller because it stands on higher ground and still has some casing stones at its top. The third, built by Menkaure, is much smaller. Each pyramid had a temple beside it and a causeway leading down to a valley temple near the Nile."],["The Great Sphinx","The Great Sphinx, a huge statue with a lion’s body and a human head, was carved from the bedrock, probably for Khafre. It is about 73 meters long and 20 meters high. Its nose was broken off centuries ago, and sand buried it up to its shoulders many times until it was dug out."]],
k:[["Location","Giza plateau, Greater Cairo, Egypt"],["Built","c. 2600–2500 BCE (4th dynasty)"],["Great Pyramid height","About 146 m originally, about 139 m today"],["Stone blocks","About 2.3 million in the Great Pyramid"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1979 (Memphis and its Necropolis)"]],
d:[["c. 2560 BCE","Great Pyramid of Khufu completed, according to most estimates"],["c. 2530 BCE","Pyramid of Khafre built"],["c. 2500 BCE","Pyramid of Menkaure built"],["c. 1400 BCE","Pharaoh Thutmose IV clears sand from the Sphinx"],["1979","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2013","Workers’ papyrus logs found at Wadi al-Jarf"]],
s2:[["Who built them?","Greek writers imagined armies of slaves, but archaeologists have found the villages of the builders near the pyramids, with bakeries, breweries and cemeteries. The workers were paid laborers and skilled craftsmen, many of them farmers who worked during the Nile flood, when fields were underwater. Their tombs show they were honored for their work."],["How were they built?","No one knows exactly. Workers cut limestone from quarries on the plateau and moved granite by boat from Aswan, hundreds of kilometers up the Nile. They probably dragged blocks on sledges over wet sand and raised them using ramps of mud brick and rubble. A papyrus diary found in 2013 records a team carrying limestone to the Great Pyramid."],["Inside the Great Pyramid","Narrow passages lead to the King’s Chamber, lined with red granite and holding an empty stone coffin, and the so-called Queen’s Chamber. The Grand Gallery, a tall sloping corridor, is one of the most impressive spaces. In 2017 scientists using particles from space called muons detected a large hidden void above it."],["Robbers and explorers","Despite heavy stones and hidden passages, robbers emptied the pyramids in ancient times. Much later, the casing stones were taken to build medieval Cairo. Scholars on Napoleon’s expedition in 1798 measured the pyramids, and Egyptologists such as Flinders Petrie surveyed them carefully in the 1880s."],["Boats for the afterlife","In 1954 archaeologists found a sealed pit beside the Great Pyramid holding a cedarwood ship in more than 1,200 pieces. Rebuilt, Khufu’s ship is about 43 meters long and may have carried the king’s body or been meant for his journey in the afterlife. It is now in the Grand Egyptian Museum nearby."],["Visiting Giza","The pyramids are among the world’s most visited sites, and visitors can enter some of them, ride camels on the plateau or watch a sound-and-light show. The Grand Egyptian Museum, near the plateau, houses the treasures of Tutankhamun and many other finds."]],
sp:["The Seven Wonders","Ancient Greek travelers made lists of the most amazing sights of their world, which became the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World: the Great Pyramid, the Hanging Gardens of Babylon, the Statue of Zeus at Olympia, the Temple of Artemis at Ephesus, the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, the Colossus of Rhodes and the Lighthouse of Alexandria. Only the Great Pyramid, the oldest of them all, still stands."],
g:[["Pharaoh","A king of ancient Egypt"],["Mummy","A body preserved after death"],["Old Kingdom","The period of Egyptian history when the great pyramids were built"],["Causeway","A raised road or path"]],
f:["The Great Pyramid has about 2.3 million stone blocks.","It was the tallest human-made structure for about 3,800 years.","The pyramid builders were paid workers, not slaves."],
fun:"The Great Pyramid was the tallest human-made structure on Earth for about 3,800 years.",
tl:"Ang mga Piramide ng Giza sa Ehipto ay itinayo mga 4,500 taon na ang nakalipas bilang libingan ng mga paraon. Ang Great Pyramid ang tanging natitira sa Pitong Kahanga-hangang Bagay ng Sinaunang Mundo."});

